window.ARG_SEARCH_INDEX = {"v":1,"entries":[{"f":"01-introduction.html","a":"","n":1,"c":"Introduction","h":"Introduction","t":"Don't fight the framework. This chapter is about why that sentence is the whole guide in miniature, and what it costs us when we do."},{"f":"01-introduction.html","a":"dont-fight","n":1,"c":"Introduction","h":"Don't fight the framework","t":"Next.js is not a rendering engine you bolt a React app onto. It is an opinionated architecture with a specific model of how pages should be composed, where data should be fetched, and where interactivity should live. When you work with that model, an enormous amount of complexity simply disappears: loading states, request timing, cache invalidation, bundle management, and layout stability are all problems the framework has first-class answers for. When you work against it, you don't just lose those answers; you actively re-create the problems they were designed to solve, and then you hand-roll worse solutions to them. The reason to work with the model is not deference; it is mechanics. React 19 and the App Router are deeply opinionated, and the App Router more so. Not opinionated for their own sake; opinionated because these are complex systems, and complex systems only deliver their guarantees when they can make assumptions about how you use them . The compiler can only optimize components that follow the rules of React. The router can only cache and stream what the server rendered. The boundary between server and client only pays off if it sits where the platform expects it. Every deviation from how the platform wants something done makes the machinery less effective, and past a point, actively destructive : the framework starts working against you, re-fetching what it would have cached, blocking what it would have streamed, shipping what it would have kept on the server. The opinions are the price of the guarantees. That is the trap this guide exists to keep us out of. The patterns we've all internalized from years of traditional React (fetch in an effect, hold it in state, show a spinner, lift state up, pass callbacks down) are not neutral habits that happen to be suboptimal here. In the App Router they are load-bearing mistakes : each one forecloses a framework capability, and the foreclosures compound. What it looks like when it compounds Pages that take visibly long to navigate to, in production, on real networks. Spinners nested inside spinners. Content that jumps as each request lands. And the insidious part: you progressively lose the ability to adopt the tools designed to fix these problems . Streaming can't help a page whose data loads after hydration. Caching can't help a request the server never sees. Partial prerendering can't help a tree that"},{"f":"01-introduction.html","a":"what-this-is","n":1,"c":"Introduction","h":"What this is, and why I wrote it","t":"A confession about scope: this document is maybe ninety percent stitched together from the official Next.js and React documentation, reorganized and elaborated, and about ten percent things I have learned the hard way about what works and what doesn't. The Next.js docs in particular have a habit of dropping a genuinely load-bearing idea in a single sentence, somewhere near the end of a paragraph, and then moving on without ever explaining the why, the how, or what it looks like in real code. This guide is my attempt to slow down on exactly those sentences: to take the ones that matter most and expand them into concrete examples and design patterns you can actually reach for. The ten percent is the part I care about most. I started wrestling with the App Router back in 2021, and it was a long, unglamorous slog of trial and error before I understood how it actually wants to be used. Most of the patterns in this guide are the residue of that process: the things I wish someone had handed me up front, instead of making me find them one painful refactor at a time. I can't give you the intuition (that part you still have to earn), but I can shorten the road, and that is the whole ambition here. A useful way to hold it Here is the mindset that makes all of this click. If you treat every type coercion as the worst thing in the world, an error to be hunted down and never tolerated, you will become very good at TypeScript. In exactly the same way: if you treat every \"use client\" and every useEffect as something that should make you wince a little every time you write it, a cost to be justified rather than a tool to reach for, you will become very good at the App Router, if only through the trial and error of avoiding them. Treat them like a smell you actively steer away from, and the design patterns in this guide start appearing on their own. I am mostly just trying to save you the months it took me to notice them. One honest caveat before we go further. No single document, and no one-hour tech collab, can cover everything about server-first React, the App Router, and React 19; the surface is too large and it moves too fast. This guide is a map and a set of patterns, not a replacement for the docs or for your own experimentation. You will still have to put in the reps. The goal is only to make those reps land faster and sting less."},{"f":"01-introduction.html","a":"read-the-docs","n":1,"c":"Introduction","h":"Read the docs","t":"Seriously: read them . Not as API reference when something breaks, but as a description of a model. The App Router docs are unusually good at explaining why each convention exists: why layouts don't re-render, why loading.tsx is a file and not a prop, why caching is opt-in, why the client boundary is a directive. Most of the patterns in this guide are not clever inventions; they are the documented, intended usage. The single biggest upgrade available to us as a team is to stop treating this stack like the React we already know and to step outside the traditional model we've all become accustomed to. This guide is the map, not the territory. Every chapter links to the primary sources, official Next.js and React documentation, collected in §20 References . And read both of them, not just Next.js. The React documentation (react.dev) is actually quite thorough, and a surprising amount of what feels like Next.js magic is really just React 19 doing its job; you will understand the framework far better once you understand the library underneath it. The division of labor between the two is worth internalizing: React explains the primitives in depth, and Next.js explains how they compose into an application, often tersely. When the two seem to disagree, they usually don't; you are just missing the React-level reason the Next.js-level rule exists. There is a specific reward for pushing through the terse parts. If you commit to server-first thinking and force your brain down those paths through trial and error until you start getting it right, the design patterns begin to emerge on their own. And then something useful happens: the next time you hit one of those single-sentence pronouncements in the Next.js docs, it finally lands, because you have already felt the problem it was quietly solving. Much of this guide is an attempt to front-load that click, to explain the why and the how behind the terse lines so you spend less time confused. It will not remove the process; nothing does. It just makes it less painful."},{"f":"01-introduction.html","a":"direction","n":1,"c":"Introduction","h":"The direction of travel","t":"\"Don't fight the framework\" carries more weight once you see that the framework is a moving object, and that it has been moving in one direction for over a decade. For roughly ten years (call it 2013 to 2020), \"a React app\" meant one thing: the server sends an empty HTML shell and a JavaScript bundle; the bundle boots; components mount; effects fire; data is fetched; the page assembles itself in the browser. Everything we internalized as \"how React works\" (fetch-in-effect, loading flags in state, lifting state up, client routers, spinners) is machinery for surviving inside that model. But that model was a stop on the road, not the destination, and the history makes the trajectory hard to argue with. Trace the actual events: Thirteen years, one direction. The client era built machinery for surviving in the browser; everything since December 2020 has been about no longer needing to. Note what never shipped: a better fetch-in-effect, a nicer loading flag, a faster global store. The problems of the client era weren't refined; they were obsoleted. Dates are the official announcements (react.dev, nextjs.org, vercel.com). Read the second half of that timeline the way the React and Next.js teams read it: every major primitive shipped since December 2020 assumes a server-first architecture. React Server Components moved rendering (and with it, data access) to the server, making \"component that runs in the browser\" the explicitly-marked exception rather than the assumption. Actions moved mutations to the server, making \"POST to an API route, then juggle client state\" unnecessary for the common case. Suspense and streaming moved loading states out of components and into the tree structure, making \"this component shows its own spinner\" an anti-pattern rather than a convention. Next.js 16's caching model (opt-in caching, partial prerendering by default when enabled) is built on the assumption that most of a page is server-rendered and cacheable, with dynamic regions marked by Suspense, an assumption an SPA-style app violates on every page. The React Compiler removes the memoization busywork that made \"just put everything in one big client component\" feel pragmatic. None of them help (several literally cannot function) in a tree that is client-rendered from the root. The feature-level details live in §19 ; what matters here is the shape of the history. The client era's ch"},{"f":"01-introduction.html","a":"stakes","n":1,"c":"Introduction","h":"The stakes","t":"The architecture and approach you use now sets the stage for the rest of the product's life. That's not a platitude; it's the compounding math of a codebase. Every new page copies the patterns of the page before it. Every hook written around a client-side fetch becomes a dependency of the next feature. Conventions harden in months; unwinding them takes years. I say this as someone who has tried to refactor ojl-tracker out of exactly these problems, repeatedly. It is a never-ending battle, and the debt was cheapest on the day it was created. We do not want a re-Tracker: imagine how much more confusing the Slack channels, the product names, and the team names would become. Server-first is not optional Let's be unambiguous about this, because everything else in the guide depends on it. A server-first mentality is not a preference, not a style, and not one architecture among several we could reasonably choose . It is the operating assumption of React 19 and the App Router: the contract every primitive in this stack is built against. Every one of them assumes you are already thinking this way, so when you aren't, nothing throws an error; it just quietly works worse. The framework doesn't offer \"SPA mode\" as a supported alternative; it offers silence. Build against the grain and nothing stops you: the page renders, the demo works, the sprint closes. What you've actually done is taken out a loan no one issues a statement for. So the question is never whether building the old way will cause problems, only when they surface, and how much product is sitting on top of them by then . And when they surface, the menu is short and every item on it is expensive: hand-roll custom machinery that dampens the symptoms without ever eliminating them; accept the degradation as a permanent casualty and ship around it; retroactively refactor large portions of the UI, which is an order of magnitude harder than building it correctly was; or rewrite. There is no fifth option where it quietly works out. The mental-model shift this guide describes is the requirement; the only choice we get is whether we make it now, deliberately and cheaply, or later, under duress, at whatever the price has grown to. The good news You're smart, and you'll figure this out. None of this is beyond anyone on this team: it's a mental-model swap, not a skill gap. But it has to be deliberate: if we keep buildin"},{"f":"01-introduction.html","a":"how-organized","n":1,"c":"Introduction","h":"How this guide is organized","t":"§2 defines what we're optimizing: the goals behind every pattern, each tagged as universal React or Next-specific. §3 is the pivot of the document: what actually happens when an App Router page renders, and why the traditional mental model fails quietly now and loudly later. Parts A–C then elaborate each goal family with its patterns, Part D applies all of it: a worked example, the \"Signs You're Doing It Wrong\" checklist, and before/after case studies from our own codebase. Two chip systems annotate every practice throughout: React / Next.js : whether the practice applies to any React app or is specific to (or dramatically amplified by) the App Router. Critical / Very important / Important : how much it matters that we follow the practice. Critical items are foundational: getting them wrong undermines the architecture itself. Very important items are our default on every page, with occasional exceptions. Important items are strong habits with the lowest ceremony to deviate from. Wherever a practice has legitimate exceptions, they're named, so you know what a valid exception looks like, and the rule never feels arbitrary."},{"f":"02-goals.html","a":"","n":2,"c":"The Goals","h":"The Goals","t":"What you are optimizing, on every page, every time. Patterns come later; this chapter is only about ends, so that when techniques conflict you know which one wins."},{"f":"02-goals.html","a":"thesis","n":2,"c":"The Goals","h":"The thesis","t":"Every page you build divides into two kinds of content: what can be known the moment the first render happens, and what cannot. Your job (the actual job, underneath every framework feature and pattern in this guide) is to: Separate the known from the unknown, deliberately, on every page. Maximize the known : make the first render carry as much finished content as possible. Contain the unknown : the parts you must wait for may not disturb anything around them. Decouple the parts , so each region's requirements can change without rewiring its neighbors. Decoupling buys more than safe change: a component that asks for its own data instead of receiving it through props is portable , so it can be dropped onto any page without a chain of wiring following it ( §13 ), and it lets the interactive parts that need hydration be pushed down into small leaves instead of dragging whole regions across the client boundary ( §5 ). Server-side rendering is what makes this framing powerful: in a browser-only app, almost nothing is knowable at first render : the client starts blank and earns every pixel through requests. On the server, the page's structure, its static content, and any data the server can fetch are all knowable before the first byte reaches the browser . The App Router exists to make that surface as large as possible . Treat it like an SPA and you shrink the known back down to a spinner. The anatomy of a page under this model: the structure and containers are known and render immediately; data-dependent regions are unknown and stream into space reserved for them; interactivity is confined to small leaves. The three meta-goals below organize the rest of this document. Each sub-goal is an end , tagged by scope; the chapters of Parts A–C cover the techniques (the means) for achieving them. A"},{"f":"02-goals.html","a":"goal-a","n":2,"c":"The Goals","h":"Maximize the Known","t":"Make the first render carry as much finished content as possible. Elaborated in §4 – §8 . Maximize above-the-fold content delivered as real HTML on the first response, not shells that earn their content later. Next.js §4 Push interactivity down as far as humanly possible: client boundaries at the leaves (a button, a filter, an input), never at pages or layouts. Next.js §5 Minimize the JavaScript required for first render: anything not needed at first paint loads behind a dynamic boundary. React §6 Prefer state the server can resolve (the URL, route params, search params, cookies) over client state the server can't see. Next.js §8 B"},{"f":"02-goals.html","a":"goal-b","n":2,"c":"The Goals","h":"Contain the Unknown","t":"The parts you must wait for may not disturb anything around them. Elaborated in §9 – §12 . Minimize content shifting. Containers are known: they render immediately and reserve accurate space for both the fallback and the real content. React §9 Loading and error states belong to boundaries, not components: a component never shows its own spinner. React §10 Minimize what sits behind any single blocking request, on the server and the client alike. React §12 Isolate data dependencies, so a change in what a region needs affects only the region it determines, never the surrounding content. React §10 – §12 C"},{"f":"02-goals.html","a":"goal-c","n":2,"c":"The Goals","h":"Decouple the Parts","t":"Minimize the wires that connect parts of the UI together. Elaborated in §13 – §15 . Minimize data exchange between components and modules: never pass a child anything the parent doesn't itself need. React §13 Prefer repetition backed by dedupe over shared plumbing: it is better for two components to ask the same question than to be wired together; deduped fetches and cached functions make the repetition free. Next.js §14 Communicate through the URL and the cache, not through callbacks up to parents. React §13 , §8 Break components down by UI and responsibility - small, portable, one component per file. React §15"},{"f":"02-goals.html","a":"tension","n":2,"c":"The Goals","h":"When goals collide","t":"These goals occasionally pull against each other, and it's worth knowing the tiebreakers in advance. Containing the unknown (B) can tempt you to over-fragment a page into a dozen suspense boundaries, but decoupling (C) is about dependencies , not confetti; a boundary should trace a real data dependency, not a visual whim. Maximizing the known (A) can tempt you to block the whole page on a fetch so everything arrives \"known\", but that violates B: knowledge that arrives late and all-at-once is worse than structure that arrives instantly with the slow parts contained. When in doubt: structure first, then contain, then optimize what's inside . Why no importance chips in this chapter Goals are ends, not rules: there's nothing here to have an exception to. The importance chips ( Critical Very important Important ) appear from §5 onward, where concrete practices start."},{"f":"03-mental-model.html","a":"","n":3,"c":"The Mental Model","h":"The Mental Model","t":"This is the pivot chapter. If you take one thing from this guide, take this: the model you learned from years of traditional React is not the model this stack is built on, and the ecosystem is moving away from it, not toward it. Chapter 1 traced the direction of travel : thirteen years of platform history converging on server-first. This chapter is the practical consequence: the model itself. What actually happens when an App Router page renders, and exactly where the old instincts betray you. And carry in the warning from Chapter 1 : these are deeply opinionated systems, and the opinions are the price of the guarantees . What follows is not a style this guide happens to prefer. It is the model the platform's machinery assumes, and every deviation from it costs you some of the machinery. The shift, concretely In an SPA, the browser receives a nearly empty shell and a JavaScript bundle; after the bundle mounts, each component pulls its own data into place, and the page assembles itself in front of the user. The question that model trains you to ask is \"how do I get data into my components?\" In the App Router, the page arrives already assembled: the server had the data and rendered with it before the browser saw a single byte. The only question left is \"which parts of my UI can't be finished on the server, and why?\" , and every such part is a cost to be justified."},{"f":"03-mental-model.html","a":"rendering-model","n":3,"c":"The Mental Model","h":"3.1 What actually happens when a page renders","t":"In the App Router, every component is a server component by default . When a request comes in, the server executes your component tree (running your async components, awaiting your data) and streams the result to the browser as HTML, immediately, including real content for everything that was knowable and reserved fallbacks for everything that wasn't. Client components (only the subtrees you explicitly mark with \"use client\" ) are the only parts that ship JavaScript, hydrate, and become interactive. One request. The server resolves everything knowable, streams structure immediately, and the unknown fills in without moving anything. No client-side fetch chain, no spinner-then-layout-shift choreography. Three consequences of this model are worth internalizing, because they invert traditional-React instincts: Data access is a render-time concern, not a lifecycle concern. A server component doesn't \"mount and then fetch\"; it simply awaits what it needs while rendering. There is no timing to coordinate, no \"is the data here yet?\" state to manage. An entire category of client-side complexity (effects, request state machines, race conditions, stale closures) exists only on the other side of the client boundary. This is the deeper reason backend code has always felt simpler than frontend code: the server deals in one request → one response, while the client juggles user interactions, network variability, browsers, extensions, ad blockers, and time itself. SSR moves work from the hard side to the easy side. The bundle is the exception, not the app. Server components ship zero JavaScript. Your app's JS payload is exactly the sum of its \"use client\" subtrees, which is why boundary placement ( §5 ) is an architectural decision, not a style preference. Navigation re-renders on the server too. Client-side navigations fetch the new route's server output (the RSC payload), not JSON-for-you-to-assemble. Layouts persist without re-rendering; only the changing segments re-render. The router, not your state management, owns the page's identity."},{"f":"03-mental-model.html","a":"app-directory","n":3,"c":"The Mental Model","h":"The app directory is your app, not \"routes\"","t":"It's tempting to see app/ as the router's configuration area: thin page.tsx files that immediately hand off to the \"real\" components living in components/ . That inversion is exactly backwards. The app directory is the application : its structure, its layouts, its containers, its core content. Everything related to the core parts of a page belongs there; the pieces that slot into that scaffold are what live outside it. This is also the answer to a question worth asking yourself: why does Next.js use file-based routing at all? Because the filesystem makes the page's composition statically knowable . Layouts nest by directory. Loading and error boundaries are files, so the framework knows where they are before any code runs. Code splitting falls out per-segment for free. Prefetching a link means the router already knows exactly what the destination needs. Every one of those capabilities depends on the tree being declared in the filesystem rather than assembled at runtime in the browser. A five-line page.tsx that delegates everything to a giant client component doesn't just \"move code around\"; it hides the entire page from the framework."},{"f":"03-mental-model.html","a":"old-vs-new","n":3,"c":"The Mental Model","h":"3.2 Why the old mental model bites you later","t":"The dangerous thing about SPA-style code in the App Router is that it works at first . The page renders. The data arrives. Nothing warns you. The costs arrive later, on real networks, at real scale, and by then they're structural: The same page, same data, two architectures. The SPA chain serializes five round-trips before anything meaningful paints, and pays them again on every hard navigation. The server-first version parallelizes on the server and paints real content in one. And the failure modes compound, because each one blocks the escape route from the others: Habit you keep Tool you lose Fetch after hydration (effects, client query hooks) Streaming and loading.tsx : there's nothing to stream ; the server finishes instantly with an empty shell. \"use client\" at the page/layout level Server components below it: the entire subtree is client forever ; every child you add ships to the bundle. Requests the server never sees The whole caching model: \"use cache\" , revalidation, request dedupe. You rebuild caching by hand , per hook. Mutations via hand-rolled API routes Actions, revalidatePath / revalidateTag : read-your-writes consistency becomes your problem , solved with things like consistency tokens. Client state for tabs, filters, visibility Shareable URLs, server-resolvable first render, and the router's back/forward behavior: all gone at once . Read the left column against our own codebase and the pattern is uncomfortable; §18 does exactly that, file by file. The point of this chapter isn't blame; every one of those habits was the correct professional instinct in 2019. The point is that they are instincts , and instincts don't update from warnings; they update from understanding the machine underneath. That's what Parts A through C are for. Where to go deeper Primary sources for this chapter: the Next.js docs on Server and Client Components , the React docs on Server Components , and the Next.js 16 release notes for the caching model. Full list in §20 ."},{"f":"04-ssr.html","a":"","n":4,"c":"Server-Side Rendering","h":"Server-Side Rendering","t":"Part A is about maximizing the known, and server-side rendering is the machine that makes the known possible at all. This chapter is the why behind every technique that follows: what SSR is, what it buys us, how it changes what \"fast\" means, and how routing actually moves content around once the server is doing the rendering."},{"f":"04-ssr.html","a":"what","n":4,"c":"Server-Side Rendering","h":"4.1 What server-side rendering is","t":"Server-side rendering means exactly what it says: your React components execute on the server , per request, and what travels to the browser is their output , real HTML, rather than the JavaScript machinery needed to produce that output. The browser can paint that HTML immediately, before any bundle downloads, parses, or runs. Compare the browser-only model, where the server sends an empty shell and the page earns every pixel through a boot sequence: download JS → execute → mount → fetch → render. In the App Router this is not a feature you enable; it is the default posture of the entire framework. Next.js Critical Every component is a server component unless you mark it otherwise, so every route starts life fully server-rendered. Even client components participate: Next.js renders their initial HTML on the server too, then hydrates them (attaches the event handlers and state) once their JavaScript arrives. The practical consequence, and the reason this chapter leads Part A: everything the server can resolve arrives finished , and only the parts you explicitly carve out ever depend on the client. The rest of Part A is about keeping that carved-out surface small ( §5 – §8 ); Part B is about handling what remains ( §9 – §12 )."},{"f":"04-ssr.html","a":"benefits","n":4,"c":"Server-Side Rendering","h":"4.2 What you get for it","t":"The Next.js docs enumerate the benefits of server rendering at length; collapsed, they come down to these: Benefit Why it matters Data fetching at the source Fetches run on the server, close to the database and internal services: fewer, faster round trips, instead of every widget dialing home from the browser across the public internet. Security by locality Tokens, API keys, and privileged queries never leave the server . The browser receives rendered results, not the credentials and endpoints that produced them. Less JavaScript shipped Server components add zero bytes to the bundle : their code, their dependencies, their markdown parser, all of it stays behind. The client downloads markup, not the machinery ( §6 ). Rendered once, served many Rendered output is an artifact the framework can cache and reuse across users ; see §4.4 and §14 . First paint and SEO Real HTML on the first response means content is visible before JavaScript runs : for users, for crawlers, and for link unfurlers alike. Those are real, and they're the ones you'll see cited everywhere. But two benefits matter more to this guide than all of the above, because they are the ones our traditional habits give away first: stability and simplicity ."},{"f":"04-ssr.html","a":"stability","n":4,"c":"Server-Side Rendering","h":"Stability: the known arrives first, already placed","t":"Content shifting happens when content arrives late and pushes its neighbors around ( §9 is devoted to it). SSR attacks the problem at its root: whatever the server resolves is part of the first paint , already in its final position, at its final size. It cannot shift anything, because it never \"arrives\"; it was simply there. React Critical This is the direct, physical link between SSR and maximizing the known: every piece of content you move from client fetching to server rendering is a piece of content that can no longer displace the page . The layout-shift surface of a page is, almost by definition, its unknown surface. Shrink the unknown and the shifting shrinks with it; what little remains gets contained inside reserved containers ( §9.3 ) instead of being fixed after the fact."},{"f":"04-ssr.html","a":"simplicity","n":4,"c":"Server-Side Rendering","h":"Simplicity: content that doesn't depend on timing","t":"Here is the elaboration this deserves, because \"SSR is simpler\" sounds backwards to anyone who learned React in the browser, and it is the single biggest quality-of-life difference in practice. On the server, an async component await s its data and renders it. That's the whole story. Between the await and the render there is no moment where the component exists but its data doesn't , so an entire category of questions evaporates: What do I render while it's loading? Where does the loading flag live? What if the response lands after the user changed filters? What's the fallback if the data isn't there yet , as opposed to not there at all ? None of these are answerable \"wrong\" on the server. They are unaskable . Content stops depending on the timing of requests and responses, so the code stops modeling time. Client-side, every one of those questions must be answered at every layer that fetches , and the answers stack. A component can't fetch until it mounts, and it can't mount until its parent's fetch resolves and renders it. So the requests serialize down the tree (render → mount → effect → fetch → children mount → their fetch…), and each layer of the DOM grows its own loading state while it waits on the layer above. The visible result is the most recognizable symptom in our own codebase: spinners rendered inside spinners , loading UI at three depths of the same page, each replaced at a different moment as its request lands. The spinner tower. Client-side, each DOM layer is blocked by the client-side request above it (the page can't fetch until it hydrates, the section can't fetch until the page renders it, the widget can't fetch until the section does), so loading states appear at every depth and resolve at different moments. Server-side, the same tree renders in one pass: each await is just a value, and the browser receives the finished result. Notice what the right side doesn't have : loading flags, fallback branches, effect dependencies, race-condition guards, \"is it undefined because it's loading or because it's empty\" ambiguity ( §10.6 ). That code was never simplified; it was never written. Multiply that by every data-bearing component on every page, and \"simplicity\" stops being an abstract virtue and becomes the difference between a codebase you can reason about and one you can't. React Very important"},{"f":"04-ssr.html","a":"output-not-machinery","n":4,"c":"Server-Side Rendering","h":"The output crosses the wire, not the machinery","t":"Two rows of the benefits table deserve to be read together, because they are one fact seen from two sides: the only thing that ever crosses the network boundary is the rendered output. Think about what it takes to produce a reports table: the component code that lays it out, the data-layer function that queries for rows, the connection string and API tokens that query needs, the formatting and charting libraries that turn numbers into markup. In a client-rendered architecture, most of that ships to the browser, because the browser is where the rendering happens. In a server-rendered one, none of it does. The client receives what those things produced : finished markup. Everything needed to produce the page stays behind the boundary; only what it produced crosses. Exactly like the diagrams in this guide: the browser receives the SVG, not the code that generated it. This is why \"security by locality\" and \"less JavaScript shipped\" are the same row twice. Security: the credentials, the privileged queries, and the internal API shapes cannot leak to the browser because they were never sent, and server-only ( §5.5 ) turns any accidental attempt into a build error. Performance: those rendering libraries are precisely the heavyweight imports of §7 , and keeping them on the server is the single cheapest bundle-size win available, because the client downloads a few kilobytes of markup instead of megabytes of machinery ( §6 ). One architectural fact, two benefits: expose the output, never the means of producing it."},{"f":"04-ssr.html","a":"perceived","n":4,"c":"Server-Side Rendering","h":"4.3 Perceived performance, and where streaming fits","t":"Perceived performance is how fast a page feels , which is not the same thing as how long it takes to finish. Users don't experience totals; they experience milestones: when something first appears, when the content they came for is readable, whether things move around while they wait, and when the page responds to input. A page that paints meaningful content in 300ms and quietly finishes a slow widget at 3s feels fast . A page that shows a spinner until everything is ready at 1.5s feels slower, despite technically \"finishing\" sooner. The Next.js docs, on why this matters \"SSR with React and Next.js helps improve the perceived loading performance by showing a non-interactive page to the user as soon as possible.\" ( Server Components, Next.js docs ) Read that carefully: non-interactive , as soon as possible . The docs are conceding something and claiming something. The concession: the first paint isn't interactive yet: hydration hasn't happened. The claim: that trade is worth it, because seeing real content immediately dominates the user's perception of speed. People spend the first moments on a page reading , not clicking; SSR uses that window to be useful while the JavaScript catches up."},{"f":"04-ssr.html","a":"streaming-principle","n":4,"c":"Server-Side Rendering","h":"Streaming: the same principle, inside one response","t":"Streaming is the same principle applied within a single response. Plain SSR has a weakness: if the server waits for all data before sending anything, the slowest query gates the entire page. Streaming removes that gate: the server sends the known immediately (structure, containers, everything already resolved) and flushes the slow parts into their reserved slots as they finish, over the same response ( §10.2 ). Perceived performance is governed by when the first meaningful content lands and whether the page holds still afterward; streaming makes the first independent of your slowest query, and containers ( §9 ) guarantee the second. That is the whole Part B playbook in one sentence, powered by the fact that rendering happens on a server that can flush incrementally."},{"f":"04-ssr.html","a":"tti","n":4,"c":"Server-Side Rendering","h":"Time-to-interactive is a dead metric","t":"A question worth asking a room of engineers: how many of you actually interact with a page within 500ms of it loading? Nobody does. You land, you orient, you read; the first click comes seconds later, after the content has told you what is worth clicking on. Optimizing for the earliest possible moment of interactivity is optimizing a moment that nobody uses. That is why TTI is, for practical purposes, a dead metric. Everything in this architecture is about optimizing what the user can see and read : getting real content onto the page as fast as humanly possible. The interactive leaves hydrate some time later, and that is fine, because interactivity is not needed immediately and hydration is invisible. A button whose click handler attaches 800ms after first paint looks identical to one that was interactive from the first frame; the user cannot tell the difference, because they were reading. What they absolutely can tell is whether the content was there. Pages built this way feel faster for a simple reason: the part the user knows about (the content) arrives first, and the parts they never needed to know about (handlers attaching, client state hydrating) become hidden work that finishes quietly in the background."},{"f":"04-ssr.html","a":"cls-feel","n":4,"c":"Server-Side Rendering","h":"Layout shift is perceived slowness","t":"The third leg of perceived performance is stillness. A page where loading indicators flash in and out while regions shift around as each response lands just looks terrible: busy, unprofessional, and, crucially, slower , even when the wall-clock time is identical. Every spinner and every reflow gives the user a blatant look into the intermediate machinery: here is what we have not fetched yet, here is the empty space we are holding with fallback content, here is everything arriving one request at a time, in whatever order the network chose. The user is made to watch the kitchen. All of that can simply be swooped up onto the server. Resolve the data there and the intermediate steps stop existing on the client: the user sees the full page, ready to go, just not yet hydrated, and none of the scaffolding that produced it. Fewer visible steps always reads as faster. The mechanics of holding still (containers, honest space reservation, CLS as a field metric) get a full chapter in §9 ; what belongs here is the perceptual claim: a page that appears at once and never moves feels faster than one that assembles itself in front of the user, regardless of what the network tab says."},{"f":"04-ssr.html","a":"page-cache","n":4,"c":"Server-Side Rendering","h":"4.4 The cached page: maximized server content becomes a cacheable artifact","t":"There's a compounding payoff hiding in all of this. Once a page is rendered on the server, its rendered output, the HTML plus the RSC payload that describes it ( §4.5 ), is a concrete artifact . And artifacts can be cached. Next.js does exactly that. Routes that don't depend on request-time information are rendered once (at build time, or on first request, or on a revalidation schedule), and the cached output is served to every subsequent visitor without re-rendering: effectively a static site, per route. When part of a page does need request-time data, the modern model (Partial Prerendering / cacheComponents ) caches the static shell and streams only the dynamic holes into it. The mechanics ( \"use cache\" , revalidation, cache tags) live in §14 ; what belongs here is the architectural point: Only server-rendered content can participate in this cache. Next.js Critical The framework can only cache output it produces; content fetched from the browser after hydration is invisible to it: every user re-pays the full cost of every client-side request, forever. So each piece of content you move to the server is upgraded twice: it arrives in the first paint and it becomes eligible to be rendered once and served to everyone. Maximizing the known isn't just making one request faster; it's making the page progressively cheaper to serve at all. This is also the trap the audit found us in: a client-rendered tree opts out of the entire caching layer silently, page by page, with no error to tell you what you gave up."},{"f":"04-ssr.html","a":"routing","n":4,"c":"Server-Side Rendering","h":"4.5 Routing: server routing, client routing, and the RSC payload","t":"One reasonable worry remains: if the server renders everything, doesn't every click become a full page reload: white flash, re-downloaded assets, lost state? This is where the App Router is cleverer than classic server rendering, and understanding it requires one piece of vocabulary: the RSC payload . The RSC payload is a compact, streamable serialization of a rendered server component tree. It contains the rendered output of your server components, placeholders marking where client components go (plus references to their JS chunks), and any props passed from server to client. It is what the server actually produces when it renders; HTML is generated from it for first visits. Think of it as \"the rendered page, described as data\", which is precisely what makes client-side merging possible. Routing then works in two modes: First visit: server routing. The browser requests a URL; the server renders the full tree and responds with a complete HTML document (with the RSC payload embedded for hydration). Real content paints before JavaScript loads: everything §4.1–§4.3 promised. Every navigation after: client routing. Clicking a <Link> does not reload the document. The router requests the RSC payload for just the route segments that change, and React merges it into the page in place. Shared layouts don't re-render and their state survives ( §3.1 ); only the content that differs is replaced. And because <Link> prefetches payloads for links in the viewport, the merge often happens from cache - navigation lands near-instantly. Two routing modes, one rendering model. The first visit is server routing: a complete HTML document, content visible before JavaScript. Every navigation after is client routing: the router fetches the RSC payload for the changing segments (often already prefetched) and merges it in place; layouts, their state, and their containers persist. This is the answer to the worry, and the reason SSR here doesn't mean \"a website from 2005.\" You get server rendering's first paint and an SPA's navigation feel, from the same architecture, without building either by hand. But notice it's conditional: the seamless merge works because the server renders segments the router can splice. A page that client-fetches everything after mount gets neither benefit: its first visit is a shell, and its navigations re-run the whole fetch choreography ( §12.4 )."},{"f":"04-ssr.html","a":"prefetching","n":4,"c":"Server-Side Rendering","h":"Links and prefetching","t":"The instant-navigation story above hangs on one component: <Link> . Internal navigation always goes through <Link> , never a plain <a> : an anchor tag opts out of the entire model with a full document load: layouts torn down, state cleared, scroll reset. Next.js Critical But <Link> does more than route on the client: it prefetches . When a link enters the viewport (or is hovered), Next.js quietly loads the target route in the background, so by the time the user actually clicks, the payload the router needs is usually already sitting on the client, and the navigation resolves without waiting on the network at all. app/layout.tsx {/* Prefetched automatically when the link enters the viewport or is hovered. */} < Link href = \"/programs\" >Programs<\/ Link > {/* A plain anchor opts out of everything: full document load, no prefetch. */} <a href = \"/contact\" >Contact<\/a> {/* Prefetching can be disabled per link: the right call for large lists of links (an infinite-scroll table of rows), where eagerly prefetching every visible row is wasted work. Prefetch-on-hover is the middle ground. */} < Link prefetch ={ false } href = \"/programs/42\" >Program 42<\/ Link > How much can be prefetched is, once again, a known/unknown question, this time asked about a route the user hasn't visited yet: Static routes are prefetched in full. The route was prerendered ( §4.4 ), so its complete payload already exists as a cached artifact; the client can fetch all of it ahead of the click and navigate entirely from cache. Dynamic routes are prefetched partially, or not at all. The server can't render request-time content before the request exists. But if the route has a loading.tsx , Next.js prefetches everything that is knowable ahead of time: the shared layouts and the loading skeleton. The known travels early; only the unknown waits for the click."},{"f":"04-ssr.html","a":"dynamic-loading","n":4,"c":"Server-Side Rendering","h":"The slow-transition trap: dynamic routes without loading.tsx","t":"Follow that second bullet to its failure mode, because the docs call it out as the number-one thing that makes transitions feel slow. A dynamic route with no loading.tsx has nothing prefetchable and nothing to show on click, so when the user navigates, nothing happens until the server finishes rendering. Click… wait… still the old page. In the docs' words, it \"can give the users the impression that the app is not responding.\" Every dynamic route should have a loading.tsx (or an equivalent Suspense boundary). Next.js Very important With the file present, the whole sequence inverts. The layouts and skeleton were partially prefetched, so the navigation commits immediately : the URL changes, the known structure paints on click, and the server's content streams into the reserved fallback when it arrives ( §10.2 ). The docs enumerate the benefits: immediate visual feedback, shared layouts that stay interactive while the route loads (the user can even interrupt the navigation and click elsewhere), better TTFB/FCP/TTI. But notice what the pattern is : contain-the-unknown, applied to navigation. The known part of the next page travels ahead of the click; the unknown part streams in behind it, into space that was reserved for it. Three edge notes close the loop. If a dynamic segment's pages are enumerable ahead of time, generateStaticParams promotes them to prerendered static routes: full prefetch, no fallback needed at all. On slow networks the prefetch may not finish before the click; useLinkStatus exposes a pending flag for an immediate, debounced hint on the link itself. And prefetching can't start until <Link> hydrates (it is a client component), which is one more reason to keep first-render JavaScript small ( §6 ). Where to go deeper React 19 docs: Server Components · Suspense (streaming SSR semantics). Next.js docs: Server and Client Components · Server Components rendering (v14 guide) (the strategies section covers static/dynamic/streaming) · Linking and Navigating (RSC payload, prefetching, client transitions, the dynamic-routes-without- loading.tsx trap) · Prefetching guide . Full list in §20 ."},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"Client/Server Boundaries","t":"The question is never \"does this component need \"use client\" ?\" It's \"how far down can I push the boundary?\" This chapter is about what the directive actually does, what genuinely requires the client, and the patterns for isolating interactivity into the smallest possible leaves."},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"what-it-means","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"5.1 What \"use client\" actually means","t":"\"use client\" is not a per-component setting, and it isn't \"this component is interactive.\" It is a module-graph marker : it declares the point where the server hands the tree to the browser. The directive goes at the top of a file, and from that file onward, every module it imports, and everything those import , becomes client code: bundled, shipped, parsed, and hydrated. React Critical That's why one careless directive is never one component's problem. A \"use client\" at the top of a page doesn't make a page interactive: it drags the page's entire import graph across the boundary. Components that render pure markup, utilities that format dates, the icon set, the table internals: all of it ships and hydrates, whether or not it ever does anything a server component couldn't have done for free. \"use client\" defines where the server hands off, not which component is interactive. The directive marks an entry point into the client module graph: everything reachable from it ships to the browser, directive or not. That downstream import graph is the cost of a boundary, which is why placement is an architectural decision. Three mechanical facts complete the picture: React Critical Client components still render on the server once. \"Client\" doesn't mean \"skips SSR\": their initial HTML is server-rendered, then hydrated in the browser. What you lose at the boundary isn't the first paint; it's async / await data access, zero-bundle rendering, and everything else in Part A. Props crossing the boundary must be serializable. The server sends them over the wire: plain objects, arrays, strings, numbers: yes; functions, class instances, DB handles: no. (Server Actions and JSX are the special, allowed exceptions.) If you're fighting serialization errors, the boundary is probably in the wrong place. The dependency arrow points one way. A client module can't import a server component; importing it would just drag it across and make it client code. Server content gets into client subtrees only by being passed through, already rendered ( §5.4 )."},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"what-requires","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"5.2 What genuinely requires the client, and what doesn't","t":"The honest list of things that cannot run on the server is short: Needs the client Does not need the client State that changes from user input: useState , useReducer Fetching and awaiting data: that's what server components are for Event handlers: onClick , onChange , onSubmit Rendering data: lists, tables, conditionals, formatting, markup of any complexity Effects and lifecycle: useEffect (and it had better not be fetching; §10 ) Reading route input: params and searchParams arrive as page props ( §8 ) Browser APIs: localStorage , observers, media queries, window Composition: laying out children, choosing variants, mapping data to components Interactivity hooks: useRouter , useOptimistic , useTransition Authorization decisions: cookie/session reads belong on the server, with redirect() Third-party components built on any of the above Anything whose output is the same HTML for the same inputs Read the right column again: it's most of every page. That's the empirical reason \"use client everywhere\" is backwards: the default assumption should be that a component is server-renderable, with the burden of proof on the exception. When a component needs one item from the left column, the answer is not to convert the component; it's to evict that one item into a leaf and keep everything else on the server."},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"push-down","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"5.3 Pushing the boundary down","t":"Next.js Critical The method, applied every time something needs interactivity: Name the interaction atoms. What does the user actually do here? Type in a search box. Click an export button. Open a row menu. Those atoms, not the sections containing them, are your client components. Extract each atom into its own leaf file. A SearchInput , an ExportButton , a RowActionsMenu : each with \"use client\" at the top, each owning exactly its interaction and nothing else. Keep the structure server-side. The section, the card, the toolbar row, the table markup: all of it stays server; it renders data, it doesn't handle input. Where interactivity must wrap content, pass the content through. A collapsible panel, a dialog, a hover card: the shell is client, but its children arrive server-rendered ( §5.4 ). Interactivity isolated into leaves. Same page, same interactions; the boundary's position decides how much of the tree pays the client tax: one version ships the whole tree, the other ships three buttons' worth of JavaScript. This is also why no \"use client\" in the app directory is our house recommendation. Next.js Very important The app directory is the scaffold: pages, layouts, structure, core content ( §3 ). If a file in app/ carries the directive, the scaffold itself has crossed the boundary, and everything slotted into it goes along. Client code lives in leaf components, imported into the scaffold. Legitimate exceptions exist ( error.tsx is required to be a client component; a page that is genuinely one interactive surface, like a canvas editor), but each one should be an explicit decision, not a reflex to make a hook error go away."},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"children-pattern","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"5.4 Getting server content inside client shells","t":"The pattern that makes leaf boundaries workable: a client component can't import server components, but it can receive them. children (and any JSX-valued prop) is rendered on the server first; the client shell receives the finished output and slots it in. The interactive wrapper ships to the browser; its contents don't. React Critical ✕ Wrapper converts its contents CollapsibleSection.tsx \"use client\" ; /* Importing it drags it across the boundary: ActivityFeed is client code now, fetch and all. */ import { ActivityFeed } from \"./ActivityFeed\" ; export function CollapsibleSection () { const [open, setOpen] = useState ( true ); return ( <section> <button onClick ={() => setOpen (!open)} /> {open && < ActivityFeed />} <\/section> ); } ✓ Wrapper receives server-rendered children CollapsibleSection.tsx: client shell, knows nothing \"use client\" ; export function CollapsibleSection ({ children }) { const [open, setOpen] = useState ( true ); return ( <section> <button onClick ={() => setOpen (!open)} /> {open && children} <\/section> ); } page.tsx: server composes through the shell < CollapsibleSection > {/* ActivityFeed stays a server component; it fetches, renders, and ships 0 KB of JS; it passes through the shell as HTML. */} < ActivityFeed programId ={id} /> <\/ CollapsibleSection > One behavior of this composition is worth being explicit about: ActivityFeed fetches its data when the page loads, whether or not the section is open. The server renders every child passed through the shell during the page's render; {open && children} only controls whether the already-rendered HTML is displayed . Collapsed by default changes nothing: the fetch already happened on the server. Usually that is exactly what you want (opening the section is instant, no spinner), and the cost is one possibly-unused query. But if the requirement is that the fetch must not run until the user opens the section , because the query is expensive or rarely wanted, then by definition the data is being requested in response to an interaction, and interaction-driven fetching is client-side fetching: a client component that queries on open (SWR, §14.6 ) behind the same shell. That is a legitimate trade, but make it deliberately: you are exchanging an instant open for a loading state the container now has to absorb. This slot pattern is the workhorse for every interactive shell: dialogs, sheets, tabs, a"},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"third-party","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"5.5 Third-party components and boundary hygiene","t":"The rest of the category, the housekeeping that keeps boundaries where you put them: Wrap third-party interactive components in your own \"use client\" file. Next.js Critical External UI libraries use state and events throughout, but many don't declare \"use client\" (it's a bundler convention, not a JS standard; older packages predate it). Importing them from a server component fails or silently misbehaves. One-line wrapper file: \"use client\"; export { Carousel } from \"acme-carousel\"; . Now it has a declared boundary, and your import graph documents exactly where the client zone starts. Bonus: the wrapper is your seam for swapping the library later ( §15 ). Worked through with examples in the subsection below . Poison-pill your server-only modules. React Very important import \"server-only\" at the top of the data layer ( §14 ) turns \"someone imported data/programs.ts from a client component\" into a build error instead of a leaked API key. client-only exists for the reverse. Mind the environment variables. Only NEXT_PUBLIC_* vars exist in client bundles, and all of them are shipped to every visitor. If a boundary move suddenly requires making a variable public, that's the boundary telling you it's in the wrong place. Next.js Critical Don't reach for client hooks to read route input. params and searchParams arrive as server page props; useParams / useSearchParams in a client page is usually the reason the page went client in the first place ( §8 ). Same for cookies and headers: next/headers , on the server. Authorization is a server decision. A guard component that fetches \"am I allowed?\" after hydration paints the page first and asks permission later. The cookie is on the server; check it in the layout or page and redirect() / notFound() before anything renders. Next.js Critical"},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"library-leaves","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"Design-system components: ship the structure and the leaves separately","t":"Everything this chapter says about applications applies with extra force to our monorepo UI library , because a library component's boundary decision is made once and inherited by every consumer. The rule: a library component with interactive parts exposes its non-interactive structure and its interactive leaves as separate components , so the consuming application gets to place the boundary, not the library. React Critical The canonical mistake is the all-or-nothing component configured through data. A sidebar that takes items={[{ icon, label, onClick }]} is one fused component: it must carry \"use client\" (it wires handlers), and because functions cannot cross the server-client boundary as props , the page that renders it is forced client too, just to be able to define those onClick s. One prop signature has converted two trees. ✕ Fused: configured with data + functions app layout (forced client) \"use client\" ; < Sidebar items ={[ { icon : < HomeIcon />, label : \"Home\" , onClick : () => router. push ( \"/home\" ), }, ]} /> Here onClick is a function, and functions cannot cross the server-client boundary, so this layout must be a client component just to describe its sidebar. The library made the boundary decision for every consumer. ✓ Composed: structure + leaf items app layout (stays server) < Sidebar > < SidebarLinkItem href = \"/home\" icon ={< HomeIcon />}> Home <\/ SidebarLinkItem > < SidebarLinkItem href = \"/reports\" icon ={< BarIcon />}> Reports <\/ SidebarLinkItem > {/* The one interactive item is an app-level client leaf that owns its handler (§5.7). */} < SignOutItem /> <\/ Sidebar > No directive anywhere in this file: the container and link items are server-safe because navigation is an href, not a click handler. Only the sign-out leaf ships JavaScript, and it owns its own click. Inside the library, that means three small files instead of one big one. Note which of them carries the directive, and which do not need it at all: packages/ui/sidebar/Sidebar.tsx: structure, no directive export function Sidebar ({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) { return <nav className = \"sidebar\" >{children}<\/nav>; } Pure structure: renders anywhere, server included, and ships nothing. packages/ui/sidebar/SidebarLinkItem.tsx: navigation, no directive export function SidebarLinkItem ({ href, icon, children }: SidebarLinkItemProps ) { return ( < Link href ={href} cla"},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"internalize-third-party","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"Internalize third-party components: one wrapper file, one leaf","t":"The first bullet above deserves its worked example, because the failure mode is subtle: it is not just that an undeclared third-party component breaks under a server import. It is that importing it directly from your own components makes every composite that touches it a client component, layout, labels, help text and all, and every component composing those composites after it. ✕ Imported directly: the composite goes client ProgramPicker.tsx \"use client\" ; import Select from \"react-select\" ; export function ProgramPicker ({ programs }) { return ( <div> < Label >Program<\/ Label > < Select options ={ toOptions (programs)} /> < HelpText >Pick one to continue.<\/ HelpText > <\/div> ); } Because react-select is interactive, this entire composite goes client: the layout, the label, the help text. Every component that embeds ProgramPicker inherits the same fate. ✓ Internalized: the third-party part is the leaf ui/Select.tsx, then ProgramPicker.tsx \"use client\" ; export { default as Select } from \"react-select\" ; The third-party component is internalized once, behind our own client file: this wrapper is the leaf, and nothing else needs the directive. ProgramPicker.tsx: no directive import { Select } from \"@/ui/Select\" ; export function ProgramPicker ({ programs }) { return ( <div> < Label >Program<\/ Label > < Select options ={ toOptions (programs)} /> < HelpText >Pick one to continue.<\/ HelpText > <\/div> ); } The composite stays a server component; only the internalized Select ships JavaScript. The wrapper is also the single seam for theming or replacing the library later."},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"searchparams-boundary","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"5.6 Client components that read the URL get their own Suspense boundary","t":"One boundary case is mechanical enough to deserve its own pattern: it sits exactly at the intersection of this chapter and §8 (the URL as state). A client component that calls useSearchParams() must render inside a <Suspense> boundary. Next.js Critical Why: search params are per-request data . While Next.js prerenders the static shell of a page, there is no request, so the value genuinely does not exist yet, and a client component that insists on reading it from the browser has to be able to suspend until it does. What the boundary buys, concretely: The shell stays static. The rest of the page prerenders and streams normally; only the reader of the search params waits for request time. The bailout is scoped. Without a local boundary, the client-side-rendering bailout climbs to the nearest ancestor boundary, often deopting the entire page to client rendering to accommodate one filter input. With one, the \"dynamic hole\" is exactly the size of the leaf. The build passes under PPR. With cacheComponents , a request-time read outside a Suspense boundary isn't a deopt; it's an error. The boundary is how you declare \"this part is dynamic, on purpose.\" Because forgetting the wrapper is easy and the failure is confusing, make the boundary part of the component itself; consumers should never need to remember it: SearchInput.tsx: the boundary is part of the component's public shape \"use client\" ; export function SearchInput (props: SearchInputProps ) { return ( < Suspense fallback ={< SearchInputSkeleton />}> < SearchInputInner {...props} /> <\/ Suspense > ); } // Only the inner component reads the URL, so only it ever suspends. function SearchInputInner (props: SearchInputProps ) { const params = useSearchParams (); // … } The exported component bakes its own Suspense boundary in, so no call site can forget it. The fallback mirrors the input's geometry (same reserved space, §9 ), so nothing shifts when it resolves. Two clarifications to keep the pattern honest. First, this applies to the client read: a server page reading searchParams from its props doesn't need this; the page is simply dynamic at that point, which is §8 's territory. Reach for the client read only in leaves that write the URL and need the live value interactively (filter inputs, sort toggles). Second, useParams and usePathname don't require this: route params and pathname are knowable from the route st"},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"self-contained","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"5.7 Encapsulation in practice: the self-contained button","t":"Everything this chapter has argued comes to a point in one concrete pattern. The purest expression of \"push the boundary down\" is an interactive leaf that owns its entire interaction : the click, the pending state, the call to the server, and any UI the interaction spawns. React Critical Nothing above it participates, which is precisely what lets everything above it stay a server component. ✕ The page owns the interaction app/programs/[id]/page.tsx \"use client\" ; /* One button's mechanics hoisted the whole page into the client. */ export default function ProgramPage () { const [isPublishing, setPublishing] = useState ( false ); return ( < ProgramHeader > < Button loading ={isPublishing} onClick ={ async () => { setPublishing ( true ); await api. publish (programId); setPublishing ( false ); refetchProgram (); }} > Publish <\/ Button > <\/ ProgramHeader > ); } ✓ The leaf owns the interaction app/programs/[id]/page.tsx /* Server component: no \"use client\", no handlers, no loading flags. */ export default async function ProgramPage ( { params } ) { const { id } = await params; const program = await getProgram (id); return ( < ProgramHeader program ={program}> < PublishButton programId ={id} /> <\/ ProgramHeader > ); } The button itself is small, boring, and completely portable. It calls a server action, and useTransition gives it a pending flag for the duration: its loading state, owned locally, exactly as the loading doctrine demands ( §10.3 ; a pending interaction indicator on the control is the legitimate kind of local loading state; it reserves no content space and replaces nothing): components/program/PublishButton.tsx \"use client\" ; import { useTransition } from \"react\" ; /* Yes, a client component can import a server action. The \"use server\" module doesn't pull server code into the bundle: the client receives a typed reference, and calling it becomes a network request the framework manages. This is the one import that crosses the boundary by design. */ import { publishProgram } from \"@/actions/programs\" ; /* The entire interaction lives here: the click, the pending state, and the call to the server. Nothing above this component knows any of it exists. */ export function PublishButton ({ programId }: { programId: string }) { const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition (); return ( < Button loading ={isPending} onClick ={() => startTransition ( async ("},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"refresh","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"5.8 Refreshing the parent without wiring it to the child","t":"Two honest answers exist, and the difference between them is most of this guide's thesis in miniature. Approach 1: a refetch context. Wrap the region in a provider that owns the query and exposes its re-execute function; the button calls useRefetch() and stays ignorant of who actually refires: The context approach: workable, but note what it costs \"use client\" ; const { refetch } = useRefetch (); return ( < Button loading ={isPending} onClick ={() => startTransition ( async () => { await publishProgram (programId); refetch (); }) } > Publish <\/ Button > ); Inside the button: no prop threading, and no knowledge of who the parent is. This is genuinely better than drilling an onSuccess callback through three layers: the intermediate components stay clean. But look at what it presumes: someone above is fetching in client land , or there would be no refetch to offer. The provider and the query pull the boundary back up the tree, and we're client-rendering a region to solve a problem the platform already solves. Acceptable inside a genuinely client island (see the SWR variant below); the wrong default for a page. Approach 2: the server is the source of truth. Next.js Critical The mutation already runs on the server; let the server decide what's stale. The action invalidates the data it changed, Next.js re-renders the server components that read that data, and a fresh RSC payload ( §4.5 ) streams back and merges in place. Nobody calls anybody: actions/programs.ts \"use server\" ; import { revalidateTag } from \"next/cache\" ; export async function publishProgram (id: string ) { try { await db.program. update ({ where : { id }, data : { status : \"published\" } }); } catch (e) { /* Returned, not thrown: the client handler decides how to surface it (the toast in §5.7). */ return { error : \"Could not publish the program. Please try again.\" }; } /* The server is the source of truth. Invalidating the tag re-renders every server component whose data carried it; the parent page updates without ever being told to. revalidatePath() is the coarser equivalent, and router.refresh() covers mutations that didn't go through an action. */ revalidateTag ( `program-${id}` ); return {}; } The mutation cycle with zero wires. The parent renders the button with one prop; the button calls the action; the action invalidates the tag; every server component that read the tagged data re-renders an"},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"swr-refresh","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"When the parent is legitimately client: the key is the contract","t":"Sometimes the parent has earned its \"use client\" , and the canonical case is UI that doesn't exist until the user does something . A drawer that slides open off a row click, a details popover, a preview panel: none of these are on the page when the server renders it, so their content can never ride the first response. When they mount, after the interaction, they fetch client-side, and useSWR is the right tool. The encapsulation pattern survives intact; only the shared infrastructure changes. SWR's cache is keyed, and the key replaces the callback : React Very important components/program/ProgramDrawer.tsx \"use client\" ; import useSWR from \"swr\" ; export function ProgramDrawer ({ programId, onClose }: ProgramDrawerProps ) { const { data: program, isLoading } = useSWR ( [ \"program\" , programId], () => fetchProgram (programId) ); return ( < Drawer onClose ={onClose}> {/* Loading ⊥ data ⊥ empty, handled per §10.6; elided here. */} < ProgramDetails program ={program} isLoading ={isLoading} /> < PublishButton programId ={programId} /> <\/ Drawer > ); } This component is legitimately client: it opens after an interaction, so the server's first render never contains it, and its data is necessarily fetched after mount. It fetches by key, and passes the button an id (identity, nothing else), exactly as the server version did. components/program/PublishButton.tsx (SWR flavor) \"use client\" ; import { mutate } from \"swr\" ; export function PublishButton ({ programId }: { programId: string }) { const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition (); return ( < Button loading ={isPending} onClick ={() => startTransition ( async () => { await publishProgram (programId); await mutate ([ \"program\" , programId]); }) } > Publish <\/ Button > ); } The button revalidates the key when its mutation lands. Every subscriber of that key refires on its own; the button holds no reference to the parent, receives no refetch callback, and works under any parent that uses the same key. Server tag, URL param, SWR key: three spellings of one idea. The leaf and the parent agree on a name for the data, and communicate by invalidating the name. The refetch mechanics live in the infrastructure ( §14 ), not in a callback prop, so the boundary stays at the leaf no matter which spelling the page uses."},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"god-components","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"5.9 The God component: one boundary to lose everything","t":"Now invert every rule in this chapter at once, because our codebase does. Put a \"use client\" component (or its more respectable-looking cousins, a provider or a useXManager() hook) at the top of a tree, and have it do three jobs: fetch the data, own the UI state, and dispense the callbacks , flowing all of it down through props and context: The shape to fear: three jobs in one hook \"use client\" ; export function useProgramsManager () { const [programs, setPrograms] = useState < Program []>(); const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState ( true ); const [filters, setFilters] = useState < Filters >(DEFAULT_FILTERS); const [selectedId, setSelectedId] = useState < string | null >( null ); const [isSaving, setIsSaving] = useState ( false ); useEffect (() => { /* Fetch #1 of the tree: nothing below renders content until this lands. */ fetchPrograms (filters). then ((data) => { setPrograms (data); setIsLoading ( false ); }); }, [filters]); async function saveProgram (input: ProgramInput ) { /* … */ } async function deleteProgram (id: string ) { /* … */ } return { programs, isLoading, filters, setFilters, selectedId, setSelectedId, isSaving, saveProgram, deleteProgram, }; } Three jobs in one hook: it fetches, it owns everyone's UI state, and it defines everyone's callbacks. Every consumer below becomes a client component wired to this one object's shape. That's the hook spelling. The same disease wears a component costume just as often: a page or container that fetches everything in sight, holds state it never reads, and threads all of it down through layers that are nothing but couriers: app/programs/page.tsx: the component spelling \"use client\" ; export default function ProgramsPage () { /* Fetches #1, #2, and #3, none of which the page itself renders. It is collecting cargo on behalf of components two and three levels down. */ const { data: programs, isLoading } = useSWR ( \"/api/programs\" , fetcher); const { data: me } = useSWR ( \"/api/me\" , fetcher); const { data: orgs } = useSWR ( \"/api/organizations\" , fetcher); /* State the page holds only so it can hand it to descendants. The page never filters anything and never selects anything. */ const [filters, setFilters] = useState < Filters >(DEFAULT_FILTERS); const [selectedId, setSelectedId] = useState < string | null >( null ); /* Every layer below is a courier. SectionLayout doesn't use `user` or `orgs`; the toolba"},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"server-actions","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"5.10 Server actions and the HTTP middleman","t":"§5.7 's button called a server action and moved on. It is worth stopping on what that server action actually is, because it is the single biggest lever for pulling mutations back across the boundary, and the reason the 73 pass-through proxy handlers the audit found ( §18 ) are unnecessary. A server action is a typed remote procedure call . When you mark an async function \"use server\" , the bundler splits it out of the client graph, leaves a reference in its place, and the framework generates an endpoint for it. Calling it from the client serializes the arguments, sends them to that endpoint, runs the function on the server, and serializes the result back. You write an ordinary function call; the framework does the transport. Because the function's signature is the contract, the types flow from the component to the server and back with no schema to hand-maintain and no client to keep in sync. The API surface (the directive, useActionState , useFormStatus , <Form> ) is catalogued in §19.2 ; what matters here is the architecture. Next.js Critical The reason it matters beyond ergonomics is what you lose when you put an HTTP layer you own between the UI and the data. The same mutation, two architectures. A server action is one typed boundary from the client straight to your data, so distinct failures stay distinct and the types hold end to end. Funnel it through proxy API routes and each HTTP hop flattens your errors into status codes and adds its own failure modes: network, timeout, serialization. Fewer middlemen, fewer things to handle. When a server action reaches your data directly, through Prisma or a service SDK, a failure is a typed value you handle precisely: the record does not exist, the user is not allowed, a constraint failed, each one distinct and catchable at the call site. Route that same mutation through a Next.js API route that proxies to another HTTP service (the Tracker pattern from §18 ) and every one of those distinct failures is flattened into a status code on the way out and reconstituted on the way back. You also inherit a second set of failure modes that have nothing to do with your domain: network errors, timeouts, retries, serialization mismatches. Each middleman is one more place the types are lost and one more surface of errors the caller has to account for."},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"actions-orm","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"The direct version: the ORM inside the action","t":"Server actions are built for this. The ordinary Prisma pattern (and it works the same with any ORM or query builder) is to use the client directly inside the action : no API between the interaction and the data at all. That buys latency (one process, no serialization hops), flexibility (select exactly the fields this action needs, nothing more), and above all a private error surface : the failure cases are enumerated and handled here, in context, for this one interaction, instead of being designed to satisfy a hundred other callers of a shared endpoint. app/programs/actions.ts - Prisma, no API in between \"use server\" ; import { prisma } from \"@/lib/db\" ; export async function archiveProgram (id: number) { const program = await prisma.program. findUnique ({ where : { id }, // Just the fields THIS action needs to decide. select : { id: true , status: true }, }); /* null is a domain answer, not a status code: the row does not exist. Name the failure for this interaction and return it in the type. */ if (!program) return { error: \"This program no longer exists.\" }; if (program.status === \"archived\" ) return {}; await prisma.program. update ({ where : { id }, data : { status: \"archived\" }, }); revalidateTag ( `program-${id}` ); return {}; } The return type is inferred, so { error } flows to the button in §5.7 with no schema to maintain. A null from findUnique can mean exactly one thing, and the action decides what this interaction should do about it."},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"actions-central-api","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"The centralized API, called directly: still fine","t":"None of this outlaws a dedicated backend. If a centralized API layer exists (other consumers, another team, another runtime), calling it directly from the action is a perfectly good architecture; the action becomes the adapter that narrows generic HTTP semantics into this interaction's domain answers. What you give up, and should give up knowingly, is precision. Compare the error surface: app/programs/actions.ts - centralized API, called directly \"use server\" ; export async function archiveProgram (id: number) { const res = await fetch ( `${API_URL}/programs/${id}/archive` , { method : \"POST\" , headers : authHeaders (), }); /* One status code, several possible truths. 404: the program is gone? the route moved in the last deploy? the resource was renamed? The action has to guess, and the response body's type is whatever we assert. */ if (res.status === 404 ) return { error: \"This program no longer exists.\" }; if (!res.ok) return { error: \"Could not archive the program.\" }; revalidateTag ( `program-${id}` ); return {}; } Same interaction through a shared endpoint. The endpoint's shape and error conventions serve every caller it has, so they can never be tailored to this one; a 404 is ambiguous in ways findUnique returning null never is, and type safety ends at the HTTP boundary. So the ranking is a gradient, not a binary. The ORM inside the action is the optimum: fastest, most flexible, typed end to end, and free of an API whose shape is coupled to a UI. A centralized API called directly from the action is a legitimate step down, taken for organizational reasons, and the action's job becomes translating its generic responses into per-interaction answers. The proxy (your own API route forwarding to the real backend) is the only indefensible rung: server actions make it structurally unnecessary, and it costs a hop of latency, a layer of error ambiguity, and the types, while providing nothing the direct call doesn't. Fewer middlemen The principle is blunt. Use server actions that talk to your real backend services directly, rather than proxying mutations through Next.js API routes on the same app. Every HTTP hop you remove is type safety you keep, latency you save, and a class of errors you no longer have to handle. And once the mutation lands, the parent refreshes through the cache, not a callback ( §5.8 , §14.2 ), so the write stays as decoupled as the read."},{"f":"05-client-server-boundaries.html","a":"rules","n":5,"c":"Client/Server Boundaries","h":"The rules","t":"Rule Scope Importance Components are server components until an interaction atom proves otherwise : push every boundary to the smallest leaf that contains the interaction. Next.js Critical No \"use client\" in the app directory : the scaffold stays server; client code is imported into it. Named exceptions: error.tsx , genuinely single-surface interactive pages. Next.js Very important Interactive shells receive server content via children /slots; never import what they wrap . React Critical Third-party interactive components get a dedicated \"use client\" wrapper file . Next.js Critical Data layer imports server-only ; secrets never ride a boundary move into NEXT_PUBLIC_* . Next.js Very important Providers are thin client wrappers around {children} , close to the root, converting nothing. React Very important Interactive leaves are self-contained: the button owns its click, pending state, action call, and any dialog or floating element it opens . The parent passes identity, nothing else. React Critical After a mutation, the server is the source of truth : revalidateTag / revalidatePath (or a keyed SWR mutate in client islands), never refetch callbacks threaded to leaves. Next.js Critical No God components : nothing may fetch the data, own the UI state, and dispense the callbacks from the top of a tree. React Critical Where to go deeper React 19 docs: 'use client' · Server Components . Next.js docs: Server and Client Components (composition patterns, third-party wrappers, context providers). Full list in §20 ."},{"f":"06-first-render-js.html","a":"","n":6,"c":"First-Render JavaScript","h":"First-Render JavaScript","t":"The known isn't only content; it's also the code required to show it. Every kilobyte of JavaScript the first render demands is a toll paid before the page becomes interactive. This chapter is about keeping that toll to what the first paint actually uses: boundaries first, dynamic() second."},{"f":"06-first-render-js.html","a":"tax","n":6,"c":"First-Render JavaScript","h":"6.1 What first-render JavaScript costs","t":"Server rendering gets content on screen before any JavaScript runs ( §4 ), but the page isn't done at first paint. The client bundle still has to download, parse, and execute before hydration attaches your event handlers, and until then the page is a photograph: visible, correct, and deaf. Everything in the bundle gates that moment, whether or not it participates in it. The dialog no one has opened, the chart on a collapsed tab, the editor behind an \"Edit\" button: if they're in the main bundle, the user pays for them before the first button click works. And the cost isn't limited to interactivity. <Link> can't start prefetching until it hydrates ( §4.5 ), so a heavy bundle doesn't just delay this page, it delays the head start on the next one. Note what doesn't help here: the React Compiler removes re-render busywork, not bytes. The only cure for shipping too much code is shipping less of it. What determines the bundle's size is exactly one thing: the module graphs of your client components ( §5.1 ). Every \"use client\" file, everything it imports, and everything those import: that is the bundle. Server components contribute zero bytes, no matter how heavy their dependencies are; their markdown parsers and date libraries run on the server and stay there ( §4.2 )."},{"f":"06-first-render-js.html","a":"boundary-first","n":6,"c":"First-Render JavaScript","h":"6.2 The first lever is the boundary, not the loader","t":"Because the bundle is the sum of client module graphs, the biggest reductions come from §5 , not from this chapter. A page that moves its boundary from the layout to three leaf buttons hasn't \"optimized its bundle\"; it has deleted most of it. Next.js Critical Get the order of operations right: first ask \"why is this client at all?\", and only then ask \"should this client code load later?\" Lazy-loading a God component ( §5.9 ) is treating a symptom: the page now downloads its architectural mistake slightly later. This chapter's tool, dynamic() , is for the code that has legitimately earned its place on the client but hasn't earned a seat in the first-render bundle. The same page, two bundles. Amber code is invisible at first paint by definition (a closed dialog, an unopened tab), yet in the top bundle every user downloads, parses, and executes it before any button works. Below, the first-render bundle carries only what the first render uses; the rest arrives as separate chunks, triggered by the interactions that reveal them."},{"f":"06-first-render-js.html","a":"dynamic","n":6,"c":"First-Render JavaScript","h":"6.3 dynamic() : loading client code on demand","t":"next/dynamic is React.lazy() plus Suspense in one call: it turns an import into a separate chunk, gives you a loading fallback for the gap, and, critically, moves the download to the moment the component first renders . Combined with conditional rendering, \"first renders\" becomes \"the user actually asked for it\": components/program/AnalyticsSection.tsx \"use client\" ; import dynamic from \"next/dynamic\" ; /* A charting library is parsing and drawing machinery the first paint never uses; the section starts collapsed. The named export comes back from the import() promise; the loading fallback mirrors the chart's geometry so nothing shifts when it lands (§9). */ const EnrollmentChart = dynamic ( () => import ( \"./EnrollmentChart\" ). then ((m) => m.EnrollmentChart), { loading : () => < ChartSkeleton /> } ); export function AnalyticsSection ({ programId }: { programId: string }) { const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState ( false ); return ( < Collapsible expanded ={expanded} onToggle ={() => setExpanded ((prev) => !prev)} > {/* Mounted only when expanded; the chunk downloads on first open. */} {expanded && < EnrollmentChart programId ={programId} />} <\/ Collapsible > ); } This is the same shape as §5.7 's dynamically-imported dialog: encapsulation and lazy loading are natural partners, because the component that owns the interaction is also the only one that knows when the code is actually needed. Two mechanics to get right: Call dynamic() inside client components. Next.js Critical When a server component dynamically imports a client component, the code is not split, and ssr: false there is a build error. Do the splitting in the client file that renders the thing; a server component's own imports never needed splitting in the first place (they ship nothing). ssr: false is for code that can't run on the server at all : libraries that touch window at import time, canvas and map widgets. It skips the server prerender pass entirely, so reach for it only when the component genuinely can't produce server HTML; otherwise you're giving away first-paint content for nothing. Declare lazy components at module top level, never inside a component. React Critical The React docs call this out directly: a lazy() / dynamic() created inside a component body is a new component on every render, so the loaded component unmounts and loses all state each time the parent re-renders. compo"},{"f":"06-first-render-js.html","a":"candidates","n":6,"c":"First-Render JavaScript","h":"6.4 What earns a dynamic import, and what doesn't","t":"The test is one question: can the user see or use it at first paint? If not, its code shouldn't be in the first-render bundle. Candidate Verdict Dialogs, drawers, popovers, command palettes Always dynamic : invisible until an interaction, by definition ( §5.7 ). Heavy libraries: charts, rich-text editors, PDF/file viewers, syntax highlighters, spreadsheet grids Always dynamic : these dwarf your own code; none belong in a main bundle. Panels behind collapsed sections or non-default tabs Dynamic when the content is client code; if the panel is server-rendered, prefer route segments ( §8.3 ) and there's nothing to split. Handler-only libraries: exporters, parsers, fuzzy search await import() in the handler : no component wrapper needed. Small interactive leaves visible at first paint (buttons, inputs, menus) Leave them alone : splitting a 2 kB component adds a round trip and a loading flash to save nothing. The chunk has to be worth its own request. Anything above the fold that the first paint shows Never dynamic with a fallback the user will see : you'd be manufacturing a skeleton for content the server could have delivered finished. Don't guess: measure . React Very important @next/bundle-analyzer shows exactly which imports own the bytes; the browser's coverage panel shows how much of the bundle the first paint actually executed. The two usual convictions: a heavy library imported by a component that's visible for 5% of sessions, and a \"shared utils\" module that drags a whale in through one re-export."},{"f":"06-first-render-js.html","a":"rules","n":6,"c":"First-Render JavaScript","h":"The rules","t":"Rule Scope Importance The bundle is the client module graphs: shrink it with boundaries first ( §5 ), dynamic() second. Next.js Critical Anything invisible at first paint loads behind dynamic() : dialogs, drawers, collapsed panels, non-default tabs. React Very important Heavy libraries never ride the main bundle : charts, editors, viewers split at their leaf; handler-only libraries via await import() . React Very important dynamic() calls live inside client components ; server-side dynamic imports of client components don't split, and ssr: false there is an error. Next.js Critical Don't split the small or the visible : a chunk must be worth its own network round trip. React Important Where to go deeper React 19 docs: lazy · Suspense . Next.js docs: Lazy Loading guide ( next/dynamic , ssr: false , named exports, library imports) · Package Bundling ( @next/bundle-analyzer ). Full list in §20 ."},{"f":"07-bundle-size-code-splitting.html","a":"","n":7,"c":"Bundle Size & Code Splitting","h":"Bundle Size & Code Splitting","t":"§6 was about the mechanics of keeping JavaScript out of the first render. This chapter is about the judgment: which dependencies are worth their weight, why a single heavyweight component can undo the entire known/unknown split, and what all that shipped JavaScript actually costs the people using the app."},{"f":"07-bundle-size-code-splitting.html","a":"tax","n":7,"c":"Bundle Size & Code Splitting","h":"7.1 The JavaScript you ship is a tax on the known","t":"Part A is about maximizing what the server can deliver as finished HTML. Every kilobyte of client JavaScript works against that goal from the other side: the browser has to download it, parse it, and execute it before the page is truly interactive, and until then the \"known\" content you worked so hard to render on the server is sitting there looking ready while doing nothing. A page can be server-rendered and still feel broken if it ships a megabyte of JavaScript in front of the first interaction. So bundle size is not a separate performance chore bolted on at the end; it is the same goal as the rest of Part A, measured at the network instead of the render. The good news is that the App Router splits code by route automatically: a given page ships the JavaScript for its own tree, not the whole app. The bad news is that this automatic splitting is easy to defeat, and the rest of this chapter is a tour of the three most common ways we do it: pulling in a component so heavy it drags the page down, fusing the known and the unknown into one undeferrable lump, and importing through barrel files that quietly negate tree-shaking."},{"f":"07-bundle-size-code-splitting.html","a":"wholesale","n":7,"c":"Bundle Size & Code Splitting","h":"7.2 The wholesale-component trap","t":"Here is the most instructive failure, because it shows how bundle size and the known/unknown thesis are the same problem. Picture a reports page with a data table. Look at what is actually knowable at first render: the toolbar, the title, the column headers, the footer, the pagination controls, the empty-state and the row skeleton are all known immediately . The only genuinely unknown part is the body rows, because those depend on data we have to fetch. Now reach for a wholesale data grid, an all-in-one client component like MUI's DataGrid that owns the toolbar, the headers, the rows, the sorting, the virtualization, and the footer in a single package. You have just fused everything back together. The frame that was knowable is now trapped inside a client component that cannot render until its bundle downloads and its data arrives. The same table, two architectures. On the left, a wholesale grid fuses the knowable frame to the unknowable rows, so nothing appears until the bundle and the data both arrive. On the right, the frame is server-rendered HTML that shows on the first byte, and a §10 boundary wraps only the rows. You cannot get the right-hand behavior out of the left-hand component. The trap is specifically that a wholesale grid takes all three of your best tools off the table at once. You cannot wrap Suspense around just the body rows , because the rows aren't a separate component. You cannot show a skeleton over just the rows while the header stays put, for the same reason. And you cannot defer the import of just the rows and render the known frame immediately, because the frame and the rows are one import. The composed version, a server-rendered table shell with a Suspense boundary around a server component that awaits the rows, gets all three for free. app/reports/hours/page.tsx (server component) import { Suspense } from \"react\" ; export default function HoursPage () { return ( <table> {/* The frame is known on the first byte: server HTML, no client JS. */} < TableToolbar /> < TableHead columns ={COLUMNS} /> {/* Only the rows are unknown, so only the rows suspend. */} < Suspense fallback ={< RowSkeleton rows ={10} />}> < HoursRows /> <\/ Suspense > < TableFooter /> <\/table> ); } The same contrast, seen as an actual page rather than a stack of boxes, makes the trade-off obvious. It is the anatomy view from §2 , aimed at one region: watch what happe"},{"f":"07-bundle-size-code-splitting.html","a":"heavyweight","n":7,"c":"Bundle Size & Code Splitting","h":"7.3 Heavyweight imports, and fallbacks that stay light","t":"Sometimes you genuinely need a heavy client component: a rich text editor, a charting library, a map, a spreadsheet grid whose interactivity you can't rebuild by hand. The rule there is not \"never use it,\" it is keep it out of the first-render bundle and give it a light fallback . In our own ojl-tracker work, the data-heavy tables are loaded lazily for exactly this reason: the route paints its frame immediately, and the heavy grid streams in behind a skeleton rather than blocking the whole page on its download. The mechanics of the dynamic import (the ssr: false option, the top-level declaration rule) live in §6.3 ; what matters here is the fallback. The fallback must be lighter than the thing it stands in for. A common mistake is to hand the loading state another heavy component, or a spinner that itself pulls in an animation library. The fallback should be cheap, static markup that reserves the right space ( §9 ): a plain skeleton, sized to match. components/reports/HoursGrid.tsx \"use client\" ; import dynamic from \"next/dynamic\" ; /* The heavy grid is pulled out of the first-render bundle and loaded on demand. Its fallback is deliberately minimal: a static skeleton that reserves the right height, not another heavy component. */ const DataGrid = dynamic (() => import ( \"./HeavyDataGrid\" ), { loading: () => < RowSkeleton rows ={10} />, ssr: false , }); export function HoursGrid ({ rows }: { rows: Row [] }) { return < DataGrid rows ={rows} />; } Deferring a heavy import is a fallback, not a fix Lazy-loading a wholesale grid is strictly better than shipping it in the first bundle, but it is still the second-best answer. It hides the weight; it doesn't remove it, and the user still pays the download the moment the component is needed. Whenever the frame is genuinely knowable, prefer the composed server-rendered table from §7.2 and reserve the heavy client component for the parts that truly need its interactivity."},{"f":"07-bundle-size-code-splitting.html","a":"barrels","n":7,"c":"Bundle Size & Code Splitting","h":"7.4 Barrel files quietly defeat code splitting","t":"A barrel file is an index.ts that re-exports everything in a folder so callers can write import { Button } from \"@/ui\" instead of reaching into the specific module. It reads nicely and it is a reliable way to inflate every bundle that touches it. The problem is that importing one name from a barrel makes your route depend on the whole barrel: the bundler now has to consider every module the barrel re-exports, and anything with a side effect, or anything tree-shaking can't prove is unused, comes along for the ride. ✕ Barrel: one name pulls the whole package components/Toolbar.tsx \"use client\" ; import { Button } from \"@/ui\" ; Pulling from the @/ui barrel adds the whole library to this route's graph: Button drags in Modal, DatePicker, RichTextEditor, and everything else the barrel re-exports. ✓ Direct: one name pulls one module components/Toolbar.tsx \"use client\" ; import { Button } from \"@/ui/button\" ; Imports only Button's module; nothing else in @/ui joins this route's bundle. Modern bundlers and Next's optimizePackageImports can claw some of this back, and for well-behaved side-effect-free packages the damage is smaller than it used to be. But the reliable habit is to not create the problem: import from the specific module, and don't ship index.ts barrels for your own component libraries . This is also where bundle size meets component design: barrels tend to grow around files that hold too many components, which is the same instinct §15 argues against. One component per file, imported directly, is both cleaner to read and cheaper to ship."},{"f":"07-bundle-size-code-splitting.html","a":"cost","n":7,"c":"Bundle Size & Code Splitting","h":"7.5 What bloated pages actually cost","t":"It is worth being concrete about who pays, because \"big bundle\" sounds abstract and the costs are not. They land on the two groups we care most about: the people using the app and the people building it. Who pays What it costs them Users, on first load A longer wait before the page is interactive, even after the server HTML is on screen. The gap between \"looks ready\" and \"responds to a click\" is pure JavaScript download, parse, and execution time. Users, on every navigation Heavier route bundles make client transitions feel sluggish, and the pain scales with the worst device and network in your user base, not your laptop on office wifi. Mobile and poor connections feel it first and worst. Users, on data Megabytes of JavaScript are megabytes of someone's mobile data plan and battery, spent re-shipping code that a server render would have turned into a few kilobytes of HTML. The team, on build Bigger dependency graphs mean slower builds and slower type-checks, a tax on every commit and every CI run, and a compounding drag on how fast the team can move."},{"f":"07-bundle-size-code-splitting.html","a":"rules","n":7,"c":"Bundle Size & Code Splitting","h":"The rules","t":"Rule Scope Importance Keep the knowable frame out of client components. Render toolbars, headers, and footers as server HTML; wrap only the unknown parts in a boundary. Don't reach for a wholesale grid that fuses them. React Critical Heavy client components load on demand, out of the first-render bundle, behind a fallback that is lighter than what it replaces. Next.js Very important Import from the specific module, not a barrel. Don't ship index.ts barrels for your own component libraries; they defeat tree-shaking. React Very important Measure first-render JS as a budget, not an afterthought; a server-rendered page that ships a megabyte of JavaScript is not a fast page. React Important Where to go deeper §6 for the dynamic-import mechanics · Next.js: Lazy Loading · Next.js: Package Bundling (bundle analyzer, optimizePackageImports ) · React: lazy . Full list in §20 ."},{"f":"08-server-resolvable-state.html","a":"","n":8,"c":"Server-Resolvable State","h":"Server-Resolvable State","t":"The first render is a function of the request. Nothing more. State the request carries (the URL, route params, search params, cookies) is known ; state living in a component's memory is invisible to the server, and every pixel that depends on it is unknown. This chapter is about putting state where the server can see it, which mostly means: use the URL more."},{"f":"08-server-resolvable-state.html","a":"principle","n":8,"c":"Server-Resolvable State","h":"8.1 The principle: state the server can see is content the server can finish","t":"When a request arrives, the server knows exactly four kinds of state: the path (which page, and its route params), the search params (the query string), the cookies , and the headers . That's the complete list. Everything the first render shows must be derived from those, so every piece of UI state you store anywhere else is a piece of UI the server cannot finish. Next.js Critical Watch what happens when a filtered table keeps its filter in useState . The server renders the page with… which rows? It doesn't know the filter: the state won't exist until a client component mounts and initializes it. So the table can't be server-rendered with real content; it becomes a client component that fetches after hydration, and we've bought the entire Part B problem set (spinners, shifting, waterfalls; §4.2 ) for a filter that would have fit in twelve characters of query string. Move the same state into the URL and the request is the state: the server reads ?status=active , renders the right rows into HTML, and the \"state management\" is the platform's. The bonus features come free, and they're not small. State in the URL is shareable (paste the link, get the view), refresh-proof (F5 reproduces the page instead of resetting it), navigable (back/forward walk through filter history), deep-linkable (dashboards, emails, and docs can point at a specific view), and supportable (\"send me the link\" replaces \"describe every dropdown you touched\"). A view that lives in component state has none of these; it dies with the tab. One URL, /programs/42/reports?q=algebra&page=3 , is a complete, serialized description of this view: the page, the active tab, the search text, and the pagination all resolve from it, on the server, into finished HTML. Anyone opening this link gets exactly this screen; nothing about it needs a client fetch or a useState to exist."},{"f":"08-server-resolvable-state.html","a":"url-state","n":8,"c":"Server-Resolvable State","h":"8.2 The URL is the state manager","t":"What belongs in it: filters, search text, sort column and direction, pagination, tabs, view modes (table/grid), date ranges, expanded-group state on reports, selected item when it drives a panel ; in short, anything that describes which view of the data the user is looking at . Next.js Very important The reading side is a server page prop; both are Promises in Next 16: app/programs/[programId]/reports/page.tsx export default async function ReportsPage ({ params, searchParams }: PageProps ) { const { programId } = await params; const { q = \"\" , page = \"1\" } = await searchParams; /* The URL fully determines this render. The same link produces the same page for anyone, the rows arrive as HTML, and there is no client-side state to hydrate, restore, or lose. */ const reports = await getReports (programId, { q, page : Number (page) }); return < ReportTable reports ={reports} />; } The writing side you've already seen: an interactive leaf updates the query string with router.replace() inside a transition, and the server page re-renders with fresh rows: §5.8 has the search input, §10.5 builds the full table around it, and §5.6 covers the Suspense boundary the reading leaf must carry. Two writing details worth pinning down: replace vs push is a product decision about the back button. Keystrokes and minor refinements should replace (back shouldn't step through every character typed); discrete view changes (a tab, a page number) can push so back walks the user's actual path. Reading a search param makes that part of the render request-time , which is exactly why it must sit behind a Suspense boundary under PPR ( §5.6 ): the static shell prerenders, and the param-dependent region is a deliberate dynamic hole ( §4.4 )."},{"f":"08-server-resolvable-state.html","a":"tabs","n":8,"c":"Server-Resolvable State","h":"8.3 Tabs are routes in disguise","t":"The clearest instance of the whole principle, and the smell we most need to unlearn, is tabs held in useState . A tab bar is a set of views with identities. \"Which view am I looking at?\" is precisely the kind of state the URL exists to answer, and holding it in memory forfeits everything at once: the link can't say which tab it points at, refresh snaps back to the default, the server can't render the active panel's content, and, since every panel must be reachable by a client-side click, all the panels ship to the client, rendered or not ( §6 ). ✕ Tabs in state: the smell ProgramView.tsx \"use client\" ; export function ProgramView () { const [tab, setTab] = useState ( \"reports\" ); return ( <> < TabButton active ={tab === \"reports\" } onClick ={() => setTab ( \"reports\" )} > Reports <\/ TabButton > {tab === \"reports\" && < Reports />} {tab === \"roster\" && < Roster />} <\/> ); } View identity held in memory: not shareable, not refresh-proof, invisible to the server, and every panel ships to the client whether shown or not. ✓ Tabs as route segments the route structure app/ └── programs/ └── [programId]/ ├── layout.tsx ├── reports/ │ ├── page.tsx │ ├── loading.tsx │ └── error.tsx ├── roster/ │ ├── page.tsx │ ├── loading.tsx │ └── error.tsx └── settings/ ├── page.tsx ├── loading.tsx └── error.tsx The header and tab bar render once in layout.tsx and persist across tab changes ( §3.1 ). Each tab is a segment whose page.tsx is server-rendered, streamed, and prefetched by its Link, and each brings its own loading.tsx and error.tsx boundaries ( §10.2 ). With segments, each tab is a page: server-rendered with real content, prefetched by its <Link> before the click ( §4.5 ), and addressable from anywhere. The layout (header, tab bar, containers) persists across switches without re-rendering ( §3.1 , §9.4 ). The tab bar itself is a row of links; its only client concern is knowing which one is active: components/program/ProgramTabs.tsx \"use client\" ; import { usePathname } from \"next/navigation\" ; /* A tab bar is a row of links. Active state comes from the URL, so it survives refresh, travels in a shared link, and the server renders the right panel on the first response. usePathname doesn't need a Suspense boundary; only the query string is per-request (§5.6). */ export function ProgramTabs ({ programId }: { programId: string }) { const pathname = usePathname (); return ( < Tab"},{"f":"08-server-resolvable-state.html","a":"url-writes","n":8,"c":"Server-Resolvable State","h":"8.4 Writing the URL: transitions, stale content, and optimistic tabs","t":"Reading the URL is half the contract; the other half is writing it, because writing the URL is how a client leaf forces the server to re-render. Next.js Critical When a leaf calls router.replace() with a new query string, the router performs a client-side transition to the same route: the server re-renders the page with the new searchParams , and the fresh RSC payload merges into the tree in place ( §4.5 ). Watch the network tab while it happens: no document reload, just a small RSC request carrying the re-rendered segments. The URL write is the refetch. Three refinements turn that mechanic into UI that feels instant. First, wrap the write in a transition : the App Router treats navigations as transitions, so an already-revealed Suspense boundary keeps its current content instead of re-showing a fallback ( §10.5 ), and your own useTransition gives you isPending as the loading indicator. Second, show the stale content while the new render is in flight : dim it against isPending , or reach for useDeferredValue when the results are client-rendered; both worked examples live in §10.5 . Third, and this is the one that makes tabs feel native: update the selection optimistically. The problem with URL-derived tab state alone: the active highlight comes from searchParams , which only changes when the server render lands. Click a tab and the highlight lags behind by the length of the render. React 19's useOptimistic closes that gap: set the optimistic value immediately inside the transition, and React reconciles it with the URL-derived value automatically when the new payload arrives (or reverts it if the navigation fails): ViewTabs.tsx (inner; the baked-in Suspense wrapper of §5.6 is omitted) \"use client\" ; export function ViewTabsInner () { const searchParams = useSearchParams (); const router = useRouter (); const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition (); /* The URL is the source of truth; the optimistic value fronts for it while the server render is in flight. */ const urlView = searchParams. get ( \"view\" ) ?? \"reports\" ; const [view, setOptimisticView] = useOptimistic (urlView); return ( < TabList data-pending ={isPending}> {TABS.map((tab) => ( < TabButton key ={tab.value} active ={view === tab.value} onClick ={() => startTransition (() => { setOptimisticView (tab.value); const params = new URLSearchParams (searchParams); params. set ( \"view\" , tab.value);"},{"f":"08-server-resolvable-state.html","a":"cookies","n":8,"c":"Server-Resolvable State","h":"8.5 Cookies: preferences that follow the user, not the URL","t":"Some state should survive across pages and sessions but has no business in a link: the collapsed sidebar, a table-density preference, the dismissed-announcement flag, theme. Put those in cookies (the other channel the request carries), and the server resolves them at first render, which is what kills the flash-of-wrong-state that localStorage approaches are doomed to (the server can't read localStorage , so it always renders the default first and corrects after hydration). Next.js Very important app/(dashboard)/layout.tsx import { cookies } from \"next/headers\" ; export default async function DashboardLayout ({ children }: LayoutProps ) { /* A preference that must survive across pages and sessions, and that the server needs at first render to avoid a flash of the wrong layout: a cookie, not useState, not localStorage. */ const store = await cookies (); const collapsed = store. get ( \"sidebar-collapsed\" )?.value === \"true\" ; return < Shell sidebarCollapsed ={collapsed}>{children}<\/ Shell >; } The same caveat as search params applies: reading cookies makes that part of the tree request-time, so under PPR it belongs inside a deliberate dynamic region ( §4.4 ). The rule of thumb for choosing the channel: the URL describes the view, cookies describe the viewer. A filter is the view: anyone with the link should get it. A collapsed sidebar is the viewer: it would be bizarre in a shared link."},{"f":"08-server-resolvable-state.html","a":"client-state","n":8,"c":"Server-Resolvable State","h":"8.6 What genuinely stays client state","t":"The point is not that useState is bad; it's that it should hold interaction , not identity . Ephemeral, mid-gesture, this-tab-only state is exactly what it's for: the text in an input before it's committed to the URL (the debounced draft in §10.5 ), the open/closed flag of a dialog or menu living in its leaf ( §5.7 ), hover and focus, an in-flight optimistic value, a drag mid-drag. React Very important Two questions sort every case. Would the user expect it back after a refresh, or want it in a shared link? Then it's the URL (view) or a cookie (preference). Does the server need it to render real content? Then it must be server-resolvable, full stop. Content the server can't finish is Part B's territory, and we only go there deliberately. If both answers are no, it's interaction state: keep it in the leaf, in memory, where it costs nothing."},{"f":"08-server-resolvable-state.html","a":"rules","n":8,"c":"Server-Resolvable State","h":"The rules","t":"Rule Scope Importance State the first render depends on must be server-resolvable : the URL, route params, search params, or cookies; never component memory. Next.js Critical Filters, search, sort, pagination, tabs, view modes, date ranges: the view lives in the URL . Next.js Very important Tabs are routes : segments first, a search param second, useState only for cosmetic switches, deliberately. Next.js Very important Cross-page, cross-session preferences go in cookies, read on the server : the URL describes the view; cookies describe the viewer. Next.js Very important useState holds interaction, not identity : drafts mid-edit, open leaf widgets, hover/focus, in-flight optimism. React Very important Where to go deeper React 19 docs: Choosing the State Structure · Sharing State Between Components (lifting state, and why the URL is the ultimate lift). Next.js docs: page.js: params and searchParams · useSearchParams · cookies · Linking and Navigating . Full list in §20 ."},{"f":"09-containers-content-shifting.html","a":"","n":9,"c":"Containers & Content Shifting","h":"Containers & Content Shifting","t":"Containers are the known half of \"contain the unknown\": they render immediately, they reserve accurate space, and everything uncertain happens inside them. Get this right and nothing on the page ever moves; get it wrong and every request landing is a small earthquake."},{"f":"09-containers-content-shifting.html","a":"cost","n":9,"c":"Containers & Content Shifting","h":"9.1 What content shifting costs","t":"Content shift is the page rearranging itself while the user is already using it. The costs are concrete: the paragraph they were reading jumps out from under their eyes; the button they were about to press moves and they press what replaced it; scroll position stops meaning anything while the page is still \"settling.\" Each shift also re-runs layout for everything below it. On long pages, repeatedly. And users read instability as unreliability: a page that jolts three times before it settles feels broken even when every request succeeded. This is measured in the field as Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) , one of Google's Core Web Vitals: every unexpected shift is scored by how much of the viewport it moved, and the scores accumulate. But the metric is just the symptom sheet. The disease has one cause: something rendered without its space having been reserved. And SPA-style pages have it worst, because when all data arrives after hydration ( §3 ), every region is a late arrival landing in unreserved space, one request at a time, in whatever order the network decides. The same late-arriving stats block, frame by frame. Top: no space was held, so the landing displaces the text and the Save button mid-click. Bottom: the container held the space from first paint; the swap is invisible to everything around it."},{"f":"09-containers-content-shifting.html","a":"doctrine","n":9,"c":"Containers & Content Shifting","h":"9.2 The container doctrine","t":"The fix is a strict separation of ownership. Split every uncertain region into two roles: The container is known. It renders immediately, from the server, with the page's first paint: its chrome (card, border, heading, toolbar) and, critically, its dimensions . It is part of the scaffold, so it belongs with the structure ( §3 's app directory, §9.4 below). React Critical The occupants are swapped inside it. First the fallback, then the content - both rendered into the container, both sized to fit the reservation. The Suspense boundary sits just inside the container's walls ( §10 ). Three rules make the separation real, and each one exists because breaking it produces a specific visible bug: Rule What breaking it looks like The loading state never includes the container it sits inside. Double chrome for a moment (a card inside a card), then a jolt as the real content's chrome replaces the fallback's. The suspended component never renders its own container. Same double-chrome bug from the other side: the content arrives wearing a card the container already provides. Both occupants fit the space the container reserved : fallback and content, same footprint. The swap itself shifts layout: a 3-bar skeleton replaced by a 400px table moves everything below it, boundary or not. ✕ Occupants own chrome and size page.tsx < Suspense fallback ={< StatsSkeleton />}> < ProgramStats programId ={id} /> <\/ Suspense > StatsSkeleton.tsx export function StatsSkeleton () { return ( < Card title = \"Stats\" > < Bars count ={ 3 } /> <\/ Card > ); } The fallback brings its own card: chrome that is torn down and rebuilt when the content arrives. Then ProgramStats renders another Card, and since neither declares a height, the swap reflows the page anyway. ✓ Container owns chrome and size; occupants fill it page.tsx < StatsCard > < Suspense fallback ={< StatsSkeleton />}> < ProgramStats programId ={id} /> <\/ Suspense > <\/ StatsCard > The container is server markup in the scaffold: chrome plus reserved height, painted with the shell. StatsCard.tsx - server, known, immediate export function StatsCard ({ children }) { return ( <section className = \"card\" > <h3>Stats<\/h3> {/* The reservation lives here; both occupants render into this box. */} <div className = \"h-24\" >{children}<\/div> <\/section> ); }"},{"f":"09-containers-content-shifting.html","a":"reserving","n":9,"c":"Containers & Content Shifting","h":"9.3 Reserving space honestly","t":"A reservation is a promise about the future: the container claims the space its content will need. The strategies, in order of preference: Exact dimensions, when the content's size is knowable. A paginated table with 10 rows of 44px is 440px before any data exists: rows-per-page times row height is the reservation. Media is the canonical case: always give images explicit width / height (or aspect-ratio ) so the browser reserves before loading; next/image enforces this. React Critical min-height , when only the floor is knowable. Reserve the minimum honest footprint: enough that the common case doesn't shift, growth is rare and downward. A dishonest reservation (a token 40px under a region that always renders 400px) is just a smaller earthquake. Flex-grow and scroll, for the main content region. The strongest reservation is the viewport itself: the page frame is a column that clips itself ( h-full max-h-full overflow-hidden ): header and toolbars sized by content, the content region growing ( grow ) with overflow-y: auto . The page as a whole never changes height ; tables can grow, filters can change the row count, and all of it happens inside a scroll area whose outer box is fixed. This is the default posture for every list/table page we build. React Very important app/(admin)/reports/layout.tsx - the flex-grow-and-scroll frame export default function ReportsLayout ({ children }) { return ( {/* The column is exactly viewport height; the page never grows. */} <div className = \"flex h-full max-h-full overflow-hidden flex-col\" > < ReportsHeader /> {/* The content region takes the remainder and scrolls inside. Whatever streams, loads, or grows in here, the frame holds. */} <main className = \"min-h-0 grow overflow-y-auto\" > {children} <\/main> <\/div> ); } The flex-grow-and-scroll frame from the snippet above, on the page. The app layout owns the chrome: the app header bar across the top and the sidebar down the left. ReportsLayout is the dashed region beside the sidebar and beneath the app header: a column that clips itself ( h-full max-h-full overflow-hidden ). Inside it, <ReportsHeader /> is a page-level header (not the app header), sized by its content; the main region takes grow and overflow-y-auto , stretching to fill everything beneath the reports header and scrolling its rows internally when they overflow. The chrome, the frame, and both headers stay put no"},{"f":"09-containers-content-shifting.html","a":"layouts","n":9,"c":"Containers & Content Shifting","h":"9.4 Containers live in the scaffold","t":"Where should containers be rendered? As high as their stability allows, which usually means in the layout or page, as part of the app directory's scaffold ( §3 ). Next.js Very important The payoff is navigation: layouts persist across route changes; they don't re-render when you move between sibling pages ( §3.1 ). A container rendered in the layout is therefore stable not just against its own content loading, but against navigation itself : moving from one program's report to another swaps what's inside the containers while the frame (header, tabs, table chrome) never repaints. The page stops feeling like a sequence of pages and starts feeling like an application. This is also the bridge to §12 : once containers live in the layout, each one can be filled independently: its own data, its own Suspense boundary, its own error state. That's exactly what parallel routes formalize ( §12.3 ): slots in the layout's scaffold, filled as independent units."},{"f":"09-containers-content-shifting.html","a":"rules","n":9,"c":"Containers & Content Shifting","h":"The rules","t":"Rule Scope Importance Containers are known, server-rendered, and painted with the first response : chrome and dimensions both. React Critical The loading state never includes the container ; the suspended component never renders its own container . React Critical Fallback and content share one footprint : the reservation is honest, and the swap moves nothing. React Critical List and table pages use the flex-grow-and-scroll frame : the page never changes height; growth happens inside the scroll region. React Very important Media always declares its dimensions ( next/image , aspect-ratio ); fonts load via next/font . Next.js Critical Containers render as high as their stability allows : usually the layout, so navigation reuses them. Next.js Very important Where to go deeper React 19 docs: <Suspense> (fallback semantics this chapter builds on). Next.js docs: next/image · next/font · layout.js . Field metric: Cumulative Layout Shift (web.dev) . Full list in §20 ."},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"Suspense & Streaming","t":"Loading is not a component concern. It's a structural one. Suspense moves \"what shows while we wait\" out of components and into the tree, where the framework can stream around it. This chapter covers how suspension works, what triggers it, and the loading-state doctrine that follows from it."},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"mechanism","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"10.1 How Suspense actually works","t":"A <Suspense> boundary is a declaration about the tree: \"everything below this point may not be ready yet; here is what to show in its place.\" When a component inside the boundary suspends (it's waiting on something asynchronous), React walks up the tree to the nearest boundary, renders its fallback there, and, when the wait resolves, swaps the real content into the exact same slot. The component itself never knows any of this happened. That's the entire point: the waiting is handled above the component, by structure, not inside it, by state. The lifecycle of one suspended region. The boundary lives inside a known container that reserves space, so the fallback → content swap moves nothing around it; this is the mechanical link between Suspense and content shifting."},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"triggers","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"What triggers a Suspense boundary","t":"These are framework mechanics, worth knowing precisely: Critical An async server component awaiting inside the boundary: the canonical App Router case. The server streams the fallback, keeps working, and streams the content when the await resolves. {/* The await inside ReportRows suspends this boundary. */} < Suspense fallback ={< RowsSkeleton />}> < ReportRows programId ={id} /> <\/ Suspense > async function ReportRows ({ programId }) { const rows = await getRows (programId); return < TableBody rows ={rows} />; } use(promise) in a client component: React 19's way to read a promise (often one passed down from a server component) with Suspense semantics. \"use client\" ; function Comments ({ commentsPromise }) { /* Suspends until the promise (started on the server) resolves. */ const comments = use (commentsPromise); return < CommentList comments ={comments} />; } Suspense-enabled data hooks : SWR with suspense: true , React Query's useSuspenseQuery . \"use client\" ; function ProgramStats ({ programId }) { /* No isLoading flag: the hook suspends, the boundary shows the fallback. */ const { data } = useSWR ([ \"stats\" , programId], fetcher, { suspense : true }); return < StatsRow stats ={data} />; } lazy() components and dynamic imports while their code loads ( §6 ). const EnrollmentChart = dynamic (() => import ( \"./EnrollmentChart\" )); {/* While the chunk downloads, the boundary shows the fallback. */} < Suspense fallback ={< ChartSkeleton />}> < EnrollmentChart /> <\/ Suspense > useSearchParams() in a client component requires a boundary above it during prerendering: a mandatory, mechanical case with its own dedicated pattern in §5.6 . \"use client\" ; function SearchInputInner () { /* Must render under a Suspense boundary; §5.6 bakes it into the component. */ const params = useSearchParams (); // … } Suspense is not a server-only feature Notice that three of those five triggers fire in the browser. It is a common blind spot to think of Suspense as a server-rendering mechanism: a client component that reads a promise with use() , a Suspense-enabled data hook, or a lazy() chunk all suspend and reveal through the same boundary. The boundary doesn't care which side of the network its child runs on; it cares only that the child is waiting. So the whole doctrine of this chapter, that the boundary owns the loading state and the component never rolls its own, applies to g"},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"streaming","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"10.2 Streaming: Suspense on the server","t":"Without Suspense, server rendering is all-or-nothing: the response can't start until every fetch on the page finishes, so the slowest request sets the time-to-first-byte for the entire page. With Suspense boundaries, the server streams the page in order of readiness: the shell (layout, containers, everything known) goes out immediately, and each suspended region follows as its data resolves, injected into its reserved slot without re-rendering anything around it. (Making the waits themselves small and parallel, rather than just streaming around them, is §12 's subject.) Recreated from the Next.js docs' streaming illustration: same fetches, same durations; blocking SSR gates the whole page on the slowest fetch, while streaming sends the known shell at once and fills each suspended region by readiness. Two placement rules follow: Next.js Critical loading.tsx is a route-level Suspense boundary the framework wires up for you: it wraps the page and shows instantly on navigation. Use it for the route transition, but treat it as the coarsest tool: a whole-page skeleton is exactly the page-level skeleton we avoid ( §10.4 ). Prefer a loading.tsx that renders the page's known scaffold, with granular boundaries inside the page doing the real work. Granular boundaries around each data-dependent region : one per self-fetching component ( §13.3 ), placed inside the known container ( §9 ). This is also what Partial Prerendering builds on: with cacheComponents , everything outside your Suspense boundaries becomes the static shell served instantly, and the boundaries define exactly where dynamic content streams in. It helps to see what these files literally are . You never write the wrappers; Next.js assembles them around your page from the files' presence alone: loading.tsx and error.tsx are sugar for boundaries Next.js wraps around your page (pattern from the Next.js docs). In contain-the-unknown terms: the Layout is the known scaffold and never unmounts; Suspense contains the waiting; the ErrorBoundary contains the failing; and the page itself knows about none of it."},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"fallback-trade","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"Every boundary is a decision: an unblocked paint, for a fallback","t":"A Suspense boundary is not free structure; it is a trade, and it helps to name both sides of it. What you buy: the page stops waiting for that region's data. The server ships the shell the moment it is ready, the browser paints it, and the slow region streams into its slot later; without the boundary, that one fetch holds the entire response hostage (the first diagram above ). What you pay: something must now occupy that region's space in the meantime. A boundary forces a fallback into existence, and the fallback is real UI: it has to reserve honest dimensions ( §9 ), look intentional next to finished content, and hand off to the real thing in a seamless swap. That makes \"should this suspend?\" a per-region judgment call, not a rule you can apply mechanically. Most of the time the answer is yes: suspend and stream, because one slow fetch must not set the time-to-first-paint for content that was ready. But the trade tilts the other way when a good fallback cannot be authored. If the region's height is genuinely unknowable in advance, the swap will shift content no matter how careful the skeleton is; if the data resolves in a handful of milliseconds, the fallback is a flash of noise that was never worth painting. In those cases it is legitimate to let the server block on the fetch, provided the wait is kept small and parallel ( §12 ). The two questions to ask per region First: is this data slow enough, often enough, that the rest of the page should not wait for it? If no, block and render it finished. If yes: can I draw a fallback that reserves this region's true shape? If yes, suspend. If no, fix the container until the answer is yes ( §9 ); shipping the shift is not one of the options."},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"no-own-loading","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"10.3 Components must not define their own loading states","t":"React Critical A component that owns its own loading indicator, whether that's a spinner, a skeleton, or any other placeholder, has made three decisions it has no business making: that it is worth waiting for (its parent's composition decides that), where the waiting UI appears (the boundary above decides that), and what the rest of the page does meanwhile (it can't know). The costs show up immediately: Loading becomes un-composable. Compose three self-spinning components and you get three spinners resolving at different moments: spinner cascades, each swap shifting layout. With boundaries, the composing parent decides: one boundary around all three, or one each, without touching any of them. The loading UI is welded to the data mechanism. Move the fetch from client to server, add a cache, wrap it in a transition, and the hand-rolled isLoading plumbing has to be rewritten every time. A fallback on a boundary survives all of those changes. It's invisible to the framework. Streaming, PPR, and transition semantics all operate on boundaries. A component-owned spinner opts out of every one of them. ✕ Component owns its loading state ProgramStats.tsx \"use client\" ; export function ProgramStats ({ programId }) { const { data, isLoading } = useProgram (programId); /* This component just decided that the page shows a spinner here. Nobody composing it gets a say. */ if (isLoading) return < Spinner />; return < StatsRow stats ={data.stats} />; } ✓ Boundary owns it: component just renders ProgramStats.tsx export async function ProgramStats ({ programId }) { const program = await getProgram (programId); return < StatsRow stats ={program.stats} />; } page.tsx - the composer decides < StatsContainer > < Suspense fallback ={< StatsSkeleton />}> < ProgramStats programId ={id} /> <\/ Suspense > <\/ StatsContainer >"},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"skeletons","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"10.4 Skeletons: scoped, colocated, structural","t":"The container doctrine is §9 's subject: containers known and immediate, fallback and content swapped inside them, one shared footprint, and neither occupant bringing its own chrome. All of it applies verbatim to fallbacks: the fallback is simply the container's first occupant. React Critical What belongs to this chapter is the fallback itself, the skeleton: Scope skeletons per server component (or per client component when needed), never per page. Very important A page-level skeleton is a second, shadow implementation of the entire page layout. It drifts the moment anyone changes the real page, and nobody remembers to update it; the maintainability cost compounds with every route. A per-component skeleton is small, sits next to the component it mirrors ( StatsRow.tsx / StatsSkeleton.tsx ), and changes in the same PR when the component changes. Keep skeletons structural, not photorealistic. They exist to hold reserved space and signal \"coming\": a few bars matching the content's rough geometry. The more detailed the skeleton, the faster it drifts. Skeletons appear exactly once per region: on first load. Never on refetch; that's the next section."},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"refetch","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"10.5 Refetches show stale content, never fallbacks","t":"React Critical A skeleton answers the question \"what goes here?\", a question that's only ever open once . On a refetch, the answer is already on screen. Replacing real content with a skeleton destroys information the user was looking at, resets their scroll and reading position, and shifts layout twice for zero benefit. The rule, in stale-while-revalidate terms: existing content is never replaced while new content loads; loading is only ever indicated , alongside the stale content. The stale-while-revalidate presentation rule. The fallback answers \"what goes here?\", asked once. Refetch, revalidation, filter change, pagination: stale + indicator, always: loading is a status you indicate, not a state you swap to. The tools, by situation: URL-driven server UI (the default case) : transitions . The App Router performs navigations inside startTransition , so when a filter changes the URL, the already-revealed table stays on screen while the server re-renders with the new searchParams ; the Suspense boundary does not revert to its fallback. Wrap the router.replace() in your own useTransition and isPending is your indicator. Assembled end to end in the first example below . Client-fetched data : SWR / React Query already behave this way: data keeps its previous value while isValidating / isFetching is true (use keepPreviousData when the key changes). Bind your indicator to isValidating ; never gate the content on it. Client-rendered results of a fast-changing value : useDeferredValue . When the value drives client-side rendering or client-fetched queries (an autocomplete, search-as-you-type against the client cache), render against the deferred value; value !== deferredValue drives the dimmed opacity. Same presentation rule, client-side mechanism. Shown in the second example below ."},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"example-url-transition","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"Example: URL-driven server UI, kept stale through a transition","t":"The first bullet, the default case, assembled end to end. Notice it's the whole guide in one example: the URL is the state ( §8 ), the input and the dim-wrapper are client leaves ( §5 ), the rows are a server component fetching by searchParams , and the fallback appears exactly once: app/reports/page.tsx - server: the URL is the input export default async function Page ({ searchParams }) { const { q = \"\" } = await searchParams; return ( // One shared, thin client provider gives the input and the table a single isPending. < QueryTransitionProvider > {/* This boundary is required because SearchInput reads the URL with useSearchParams (§5.6). It is necessary so that: (1) the static shell can prerender and stream without knowing the request's search params; (2) the client-side-rendering bailout stays scoped to this leaf instead of climbing to the nearest ancestor boundary and taking the whole page with it; and (3) the build passes under cacheComponents/PPR, which requires every request-time read to sit inside a Suspense boundary. */} < Suspense fallback ={< SearchInputSkeleton />}> < SearchInput /> <\/ Suspense > {/* The known container reserves the table's space (§9). */} < TableContainer > {/* A client shell that dims; the server-rendered rows pass through as children. */} < StaleContent > {/* The fallback appears exactly once, on first load. */} < Suspense fallback ={< TableSkeleton />}> {/* A server component that fetches based on the query in the URL. */} < ReportRows query ={q} /> <\/ Suspense > <\/ StaleContent > <\/ TableContainer > <\/ QueryTransitionProvider > ); } ReportRows.tsx - server: only ever renders finished knowledge export async function ReportRows ({ query }) { // The data layer call is cached and deduped (§14). const rows = await getReportRows (query); /* The await above has finished by the time this renders, so \"empty\" here can only mean finished-and-empty; the unknown case cannot reach this component (§10.6). */ if (rows.length === 0 ) return < EmptyState query ={query} />; return < Rows rows ={rows} />; } QueryTransition.tsx - client: one transition, shared by the leaves \"use client\" ; const Ctx = createContext ({ isPending: false , update: (fn: () => void ) => {} }); export const useQueryTransition = () => use (Ctx); export function QueryTransitionProvider ({ children }) { const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition (); return <Ctx va"},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"example-deferred","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"Example: client-rendered results of a fast-changing value","t":"The third bullet. Here nothing touches the server or the URL: the data is already on the client, and the fast-changing value is each keystroke. useDeferredValue gives the same stale-content behavior with a purely client mechanism: the input re-renders instantly with the fresh value, while the expensive results below are allowed to lag one render behind and dim until they catch up: ProgramFilter.tsx - client: search-as-you-type over client data \"use client\" ; export function ProgramFilter ({ programs }) { const [query, setQuery] = useState ( \"\" ); /* deferredQuery lags behind query while React re-renders the heavy list. The input stays instantly responsive; the results are allowed to be one keystroke stale. */ const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue (query); const isStale = query !== deferredQuery; const matches = useMemo ( () => programs. filter ((p) => p.name. toLowerCase (). includes (deferredQuery. toLowerCase ())), [programs, deferredQuery] ); return ( <> < Input value ={query} onValueChange ={setQuery} /> {/* Same presentation rule as the server case: stale results stay visible and dim; they are never swapped for a spinner. */} <div style ={{ opacity: isStale ? 0.6 : 1 }}> < ProgramList programs ={matches} /> <\/div> <\/> ); } Two mechanisms, one rule. The transition example indicates with isPending ; this one indicates with query !== deferredQuery . In both, the existing results never leave the screen, and nothing below the input ever collapses into a spinner. (The middle bullet, client-fetched data, needs no example of its own: SWR and React Query give you this behavior out of the box; just bind your indicator to isValidating and never gate content on it.)"},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"orthogonal","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"10.6 Loading, data, and empty are three separate questions","t":"The deepest of the loading-state mistakes is structural: the if (isLoading) … else if (!data) … else … chain. It welds three independent questions into one sequence, and every branch is wrong in some state: \"Is a request in flight?\" A status , true on first load and on every refetch. It decorates the UI (indicator, dimming); it must never decide which UI renders; that's how refetches turn into skeletons. \"Do we have data?\" A question about knowledge : undefined means we don't know yet (no request has finished). It's answered by the presence of a value, not by the loading flag. \"Is the data empty?\" Only askable after we have knowledge: the request finished and returned nothing. Empty state visibility has exactly one condition: finished, and empty. It has nothing to do with loading. This is also why the data layer must never default missing data to an empty array. React Critical data ?? [] feels like defensive convenience, but it erases the distinction between \"unknown\" and \"known-empty\" ; downstream code can no longer tell whether to show a skeleton or an empty state, and the classic bug appears: an \"No results\" flash on every first load, before the data arrives. undefined is information. Preserve it end to end, and let the leaf that renders decide. The empty-state flash, frame by frame. Same request, same 12 rows; the only difference is whether the data layer erased undefined . Defaulting to [] makes the component answer \"is it empty?\" before \"do we know?\" has resolved, so every first load flashes a false \"No results\". ✕ One chain, three welded questions the classic: every branch misbehaves const { data = [], isLoading } = useReportRows (q); /* On every refetch, real content is replaced by a skeleton. */ if (isLoading) return < TableSkeleton />; /* This flashes an empty state before the first response arrives, because [] can no longer say \"not loaded yet\". */ if (data.length === 0 ) return < EmptyState />; return < Rows rows ={data} />; Defaulting data to [] makes unknown and known-empty identical. ✓ Three independent decisions status decorates; knowledge decides const { data, isValidating } = useReportRows (q); const hasData = data !== undefined ; /* Loading is indicated independently of what renders inside; the container shows a subtle indicator whenever a refetch is in flight. */ return ( < TableContainer isRefreshing ={isValidating && hasData}> {!hasData"},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"one-skeleton","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"One container, one skeleton, ever","t":"The table reads row by row; watched as one container over its lifetime, it collapses to a single rule: the skeleton appears exactly once, on first load, while the answer is unknown. The moment the first response lands, the container has something honest to show forever after: rows if there are rows, a real empty state if there are none. Every fetch from then on (revalidation, filter change, a mutation's refresh) decorates that content instead of replacing it: same UI, dimmed or badged while the request is in flight. And the treatment does not depend on what the content is. A known-empty list being refreshed keeps its empty state and gains the indicator, exactly like a populated list keeps its rows; the skeleton does not get to come back just because the container happens to be empty. One container's whole life. The skeleton exists only in the first frame, while the answer is unknown; after that the container always shows its truth (rows or a real empty state) and every subsequent fetch decorates it with a non-destructive indicator, identically for empty and populated content."},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"transitions","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"10.7 Transitions","t":"Every state update in React is urgent by default: React drops what it is doing and re-renders to reflect it as fast as it can. React Very important useTransition (and its standalone form startTransition ) marks an update as non-urgent : React prepares the resulting UI in the background while the current one stays visible and interactive, and if a genuinely urgent update (a keystroke) lands mid-render, React abandons the background work, handles the keystroke, and restarts. One flag, and three behaviors fall out of it, each a distinct tool."},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"transitions-stale","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"Transitions keep the old UI alive","t":"The callout in §10.1 noted that a boundary already showing content will not fall back to its skeleton when an update inside it suspends during a transition. That behavior is how you get updates that don't flash, and it is the same doctrine as §10.5 , one level up: don't blank what you already have just because something newer is on the way. This is the reason writes to router-owned state, a navigation or a searchParams update ( §8.2 ), belong in a transition: the current page stays live while the next view prepares, and the isPending flag from useTransition shows progress alongside the old content instead of replacing it. SortSelect.tsx - client leaf: a router write that keeps the table alive \"use client\" ; export function SortSelect ({ current }: { current: string }) { const router = useRouter (); const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition (); return ( < Select value ={current} disabled ={isPending} onChange ={(sort) => startTransition (() => { /* A router write: the server re-renders the page. Inside a transition, the table's boundary keeps its revealed rows instead of falling back. */ router. push ( `/programs?sort=${sort}` ); }) } /> ); } While the server prepares the new rows, isPending is true: dim the table, disable the select, badge the header; the old UI never blanks. The end-to-end version of this pattern is the §10.5 example ."},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"transitions-defer","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"Transitions defer expensive work","t":"The second use has nothing to do with data: a state update whose re-render is expensive. Re-filtering a large list, re-laying-out a chart, switching a heavy tab: wrap the update in startTransition and the typing, clicking, and scrolling stay responsive while the heavy render runs at low priority, restarted rather than blocking whenever the user does something urgent. Two caveats from the React docs are worth knowing. A transition cannot drive a controlled text input: the input's own value is urgent by definition (the user must see their keystroke), so the transition carries the expensive derived state and the input updates normally. And when the value whose update you would defer is not yours to wrap (it arrives through props or a store), useDeferredValue is the same mechanism applied to a value instead of an update: the type-to-filter example in §10.5 . ProgramSearch.tsx - client: urgent keystroke, non-urgent filter \"use client\" ; export function ProgramSearch ({ all }: { all: Program [] }) { const [query, setQuery] = useState ( \"\" ); const [results, setResults] = useState (all); const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition (); function onInput (next: string) { // Urgent: the keystroke must paint immediately. setQuery (next); startTransition (() => { // Non-urgent: rendered in the background, restarted // if another keystroke lands mid-render. setResults ( filterPrograms (all, next)); }); } return ( <> < Input value ={query} onChange ={onInput} /> < ResultsList items ={results} dimmed ={isPending} /> <\/> ); } Two updates from one keystroke. The input's own value is the urgent one and paints at once; the expensive re-filter of results runs at low priority and never blocks typing."},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"transitions-async","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"Async transitions: actions and pending state","t":"React 19 extended transitions to async functions, which the docs call actions : startTransition(async () => { … }) . isPending flips to true at the first call and stays true until every update the action scheduled has landed, which is exactly the lifecycle a submit button needs; this is the machinery behind firing a server action from a leaf ( §5.7 ), where isPending drives the button and the result object carries the failure ( §5.8 ). Errors thrown inside an action surface to the nearest error boundary, and optimistic UI ( useOptimistic , §8.2 ) reverts automatically when the action fails. One sharp edge from the docs: after an await , further state updates inside the action must be wrapped in startTransition again, or they lose their transition marking. ArchiveButton.tsx - client leaf: an async action \"use client\" ; export function ArchiveButton ({ programId }: { programId: number }) { const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition (); return ( < Button disabled ={isPending} onClick ={() => startTransition ( async () => { /* The action: awaited inside the transition, so isPending covers the server round trip AND the re-render its revalidation triggers. */ const { error } = await archiveProgram (programId); if (error) toast. error (error); }) } > {isPending ? \"Archiving…\" : \"Archive\" } <\/ Button > ); } The same machinery as the publish button in §5.7 : isPending flips at the first line and holds until every update the action scheduled has landed, so the button cannot double-fire and the label is honest. The API reference for useTransition and useDeferredValue is in §19.3 ; the caveat list lives in the React docs ."},{"f":"10-suspense-streaming.html","a":"rules","n":10,"c":"Suspense & Streaming","h":"The rules","t":"Rule Scope Importance Components never define their own loading states ; boundaries above them do. React Critical Fallbacks live inside known containers ; neither fallback nor suspended component includes the container. React Critical Skeletons are scoped per component, colocated with it , never page-level. React Very important Refetches never replace content: stale stays visible, loading is indicated ( isValidating , useDeferredValue , isPending ). React Critical Loading status, data presence, and emptiness are decided independently : no if/else chains across them. React Critical The data layer never defaults undefined to [] ; unknown and known-empty are different facts . React Critical Use granular Suspense boundaries per data region ; loading.tsx renders scaffold, not a page skeleton. Next.js Very important Where to go deeper React 19 docs: <Suspense> · useDeferredValue · useTransition · use . Next.js docs: loading.js · Fetching Data & Streaming · cacheComponents / PPR . Full list in §20 ."},{"f":"11-designing-for-the-unknown.html","a":"","n":11,"c":"Designing for the Unknown","h":"Designing for the Unknown","t":"Everything in §9 and §10 assumed the layout can be contained. That is not an engineering given; it is decided earlier, in design. Some layouts are containable and some fight containment no matter how carefully we implement them, which makes stability a cross-team goal: engineering, UX, and design deciding together what the page does while the data is still unknown."},{"f":"11-designing-for-the-unknown.html","a":"shared-goal","n":11,"c":"Designing for the Unknown","h":"11.1 Stability is a cross-team goal","t":"Cumulative Layout Shift is the engineering measurement of a goal that is fundamentally shared: the page must not move under the user. Nothing they are reading jumps, nothing they are about to click dodges, nothing \"settles\" in three visible steps. The metric lives in our world, but its causes mostly do not: they live in composition decisions (what shares an axis with what, what gets how much space, what the page shows before the data) that are made in design, before any code exists. A goal whose measurement belongs to one team and whose causes belong to another cannot be owned by either alone. It has to be a standing conversation. Today that conversation mostly doesn't happen, and the reason it must is timing. Very important By the time engineering discovers a layout is unstable, the design is approved, the component is half built, and every fix is a compromise: a hardcoded height that is wrong half the time, a spinner where a skeleton should be, a container that scrolls awkwardly because the space it was given never matched its content. The cheap moment to catch instability is while the design is still a design : identifying the places where a composition introduces content that is hard to contain, early enough that the UX can be shaped toward predictability instead of patched toward it. Done consistently, that pressure compounds: the design system itself drifts toward layouts that are stable by default, for the screens we are building now and the ones we haven't designed yet. What the channel is for Not a review gate, a two-way channel. Engineering brings what only engineering can see: which regions arrive late, in what order, and what the page does in the states the mock doesn't show. UX brings what only UX can see: which compositions are worth the containment cost and where stability should bend to hierarchy. The failure mode this chapter exists to prevent is both sides discovering the conflict after the layout is built."},{"f":"11-designing-for-the-unknown.html","a":"happy-path","n":11,"c":"Designing for the Unknown","h":"11.2 The comp is a happy path","t":"A Figma comp is a snapshot of exactly one state: the happy path . The data has arrived, every region is populated, and the row counts were chosen so the composition looks balanced inside the frame: the table shows the six rows that fit, the cards have names of pleasant length, and nothing is empty, loading, or overflowing. None of this is laziness; it is what comps are for : selling a composition. But a static tool produces static answers, and the states that break layouts are precisely the ones a static frame never has to confront: the second before the data arrives, the day the table has one row, the customer whose program list needs three screens of scrolling. The subtler version of the problem survives even in thorough comps. Often the empty state is mocked, but it's drawn at the same size the container had when it held the artboard's eight rows, as if \"empty\" were just different content in the same box. The box only had that size because eight rows fit it; the height was an artifact of the sample data, not a decision. What the comp never shows is the page flipping between those states: empty today, 1 row after the first save, 300 rows next quarter, with everything below the container living or dying by which state it's in. We will never know the count ahead of time; that is permanent. So the real design question is not \"what does empty look like?\" but \"how do all of these states swap through the same space without pushing everything else around?\" So the questions engineering must put on the table, for every data-driven region in every mock, are these: \"What does this region show before we know what's in it?\" Not \"a spinner\": what shape, what height, what happens to its neighbors while it waits ( §10.2 ). \"What happens when the content is much smaller than this?\" One row. Zero rows: the empty state is a designed state, not a gap ( §10.6 ). \"What happens when the content is much larger than this?\" Does the region grow (pushing what's below it), scroll, paginate, or truncate? Someone must pick one, and the pick changes the layout. \"Which of these regions arrive at different times?\" Two regions that stream independently ( §10 ) will resolve in either order; the design has to look intentional in both. These questions are the channel from §11.1 in practice, and they flow both ways. The more consistently they get asked, the more natural they become on both sid"},{"f":"11-designing-for-the-unknown.html","a":"stacked-tables","n":11,"c":"Designing for the Unknown","h":"11.3 The worked example: two stacked tables","t":"Here is a common hard case, and it looks completely innocent in a comp: two tables stacked vertically inside the app chrome, say Assignments above Submissions on a program page. In the mock, each shows a handful of rows and together they fill the content area exactly. Now walk the states. On first paint the row counts are unknown (and even with SSR, the server knows them but the designer still didn't when the heights were drawn). So how much vertical space does each table get? Every naive answer fails a different way. Guess 1, split the height 50/50: the guess is wrong almost every time, because real data is skewed: the top table has 1 assignment and sits in a sea of dead space while the bottom one crams 50 submissions into the same half and scrolls furiously. Guess 2, size each table to its content with a min-height: now the pre-data state reserves the min-height, but the moment the top table's 30 rows arrive it grows and shoves the bottom table down the page , possibly below the fold: the exact reading-interrupting shift we exist to prevent, and it happens on every load, in front of the user. Guess 3, just let both tables be auto-height: no constraints at all, so the table bodies are never contained and never scroll; the content area around them (usually the whole page) becomes the scroll container instead. The shift is the same as Guess 2: whatever the top table turns out to be pushes everything below it, except now the page's total length is also data-dependent, the scrollbar jumps as each table resolves, and the app chrome is the only thing standing still. Auto-height is not a neutral default; it is a decision to let the data lay out the page. The 50/50 guess is stable (nothing moves when data lands) but wrong: real row counts are skewed, so one table drowns in dead space while the other scrolls inside a cramped half. Stability achieved, usability lost. The min-height guess inverts the failure: the pre-data state is compact and tidy, and then the real content arrives and rewrites the page. The top table grows tenfold and the bottom table is thrown below the fold: a layout shift the user watches happen on every single visit."},{"f":"11-designing-for-the-unknown.html","a":"stacked-tables-resolution","n":11,"c":"Designing for the Unknown","h":"The resolution is a design decision","t":"Notice what kind of problem this is. There is no clever CSS that makes \"two stacked, unbounded tables\" stable, because the instability is in the concept : two regions of unknowable size competing for one axis. The fixes all involve changing the design, which is exactly why design has to be in the room: Partition the viewport deliberately. Give each table a fixed share of the content area (not necessarily 50/50: weighted by expected volume) and let each scroll internally . The frame never moves; only content inside each pane changes. This is the strongest containment, and it is the running worked example of this guide ( §16 ). Cap the secondary table. Decide one table is primary and give the other a fixed budget: \"5 most recent submissions\" plus a \"View all\" link to its own page. Fixed row count, fixed height, fully containable; and often better UX than the original. Don't stack them. Tabs, a master-detail split, or side-by-side panes each remove the shared vertical axis entirely. If the user rarely needs both tables at once, the stack was the wrong composition to begin with. The stable version of the same page. The panes' geometry is identical before and after the data: the sparse table shows its one row inside its reserved pane, the busy table scrolls internally, and nothing outside either pane ever learns what arrived. All three resolutions share the property that matters: the space each region occupies is decided by design, before the data, instead of by whichever response happens to arrive. That is what \"contain the unknown\" means at the design layer. And be clear about what the partitioned version is: a compromise, not the ideal . It makes the stacked composition survivable (nothing moves), but the user still pays for it: a pane that is mostly dead space when its table is sparse, an internal scrollbar working hard when it is busy, two regions forever negotiating one axis. The genuinely better outcome is the one further upstream, where the composition never puts two unbounded regions on top of each other in the first place: tabs, a capped secondary list, a master-detail split. Engineering cannot get there alone, because \"don't stack these\" is a design decision; this is §11.1's channel paying for itself. When the conversation happens at mock time, the answer is a better composition; when it happens at build time, the answer is a partition."},{"f":"11-designing-for-the-unknown.html","a":"hard-patterns","n":11,"c":"Designing for the Unknown","h":"11.4 Other patterns that fight containment","t":"Stacked unbounded regions are the most common offender, but the same analysis (walk the states, find who moves) catches a family of patterns worth recognizing on sight, in review, before they are built: Pattern Why it shifts Stable version Stacked unbounded lists/tables Regions of unknown size compete for one axis; whoever loads first wins. Partitioned panes with internal scroll; or cap one region. Wizard dialogs sized per step Each frame has drastically different content, so the dialog resizes (and re-centers!) on every step: the world moves between every click. One frame sized for the largest step (smaller steps center inside it), or a top-anchored dialog that only ever grows downward. Banners/alerts injected above content Appears after first paint and pushes the entire page down exactly when the user starts reading. Reserved banner slot in the chrome, or overlay/toast that floats. Images and embeds without dimensions Height is unknown until the asset loads; every image is a small earthquake. Aspect-ratio boxes reserved in CSS ( next/image requires dimensions for exactly this reason). Accordions/rows that expand inline Expanding one row relocates every row after it; collapse and the page snaps back. Fine when user-initiated (expected motion); unstable when expanded state is data-driven on load. Never auto-expand after paint. Type-dependent detail panels A panel that renders wildly different content by record type changes size when the record loads. One panel frame with a designed layout per type, all sharing the frame. The wizard case deserves the spelled-out version because it hides so well in a comp. Each step is mocked as its own artboard, each artboard is individually beautiful, and no artboard shows the transition : a short confirmation step does not need the space the split-pane review step needs, so the dialog snaps from 720px wide to 400px and back, re-centering each time, and the user's eyes chase the buttons around the screen. The stable versions cost one design decision: size the frame once for the largest step and let smaller steps breathe inside it, or anchor the top edge so growth only ever extends downward. Both are decisions about the set of steps, which is precisely what a per-artboard mock never forces anyone to make. The same three-step flow, mocked per artboard versus designed as a set. In the top row every step is individually fine and"},{"f":"11-designing-for-the-unknown.html","a":"seed-data","n":11,"c":"Designing for the Unknown","h":"11.5 Seed data is part of the discipline","t":"There is a development-side habit that keeps all of the above honest: test with data volumes that vary wildly. Very important A dev database seeded with three tidy rows per table is the engineering version of the happy-path comp: every layout looks contained because nothing ever has enough content to fight. The failure modes in this chapter only become visible when the seed data forces them: one table with 1 row above a table with 50; a program title that wraps to three lines; a list that is legitimately empty; a page where one region resolves instantly and its sibling takes two seconds. So seed generously and unevenly, on purpose: some entities with 0 children, some with 1, some with 5, some with 50 or 500. Make the skew part of the fixture set rather than something you construct by hand once and lose. And when a layout survives the skewed seed and a throttled network, it has actually earned the word \"stable\": the states design signed off on and the states the seed data forces are, together, the whole state space. The checklist, for design reviews For every data-driven region in the mock: (1) What renders before the data? (2) What does 0 / 1 / 50 items look like? (3) When it's bigger than its space: grow, scroll, paginate, or truncate? (4) Which regions arrive at different times, and does the page look intentional in both orders? (5) Does anything already painted ever move? If a question has no answer, the mock isn't done; if the answer is \"it moves,\" the design isn't stable."},{"f":"11-designing-for-the-unknown.html","a":"rules","n":11,"c":"Designing for the Unknown","h":"The rules","t":"Rule Scope Importance Keep the engineering/UX channel open, in both directions: containment concerns surface while a design is still a design, not after the layout is built. UI/UX Critical Every data-driven region in a mock needs its pre-data, empty, sparse, and overflowing states answered before it is approved. UI/UX Critical Regions of unknown size must not compete for one axis. Partition the space, cap one region, or change the composition. UI/UX Critical Overlays and dialogs get one frame per flow , sized for the largest step; steps change inside the frame. UI/UX Very important Seed data skewed on purpose (0 / 1 / 5 / 50 / 500) so unstable layouts fail in development, not in front of users. UI/UX Very important Where to go deeper web.dev: Cumulative Layout Shift - the metric itself, with the field data on how shifts are scored; useful for the engineering half of the conversation. web.dev: Optimize CLS - the standard remediation catalog (dimensions on media, reserved space for late content). Next.js docs: <Image> - why the component demands width/height: reserved space as an API requirement. React docs: <Suspense> - fallbacks are UI you design, not UI you get."},{"f":"12-blocking-requests.html","a":"","n":12,"c":"Blocking Requests","h":"Blocking Requests","t":"Every await is a gate, and everything rendered behind it waits. The goal: make the region behind any single blocking request as small as possible, and make independent requests run at the same time, on the server and on the client alike."},{"f":"12-blocking-requests.html","a":"scope","n":12,"c":"Blocking Requests","h":"12.1 What sits behind a request","t":"When a component awaits, everything it renders waits with it. That's the blocking scope of the request, and it's determined entirely by where in the tree the await happens . An await at the top of the page puts the whole page behind one gate: header, navigation, static text, all of it held hostage by the slowest fetch. The same await, pushed down into the component that actually uses the data, holds only that region: the rest of the page renders and streams immediately ( §10.2 ). So the rule is positional: await where the data is used, not where it's convenient to write. React Critical This is the same force behind §13 's independence argument: self-fetching children don't just decouple the code, they shrink each request's blast radius to the component that needed it. The same page, same two fetches. Blocking scope is positional: an await at the page level gates everything behind the slowest request; awaits pushed into the regions that use the data gate only themselves."},{"f":"12-blocking-requests.html","a":"parallel","n":12,"c":"Blocking Requests","h":"12.2 Parallel by default","t":"The second half of the discipline: independent requests must not wait for each other . Sequential awaits in one component are the classic server-side waterfall - each fetch starts only after the previous one finishes, and the region's wait is the sum of its requests instead of the max : React Critical ✕ Serialized: wait is the sum page.tsx export default async function Page ({ params }) { const { id } = await params; /* Each await gates the next. Three independent questions, asked one at a time: 300 + 400 + 500 ms. */ const program = await getProgram (id); const stats = await getStats (id); const rows = await getRows (id); // …render everything from here } ✓ Parallel: wait is the max page.tsx: siblings parallelize naturally export default async function Page ({ params }) { const { id } = await params; /* Three self-fetching server components (§13). The server renders siblings concurrently: all fetches start at t₀. */ return ( <> < ProgramHeader programId ={id} /> < ProgramStats programId ={id} /> < ReportRows programId ={id} /> <\/> ); } when one component needs several answers const [program, stats] = await Promise.all ([ getProgram (id), getStats (id), ]); Inside a single component: start all the requests first, then await them together. Two mechanics worth knowing precisely. Sibling async server components render concurrently - composing self-fetching components ( §13 ) gives you parallelism for free, which is another reason independence beats drilling. React Critical And a call into the deduped data layer starts the request whether or not you await it - so when a fetch is genuinely slow and needed below, you can kick it off early ( const p = getRows(id) with no await) and let the component that needs it await the same deduped promise later. Start early, await late, and only where needed. Genuinely dependent requests - B needs A's answer - are the one honest waterfall. The discipline there: confine the chain to the smallest region that needs its result. React Very important ProgramManagerCard.tsx - the chain waits here, not at the page export async function ProgramManagerCard ({ programId }) { /* getUser genuinely depends on the program's answer. Because the chain lives in this leaf, only this card pays for two round trips - the page, the stats, and the table never knew. Both calls are deduped (§14). */ const program = await getProgram (programId); const m"},{"f":"12-blocking-requests.html","a":"parallel-routes","n":12,"c":"Blocking Requests","h":"12.3 Parallel routes: independence with a file address","t":"§9.4 put the containers in the layout; §13 made the regions self-fetching; §10 gave each one a boundary. Parallel routes are the framework formalizing that whole pattern as filesystem structure: a layout declares named slots ( @header , @summary , @table ), each slot is its own mini-page, and the framework fills them independently - each with its own loading.tsx , its own error.tsx , its own streaming lifecycle. Next.js Very important Slots are filesystem convention becoming layout props (redrawn from the Next.js parallel-routes docs). Each @folder is a mini-page; the layout receives it as a prop and places it - H, S, and C mark the same slot in the tree, the rendered page, and the code. app/programs/[id]/layout.tsx - slots arrive as props export default function ProgramLayout ({ children, header, summary }) { return ( <div className = \"flex h-full flex-col\" > {header} <div className = \"grid grid-cols-[1fr_320px] gap-6 min-h-0 grow\" > <main className = \"overflow-y-auto\" >{children}<\/main> <aside>{summary}<\/aside> <\/div> <\/div> ); } When to reach for a slot instead of a plain component: when the region deserves route-level independence - its own loading.tsx / error.tsx files, participation in navigation, or different content per sub-route. Nick's canonical example: a page header. If the header is static , it's just layout markup - no slot needed. If the header depends on server data (program name, status badge, owner), a @header slot gives it an error and loading lifecycle that can't take the table down with it, and vice versa. One convention matters in practice: default.tsx is what a slot renders when the current URL doesn't match anything inside it (typically: re-render nothing-specific, or return null ) - omit it and navigation into unmatched states 404s the whole route. Define it for every slot. Next.js Critical Route-level independence in practice. Left: the summary slot streams behind its own loading.tsx while the header and children shipped at first paint. Right: the same slot throwing renders its own error.tsx - the rest of the page never finds out. One more capability rounds out the picture: conditional slots . Because slots arrive as props, the layout can choose which one to render - which means one URL can serve fully separated experiences. The canonical case is role-based UI: @admin and @user are two complete page trees with their own data, loadin"},{"f":"12-blocking-requests.html","a":"client","n":12,"c":"Blocking Requests","h":"12.4 The client has waterfalls too","t":"Everything above has a client-side mirror, and it's the expensive one - each client-side gate is a full network round trip plus a render cycle. The patterns to hunt: dependent query chains ( useQuery / useSWR hooks gated on each other's results - profile → organization id → dashboard data, each hop serialized after hydration), and fetch-after-mount cascades , where a component fetches, renders children, and the children then begin their fetches. Our audit found 53 enabled: -gated dependent queries in one app - 53 serialized round trips that a server component tree would have resolved in one pass. React Critical The fixes, in order: move the chain to the server , where the dependency is an in-datacenter hop inside one request ( §12.2 's dependent-chain pattern) - this is the structural fix and it's usually available; collapse the chain's key , so the client asks one question (\"dashboard for current user\") and the server resolves the intermediate steps; and where client fetching is legitimate ( §14.3 ), make sibling queries independent - two hooks with no enabled: coupling fire in parallel. The same three reads, four ways. Chained on the client, each one waits for the last, so latency stacks. Moved to the server, the dependency chain becomes in-datacenter hops inside a single round trip. Independent reads on the server go one better: each region's component fetches in parallel and streams into its own Suspense boundary the moment it resolves ( §10 ). And where client fetching is legitimate, decoupling the queries lets them start together, so the user waits for the slowest one, not the sum."},{"f":"12-blocking-requests.html","a":"rules","n":12,"c":"Blocking Requests","h":"The rules","t":"Rule Scope Importance Await where the data is used - the blocking scope of a request is exactly the subtree behind the await. React Critical Independent requests run in parallel - sibling self-fetching components, or Promise.all within one component. Never serialized awaits for unrelated data. React Critical Dependent chains are confined to the smallest region that needs their result. React Very important Regions deserving route-level independence become parallel-route slots - own loading, own error, own stream. Next.js Very important Every slot defines default.tsx - unmatched navigation must not 404 the route. Next.js Critical No client-side dependent query chains - resolve dependencies on the server , or collapse them into one key. React Critical Where to go deeper React 19 docs: Server Components (async components and composition) · <Suspense> . Next.js docs: Fetching Data (sequential vs parallel, preloading) · Parallel Routes . Full list in §20 ."},{"f":"13-minimize-the-wires.html","a":"","n":13,"c":"Minimize the Wires","h":"Minimize the Wires","t":"Every prop, callback, and context read is a wire: a dependency connecting two parts of the UI. Wires are not free: each one couples a component's future to its neighbors'. This chapter is about keeping components independent, and why repetition beats plumbing."},{"f":"13-minimize-the-wires.html","a":"cost","n":13,"c":"Minimize the Wires","h":"13.1 The cost of a wire","t":"When a parent fetches data and passes it down, the parent stops being a layout concern and becomes a data router . It must know what every descendant needs, fetch the union of it, and thread it through. And from that moment, three components change together forever. Child B needs one more field? You edit Child B, the parent's fetch, and the prop signature in between. Multiply by every feature for the life of the product and the parent becomes what we'll call a coupling layer : a component whose main job is re-wiring everyone else's requirements. The rule that falls out of this is blunt: never pass a child anything the parent does not itself need. React Critical If the parent isn't using the data (isn't branching on it, isn't laying it out), the parent is just a wire, and wires should be deleted. Legitimate exceptions: identity (an id the child will use to ask its own questions), composition ( children and other slots), and configuration the parent genuinely owns (a variant, a size). The same three components, wired two ways. Left: the parent fetches once and drills: every requirement change touches three files. Right: children ask the data layer themselves; the dedupe layer ( §14 ) collapses their identical questions into one real request."},{"f":"13-minimize-the-wires.html","a":"repetition","n":13,"c":"Minimize the Wires","h":"13.2 Repetition over dependency","t":"The instinct that produces drilling is a good one: don't fetch the same thing twice . For most of React's history, the only way to honor it was to fetch once at the top and share downward. Hooks and context were the first crack in that constraint; in Next, deduped fetches and cached functions remove it entirely. It is better for two components to ask the same question independently than to be wired together , because the framework guarantees the question is only answered once per request. Next.js Very important ✕ Parent as data router app/programs/[id]/page.tsx export default async function Page ({ params }) { const { id } = await params; // This fetches the union of every child's needs. const program = await getProgram (id); return ( <> < ProgramHeader name ={program.name} status ={program.status} sponsor ={program.sponsor} /> < ProgramStats enrolled ={program.enrolledCount} completed ={program.completedCount} /> <\/> ); } The Header needs one more field? Edit Header, Page, and getProgram : three files, every time. ✓ Independent children, deduped data layer app/programs/[id]/page.tsx export default async function Page ({ params }) { const { id } = await params; // The only wire is identity. return ( <> < ProgramHeader programId ={id} /> < ProgramStats programId ={id} /> <\/> ); } components/program/ProgramHeader.tsx export async function ProgramHeader ({ programId }) { /* It asks for itself; the dedupe layer makes this free (§14). */ const program = await getProgram (programId); return <header>{program.name} …<\/header>; } Both ProgramHeader and ProgramStats call getProgram(id) ; only one real request happens, because the data layer is deduped ( §14 covers the mechanics: cache() , fetch dedupe, SWR keys). The page went from a data router back to what it should be: structure. And each child is now portable : drop it into any page, any parallel route, any dialog, hand it an id, and it works. This works on both sides of the boundary Server-side, independence is free via React.cache() and fetch dedupe. Client-side, the same pattern holds with useSWR /React Query: two components calling useProgram(id) share one cache entry and one request. What you should not do is hand-roll it: a fetch in an effect has no dedupe layer, so drilling becomes \"necessary\" again. The anti-patterns feed each other."},{"f":"13-minimize-the-wires.html","a":"streaming","n":13,"c":"Minimize the Wires","h":"13.3 Independence changes how the page streams","t":"Wiring isn't just a maintenance cost; it determines where your Suspense boundaries can go . A boundary must sit above whatever awaits. If the parent fetches and drills, the boundary is forced above the parent: the whole subtree is one all-or-nothing unit, gated by its slowest request. If children fetch for themselves, each child gets its own boundary inside the parent: the structure renders instantly and each region streams in as its answer arrives. Boundary placement follows the data. Drilled props force one big boundary above the parent; self-fetching children let each region suspend (and stream) on its own. Full treatment of boundary placement in §10 ."},{"f":"13-minimize-the-wires.html","a":"callbacks","n":13,"c":"Minimize the Wires","h":"13.4 Callbacks up are wires too","t":"Passing data down is half the coupling story; the other half is children reporting back up. A callback prop that exists so a child can tell its parent \"something changed, please refetch/re-render\" is a wire pointing the other direction, and it usually means the coordination is happening at the wrong layer. Communicate through shared infrastructure instead of through parents : React Critical The URL : filters, tabs, selection, pagination. A filter bar that calls router.replace() with new search params doesn't need to know a table exists; the table re-renders because the page's inputs changed ( §8 ). Cache invalidation : after a mutation, revalidatePath / revalidateTag on the server, or SWR/React Query invalidation on the client. Every component reading that data updates, with no parent orchestrating ( §14 ; the full mutation cycle is drawn in §5.8 ). Concretely: the callback you will be tempted to add most often is onSuccess : a mutation button reporting up so someone can refetch. Here is the same button, wired and wireless: ✕ Reports up through a wire PublishButton.tsx \"use client\" ; function PublishButton ({ programId, onSuccess, }: Props ) { return ( < Button onClick ={ async () => { await publishProgram (programId); onSuccess (); }} > Publish <\/ Button > ); } The button must be handed a wire: someone above must exist, must care, and must never change shape. ✓ Invalidates the data's name PublishButton.tsx \"use client\" ; function PublishButton ({ programId }: Props ) { return ( < Button onClick ={() => startTransition ( async () => { /* The action revalidates its tag (§5.8); or, inside an SWR island, mutate the key: */ await publishProgram (programId); await mutate ( [ \"program\" , programId] ); }) } > Publish <\/ Button > ); } No wire: the mutation invalidates the name the data lives under, and every reader refires on its own. The name (a cache tag, a search param, an SWR key) is the entire contract between the leaf and its surroundings. This is deliberately the same PublishButton as §5.7 – §5.8 : that chapter argues the pattern as boundary placement (the leaf is the only client component, so the tree stays server), this one argues it as wiring (the leaf holds zero references to its surroundings, so the tree stays decoupled). Same code, two independent reasons it's right, and the two failures arrive together, too. Which is worth saying explicitly: the God co"},{"f":"13-minimize-the-wires.html","a":"portability","n":13,"c":"Minimize the Wires","h":"13.5 Portability: components you can pick up and move","t":"Everything above argued wires are expensive to maintain . The payoff for cutting them has a name: portability . A component whose data arrives through props can never leave its page without the wires coming along: whoever hosts it next must fetch the same data, hold the same state, and pass the same callbacks, so \"move the stats card to the dashboard\" becomes a plumbing project. A component that addresses its data by name (a fetch keyed by its own props on the server, an SWR key on the client, a search param it reads itself) moves as one line of JSX . The dedupe layers of §14 are what make this free: wherever the component lands, its question collapses into the cache with everyone else's. Why wires cost more than maintenance. The wired component's requirements live in its host, so every move re-plumbs them; the self-contained component names its data and can be placed on any page, in any layout, and the dedupe layers make its repeated question free. Portability is what makes a codebase malleable : pages stop being wiring diagrams and become compositions. The mechanism that keeps compositions flexible across the server/client boundary is the slot : any JSX-valued prop ( children included). Slots work in both directions , and the two directions have different rules worth keeping straight. A server component can use client components freely : import them, render them, pass them into other components' slots; that direction is always allowed. A client component cannot import a server component , but it can receive one through a slot ( §5.4 ): the server renders the content first, and the client shell places the finished output without ever owning it. app/programs/page.tsx - a server page slots BOTH kinds into a client shell export default function Page () { return ( < ResizableSplit {/* client leaf into a client shell's slot */} left ={< ProgramFilters />} {/* SERVER component into the same client shell: rendered on the server, passed through as output */} right ={< ProgramTable />} /> ); } The page is a server component, so it can compose freely: it imports the client shell and the client leaf, renders the server table, and hands all of them to slots. components/layout/ResizableSplit.tsx - client shell with two slots \"use client\" ; export function ResizableSplit ({ left, right }: ResizableSplitProps ) { const [ratio, setRatio] = useState ( 0.3 ); return ( <div c"},{"f":"13-minimize-the-wires.html","a":"rules","n":13,"c":"Minimize the Wires","h":"The rules","t":"Rule Scope Importance Never pass a child anything the parent doesn't itself need. Identity, slots, and parent-owned config are the exceptions. React Critical Don't pass external data to children in parallel: let each child ask the deduped data layer itself . Next.js Very important Prefer repetition backed by dedupe over shared plumbing. Next.js Very important App-level communication goes through the URL or the cache , not callbacks up the tree. React Critical Where to go deeper React 19 docs: Passing Data Deeply with Context (including its \"before you use context\" checklist) · You Might Not Need an Effect . Next.js docs: Server and Client Components (composition patterns) · revalidateTag . Full list in §20 ."},{"f":"14-dedupe-caching.html","a":"","n":14,"c":"Dedupe & Caching","h":"Dedupe & Caching","t":"§13 argued that components should ask for their own data instead of being wired together, and that the repetition is free. This chapter is the receipts: the layers that collapse many identical questions into one real request, on the server and on the client."},{"f":"14-dedupe-caching.html","a":"layers","n":14,"c":"Dedupe & Caching","h":"14.1 The layers","t":"Think of every data access as a question ( getProgram(42) ) asked by whichever components need it, however many times they like. Between the question and the origin (your API, your database) sit deduplication and caching layers. Each layer has a different lifetime , and the lifetimes are the whole game: Layer Lifetime What it does Importance fetch() dedupe One render pass Identical fetch(url, options) calls during a single server render share one request . Automatic. Critical React.cache() One request Memoizes any server function per request: DB queries, SDK calls, computations. Same args → one execution. Critical \"use cache\" Cross-request Caches a function/component's output across requests and users ; cacheLife sets duration, cacheTag enables targeted invalidation. Opt-in (Next 16, cacheComponents ). Very important SWR / React Query Client session Key-based client cache: components sharing a key share one entry and one request ; stale-while-revalidate keeps it fresh. Critical Both sides of the boundary have a dedupe stack. Server: any number of components call the same cached function during a request: one execution, and with \"use cache\" possibly zero origin hits. Client: components sharing an SWR key share one cache entry and one request."},{"f":"14-dedupe-caching.html","a":"server","n":14,"c":"Dedupe & Caching","h":"14.2 The server data layer","t":"The pattern that makes all of Part C work: every server data access lives in one module, and every function in it is wrapped in cache() . Next.js Critical Components never call fetch or the database directly: they call the data layer, from anywhere in the tree, as many times as composition requires. data/programs.ts import { cache } from \"react\" ; // This import turns any leak into a client bundle into a build error. import \"server-only\" ; /* This is a per-request memo: any component may call it with the same id, and it executes once per request, no matter who asks or how often. */ export const getProgram = cache ( async (id: string ) => { const res = await fetch ( `${API_URL}/programs/${id}` , { headers: await authHeaders () }); if (!res.ok) throw new ProgramError (res); return res.json() as Promise < Program >; }); Two subtleties worth knowing. First, fetch itself dedupes identical calls within a render pass Critical , but cache() is the habit to build, because it also covers database clients, SDKs, and computed values, and it gives the function a single authoritative home. Second, cache() 's lifetime is one request : it is a dedupe mechanism, not a performance cache. User A's request never sees user B's data through it, which is exactly what you want for anything personalized. When data is not personalized and survives across requests (templates, org lists, filter options, reference data), promote it one layer up with \"use cache\" : Next.js Very important data/templates.ts import { cacheLife, cacheTag } from \"next/cache\" ; export async function getFormTemplates () { // The output is cached across requests AND users. \"use cache\" ; // The acceptable staleness is declared, not implied. cacheLife ( \"hours\" ); // The tag is a handle for targeted invalidation. cacheTag ( \"form-templates\" ); return api.templates. list (); } actions/templates.ts \"use server\" ; import { revalidateTag } from \"next/cache\" ; export async function createTemplate (input: TemplateInput ) { await api.templates. create (input); // Every reader updates: no callbacks up the tree, no refetchAll(). revalidateTag ( \"form-templates\" ); } Note what the mutation does not do: it doesn't call a callback up to some parent, doesn't set a consistency token in the URL, doesn't orchestrate a refetchAll() . It invalidates the tag, and every component that reads getFormTemplates() , wherever it sits in the"},{"f":"14-dedupe-caching.html","a":"client","n":14,"c":"Dedupe & Caching","h":"14.3 The client data layer","t":"Some data genuinely must load from the client: it only exists after an interaction, or it polls, or it's driven by rapidly-changing client state. For that: one shared hook per question, built on SWR (or React Query), keyed canonically. Never raw fetch in a component, never fetch-in-effect. React Critical ✕ Hand-rolled: no dedupe layer exists every component, separately \"use client\" ; const [program, setProgram] = useState ( null ); const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState ( true ); useEffect (() => { fetch ( `/api/proxy/programs/${id}` ) . then (r => r. json ()) . then (setProgram) . finally (() => setIsLoading ( false )); }, [id]); Two components asking this way means two requests, two loading states, and two error paths, with zero sharing between them. ✓ Shared hook: the key IS the dedupe hooks/useProgram.ts \"use client\" ; import useSWR from \"swr\" ; export function useProgram (id: string ) { return useSWR < Program >( `programs/${id}` , fetcher); } Any number of components can call this hook with the same id: one cache entry, one request, revalidated on focus and updated by mutate() . Client cache is a complement, not the architecture SWR's cache lives in browser memory: it evaporates on a hard navigation and starts empty for every visitor. It cannot make first render fast; only the server layers can (that's Part A). The division of labor: server cache for what the page is born with; client cache for what it learns afterwards. If you find most of a page's data flowing through SWR, the problem isn't the caching: the fetching is on the wrong side of the boundary."},{"f":"14-dedupe-caching.html","a":"choosing","n":14,"c":"Dedupe & Caching","h":"14.4 Choosing the layer","t":"The data is… Use Example Needed at first render, personalized cache() in the server data layer program detail, user profile, permissions Needed at first render, shared across users \"use cache\" + cacheLife / cacheTag templates, org lists, filter options, reference data Born from an interaction SWR / React Query shared hook search-as-you-type results, autocomplete Live / polling SWR with refreshInterval job status, notifications badge Changed by a mutation Action + revalidateTag (server) / mutate() (client) everything above, after a write"},{"f":"14-dedupe-caching.html","a":"extends-fetch","n":14,"c":"Dedupe & Caching","h":"14.5 How Next.js extends fetch","t":"One thing that trips people up: fetch on the server does not behave like fetch in the browser. Next.js replaces the global fetch with an extended version that adds two of the layers above directly to the call. Next.js Critical First, it deduplicates identical requests within a single render : two components that fetch the same URL with the same options during one pass share one request. Second, it wires the call into the Data Cache , a persistent, cross-request store you control through the cache and next options. data/stats.ts export async function getPublicStats () { const res = await fetch ( `${API_URL}/stats` , { // Cache the result and treat it as fresh for an hour. next: { revalidate: 3600, tags: [ \"stats\" ] }, }); return res.json(); } // Personalized or always-fresh reads opt out explicitly. export async function getLiveQueue () { const res = await fetch ( `${API_URL}/queue` , { cache: \"no-store\" }); return res.json(); } Next reads the options off fetch itself: this response is cached across requests and tagged, so a mutation can invalidate exactly this data later. This is why the audit's blanket export const dynamic = \"force-dynamic\" plus no-store everywhere ( §18 ) is so costly: it tells this extended fetch to skip the Data Cache on every call, throwing away a layer the framework hands you for free. The habit to build is the inverse: cache by default with a declared revalidate and tags , and reach for no-store only where the data is genuinely per-request. When you call through the database or an SDK instead of fetch , you don't get this automatically, which is exactly why §14.2 's cache() and \"use cache\" exist to fill the gap."},{"f":"14-dedupe-caching.html","a":"swr-depth","n":14,"c":"Dedupe & Caching","h":"14.6 SWR, in more depth","t":"On the client, the whole model rests on one idea: the key is the identity of the data. Two components that pass the same key read the same cache entry; a component that passes null as its key makes no request at all. Everything else (deduplication, revalidation, mutation) hangs off that key. hooks/useJobStatus.ts \"use client\" ; import useSWR from \"swr\" ; export function useJobStatus (jobId: string | null ) { return useSWR ( // A null key disables the request until we actually have an id. jobId ? `jobs/${jobId}` : null , fetcher, { // Poll while the job runs; the last value stays visible between polls. refreshInterval: 3000, // Collapse duplicate requests for this key inside a 2s window. dedupingInterval: 2000, }, ); } The revalidation triggers are the reason SWR feels alive without any manual wiring: by default it refetches on window focus and on network reconnect, plus on whatever refreshInterval you set. Crucially, it never blanks the existing content to do so. The stale value stays on screen while the new one loads, and swaps in when it arrives. That is the stale-while-revalidate contract, and it is the client-side echo of the loading doctrine in §10 : revalidation is shown alongside content, never in place of it. Writes go through mutate , which is the client mirror of revalidateTag : instead of calling a parent back to refetch, you tell the cache that a key is stale and every reader of that key updates itself. Passing new data to mutate paints it optimistically and revalidates in the background, which pairs naturally with §19's useOptimistic for action-driven writes."},{"f":"14-dedupe-caching.html","a":"swr-vs-query","n":14,"c":"Dedupe & Caching","h":"Why SWR and not TanStack Query","t":"The honest comparison first, because TanStack Query is an excellent library and the more popular one (roughly 12 million weekly downloads to SWR's 8 as of this writing, and growing faster). It is also the more powerful one: a dedicated useMutation hook with built-in optimistic updates and rollback, query invalidation primitives, infinite-query and pagination helpers, and first-class devtools. If we were building a client-heavy app where the browser cache is the data layer, TanStack Query would be the stronger tool, and general-audience comparisons rightly recommend it as the default for that world. We are not building that app, and that is the whole argument. Next.js Very important In this architecture the client cache's job is deliberately small: the server layer owns first-paint data, mutations flow through server actions with revalidateTag ( §5.8 ), and optimism is useOptimistic . What remains for a client library ( §14.7 's interaction-born, on-demand, and polling cases) is exactly the surface SWR covers, in about 4 KB gzipped against TanStack Query's ~13 KB, which matters under §7 's budget thinking. TanStack Query's extra machinery would mostly duplicate responsibilities the server layer already owns. The tiebreaker is alignment. SWR is built and maintained by Vercel, the same team that ships Next.js: its defaults are designed against the App Router's behavior, its releases track Next's, and the Next.js docs use it in their own client-fetching examples. When the framework and the cache library share an owner, the seams between them are somebody's job to keep smooth. Choosing TanStack Query here would buy capabilities this architecture works to make unnecessary, at three times the bundle weight and with one more vendor's opinions in the mix."},{"f":"14-dedupe-caching.html","a":"when-client","n":14,"c":"Dedupe & Caching","h":"14.7 When client fetching is the right tool","t":"Server-first does not mean the client never fetches. It means client fetching is a deliberate choice for the cases that genuinely call for it, not the default for everything. React Very important Those cases have a clear signature: the data is not needed at first paint , and it is born from an interaction, a poll, or rapidly-changing client state. Data that only exists after an interaction: search-as-you-type results, autocomplete suggestions, a filter applied in the browser. There is nothing to render on the server because the input hasn't happened yet. Content inside a dialog, drawer, or sheet that isn't part of the main page and is opened on demand. It isn't in the first paint, so loading it when the panel opens is correct, not a smell. Live or polling data: a job status, a notifications badge, anything that changes while the user watches it. The dialog case is the one worth showing, because it is the clean inverse of the God component: the panel and its data are both loaded only when needed. components/programs/RowActions.tsx \"use client\" ; import { useState } from \"react\" ; import dynamic from \"next/dynamic\" ; // The drawer and its data are both born from the interaction: loaded on open. const DetailsDrawer = dynamic (() => import ( \"./DetailsDrawer\" )); export function RowActions ({ id }: { id: string }) { const [open, setOpen] = useState ( false ); return ( <> < Button onClick ={() => setOpen ( true )}>Details<\/ Button > {/* DetailsDrawer calls useDetails(id), a shared SWR hook, once mounted. */} {open && < DetailsDrawer id ={id} onClose ={() => setOpen ( false )} />} <\/> ); } The test is the one from §8.5 , applied to data: if the content is part of what the page is , the server should fetch it; if it is something the page does in response to the user, a client hook is the right home. The failure mode the audit found is the first kind masquerading as the second: a whole page's primary data loaded client-side as if it were interaction-born, when it was the page all along."},{"f":"14-dedupe-caching.html","a":"rules","n":14,"c":"Dedupe & Caching","h":"The rules","t":"Rule Scope Importance All server data access lives in a dedicated data layer, wrapped in cache() , marked server-only . Next.js Critical Shared, non-personalized data is cached cross-request with \"use cache\" and tagged for invalidation. Next.js Very important Client fetching goes through shared SWR/React Query hooks with canonical keys ; never raw fetch in components or effects. React Critical Mutations invalidate rather than orchestrate : tags and mutate() , not callbacks and refetchAll. Next.js Critical Where to go deeper React: cache() · Next.js: Caching · Next.js: Migrating to Cache Components · SWR docs . Full list in §20 ."},{"f":"15-component-breakdown.html","a":"","n":15,"c":"Component Breakdown","h":"Component Breakdown","t":"Every chapter so far has quietly been about where to draw component lines. This one makes it the subject: how a server-first mentality changes the way you break a feature into components, why the splits feel awkward at first, the patterns to reach for, and why one component per file is what holds the whole scheme together."},{"f":"15-component-breakdown.html","a":"axes","n":15,"c":"Component Breakdown","h":"15.1 The axes of a split have changed","t":"Traditional React taught one decomposition instinct: split by visual shape and reuse . A card here, a toolbar there, extract when you see it twice. Those considerations still exist, but server-first demotes them, because three harder axes now come first: The boundary axis: server or client. A component either stays on the server (renders to HTML, ships no JS, may be async ) or crosses to the client (hydrates, interacts, costs bundle). One component cannot be both, so anything that mixes \"renders known content\" with \"handles interaction\" is two components waiting to be separated ( §5 ). The data axis: one component per independent question. Each independent data need becomes its own async server component, because the component is the unit that can await, suspend, and stream on its own ( §12 , §13 ). Splitting by data is what makes parallelism and streaming fall out for free. The containment axis: frame or content. The known frame renders immediately and reserves space; the unknown content suspends inside it. If one component renders both, the frame is hostage to the content's data ( §9 , §10 ). This is why server-first decomposition feels awkward at first . The old instinct says a program card is obviously one component: it is one visual thing. The new axes say it is at least three: a frame that is known, a stats region that awaits data, and a menu that interacts. Cutting along those seams feels like over-fragmenting until you notice what each fragment is: exactly one capability. One frames, one fetches, one interacts. The seams follow capabilities , not the visual outline, and every capability can then be moved, cached, streamed, or shipped (or not shipped) independently. The instinct to retrain \"Is this one visual thing?\" is no longer the question. The questions are: does any part of this wait for data the rest doesn't? Does any part of this interact while the rest just renders? If either answer is yes, the component line goes there , even when the mock shows a single seamless card."},{"f":"15-component-breakdown.html","a":"worked","n":15,"c":"Component Breakdown","h":"15.2 The same card, broken both ways","t":"Here is the old-world version: one file, one component, visually faithful to the mock, and wrong on all three axes. ProgramCard.tsx - one component, three capabilities welded together \"use client\" ; export function ProgramCard ({ programId }: { programId: number }) { const { data, isLoading } = useProgramStats (programId); const [menuOpen, setMenuOpen] = useState ( false ); if (isLoading) return < CardSkeleton />; return ( <section className = \"card\" > <header> <h3>Program<\/h3> < Menu open ={menuOpen} onToggle ={setMenuOpen}>…<\/ Menu > <\/header> < StatRow stats ={data} /> <\/section> ); } Because the menu needs a click, the whole card is client code; because the stats need data, the whole card (frame included) hides behind isLoading ; and because it is one component, none of it can stream, cache, or ship independently. The header that required nothing waits for everything. Cut along the capability seams instead and the card becomes a small folder: components/programs/card/ - one capability per file card/ ├── ProgramCard.tsx server: the known frame ├── ProgramCardStats.tsx server, async: one data question ├── ProgramCardStatsSkeleton.tsx the fallback, colocated (§10) └── ProgramCardMenu.tsx client: the only interactivity ProgramCard.tsx - server: frame renders immediately, reserves space export function ProgramCard ({ programId }: { programId: number }) { return ( <section className = \"card\" > <header className = \"flex items-center justify-between\" > <h3>Program<\/h3> < ProgramCardMenu programId ={programId} /> <\/header> <div className = \"h-24\" > < Suspense fallback ={< ProgramCardStatsSkeleton />}> < ProgramCardStats programId ={programId} /> <\/ Suspense > <\/div> <\/section> ); } The frame is a server component with no data and no state: it paints on the first byte, reserves the stats region's height, and composes the other capabilities without owning them. ProgramCardStats.tsx - server, async: exactly one question export async function ProgramCardStats ({ programId }: { programId: number }) { // React.cache'd (§14): duplicate askers collapse into one execution. const stats = await getProgramStats (programId); return < StatRow stats ={stats} />; } The whole file is one await and one render. That smallness is not tidiness for its own sake: it is what lets this region suspend and stream alone while the frame and the menu never wait. ProgramCardMenu.tsx - client: "},{"f":"15-component-breakdown.html","a":"catalog","n":15,"c":"Component Breakdown","h":"15.3 The pattern catalog","t":"The recurring shapes, in one place. Each is defined in depth in its home chapter; this table is the index you skim while decomposing a feature. Pattern Job Home Known frame Server component that renders chrome and reserves space; contains the unknown, never waits for it. §9 Async region async server component owning exactly one data question; the unit of suspending and streaming. §10 , §12 Colocated skeleton The async region's fallback, next to the component whose shape it mirrors. §10.4 Interaction leaf Smallest client component containing the interaction atom; self-contained via names (key, tag, param), not wires. §5.2 , §5.7 Client shell with slots Client wrapper owning one interaction (open, drag, select); server content passes through JSX-valued props. §5.4 , §13.5 Thin provider Context provider as a client wrapper around {children} , rendered as deep as possible; converts nothing below it. §5.4 Wrapped third party One-line \"use client\" re-export that gives an undeclared library a boundary and your import graph a seam. §5.5 Lazy heavyweight Client component loaded on interaction via dynamic() ; charts, editors, anything that shouldn't tax first render. §7"},{"f":"15-component-breakdown.html","a":"sequence","n":15,"c":"Component Breakdown","h":"15.4 The decomposition sequence","t":"Applied in order, the axes become a repeatable procedure for any page or feature: The decomposition sequence. Frame first, then data, then containment, then interactivity; the one-component-per-file rule at the end is what keeps every earlier step reversible and every later refactor a file move instead of surgery."},{"f":"15-component-breakdown.html","a":"one-per-file","n":15,"c":"Component Breakdown","h":"15.5 One component per file","t":"This habit sounds like style guidance. In the App Router it is structural, because the module, not the component, is the unit that crosses the server/client boundary. React Critical \"use client\" marks a file ; every export in it, and everything that file imports, crosses together ( §5.1 ). The moment a file holds two components, their fates are welded: a helper component sharing a file with an interactive one becomes client code it never asked to be; a server-able piece of UI stuck in a client file drags its imports into the bundle ( §7 ). One component per file means the import graph is the boundary map: you can read where the client zone starts from the filesystem. It also keeps every decomposition decision cheap to revise. Splitting a stats region out of a card, moving a leaf across the boundary, lazy-loading a heavyweight, swapping a shell: each is a file operation when components live alone, and an untangling when they don't. The awkward-at-first splits of §15.1 stay painless only under this rule; it is the difference between \"decomposition is how we work\" and \"decomposition is a refactor we schedule.\" The guide's own examples practice it everywhere: the sidebar split in §5.5 , the SWR pair in §5.8 , the card above. Corollaries Skeletons live in their own files next to the component they mirror (importable by the frame without touching the async region). Barrel files that re-export server and client components together undo the legibility, and a barrel that pulls in a client module can widen what ships ( §7 ); import from the file, not the folder. And a file that suddenly wants a second export is usually a component announcing it has grown a second capability: split it."},{"f":"15-component-breakdown.html","a":"rules","n":15,"c":"Component Breakdown","h":"The rules","t":"Rule Scope Importance Components split along capability seams (boundary, data, containment) before visual or reuse instincts get a vote. React Critical Anything that mixes \"renders known content\" with \"waits for data\" or \"handles interaction\" is two components waiting to be separated . React Critical One component per file. The module is what crosses the boundary; the filesystem should read as the boundary map. React Critical Skeletons are colocated, separate files ; frames import them without touching the async region they stand in for. Next.js Very important Decompose in sequence: frame → data → containment → interactivity , then keep every split one file. React Very important Where to go deeper Next.js docs: Server and Client Components - the composition-patterns reference this chapter distills: moving client components down the tree, passing server components through props, provider placement. React docs: Server Components and \"use client\" - the module-graph semantics underneath the one-component-per-file rule."},{"f":"16-worked-example.html","a":"","n":16,"c":"Worked Example","h":"Worked Example","t":"Draft pending: this chapter hasn't been written yet."},{"f":"17-signs-doing-it-wrong.html","a":"","n":17,"c":"Signs You're Doing It Wrong","h":"Signs You're Doing It Wrong","t":"A field guide to the tells. If you catch yourself doing one of these, you've probably drifted off the server-first path. Each sign says what it usually means, names its honest exception, and links to the chapter that fixes it. These are smells, not sins. Every one of them has a legitimate exception, and every one of them has a real example in §18 , because we are all of them today. The point of a checklist is speed: something feels off, you scan, you find the row, you follow the link. Read it that way. But before the list, the single reframe that makes most of it unnecessary."},{"f":"17-signs-doing-it-wrong.html","a":"the-question","n":17,"c":"Signs You're Doing It Wrong","h":"17.1 One question underneath all of them","t":"Almost every sign below is a symptom of asking the wrong question first. The instinct we carry from years of client React is: \"how do I get this data to this component?\" That question already assumes the component fetches, in the browser, after mount. It smuggles the whole broken model in through the phrasing. The right question, asked before any other, is blunter: \"why can't this be on the server?\" And when the answer is a real one (it genuinely needs the browser), the immediate follow-up is \"how far down can I push it?\" You are hunting for the smallest leaf that actually needs interactivity, not reaching for a directive at the top of the file. The whole chapter in three boxes. The client component is the bottom of the ladder, reached only after the server has been ruled out and the boundary pushed as low as it goes, never the reflex at the top. Most signs below are what it looks like to skip a rung."},{"f":"17-signs-doing-it-wrong.html","a":"questions","n":17,"c":"Signs You're Doing It Wrong","h":"17.2 The questions to keep asking","t":"Make these reflexive. If you ask them honestly on every component, most of the signs below never get a chance to appear. Why can't this be on the server? Not \"can it,\" why not. Demand a concrete answer (it needs an event handler, browser state, an interaction). \"It's easier this way\" is not an answer. If it truly can't, how far down can I push the boundary? What is the smallest piece that actually needs the browser? Everything else stays on the server. Am I about to write a spinner or a loading flag? What is rendering it, and why isn't that the boundary's job instead of the component's? Does the parent holding this data actually use it? Or is it just a pass-through wire I'll have to maintain forever? Should this live in the URL? If it describes where the user is or what they're looking at, the request already carries it; the server can resolve it. Is this import needed in the first paint? If not, where is the dynamic boundary that keeps it out of the initial bundle?"},{"f":"17-signs-doing-it-wrong.html","a":"boundary","n":17,"c":"Signs You're Doing It Wrong","h":"17.3 The boundary is in the wrong place","t":"The sign What it means, and where it's fixed \"use client\" sits on a page or layout. A directive at the top of a route segment forces everything beneath it into the browser, usually just to read params or run one handler. Push it to the leaf that needs it. Fixed in §5.2 and §5.3 . Exception: a route that is genuinely interactive end to end, which is rare and worth a hard second look. One file holds a pile of components. Especially in the app directory: it hides the boundary, blocks code splitting, and grows into a God component. One component per file, interactivity isolated. Fixed in §15 . One component owns fetching, UI state, and callbacks. That is the God component (or God hook): a single boundary that pulls the whole subtree client-side and couples everything to one object's shape. Fixed in §5.9 and §13.4 . \"use client\" is your first move, not your last resort. If adding the directive is the reflex rather than the justified exception, the model is inverted: the browser is a cost to be argued for, not the default. Fixed in §1 and §5.1 ."},{"f":"17-signs-doing-it-wrong.html","a":"fetching","n":17,"c":"Signs You're Doing It Wrong","h":"17.4 Data is fetched the wrong way","t":"The sign What it means, and where it's fixed You're fetching inside a useEffect. First-render data belongs on the server; the effect + state + isLoading triple is the tell you're rebuilding by hand what the framework does for free. Fixed in §4 and §14.3 . Exception: data that only exists after an interaction (search-as-you-type, autocomplete) does load client-side, but through a shared hook, never a raw effect. A mutation goes through a Next.js API route. A server action replaces the hand-written POST endpoint and gives you end-to-end type safety and cache revalidation for free. Fixed in §5.7 and §19 . A first-render read goes through a proxy route. If the data isn't born from an interaction, the server should fetch it directly, one hop, before paint, not bounce it through a route handler after hydration. Fixed in §4 ; seen in the wild in §18 . Exception: reads genuinely first needed after an interaction. Queries are chained: one waits on another's result. Dependent, enabled -gated client queries serialize round-trips that a server component resolves in a single pass with Promise.all . Fixed in §12.2 ."},{"f":"17-signs-doing-it-wrong.html","a":"wiring","n":17,"c":"Signs You're Doing It Wrong","h":"17.5 Components are wired together that shouldn't be","t":"The sign What it means, and where it's fixed A parent fetches data and hands it to several children. Let each child ask for its own data; deduplication makes the repetition free, and the parent stops being a distribution hub. Fixed in §13 and §14 . A child receives a prop the parent never uses. Passing anything through a parent that doesn't need it is a wire you will maintain forever for nothing. Components should take only what they themselves render. Fixed in §13 . A child calls back up so the parent can refetch. onSuccess then refetchAll() is orchestration. Invalidate the cache instead ( revalidateTag , router.refresh , SWR mutate ) and every reader updates itself, no wire required. Fixed in §5.8 and §13.4 ."},{"f":"17-signs-doing-it-wrong.html","a":"loading","n":17,"c":"Signs You're Doing It Wrong","h":"17.6 Loading states and containers are misplaced","t":"The sign What it means, and where it's fixed A component renders its own spinner. Components don't own loading states; the Suspense boundary above them does. A component that manages its own isLoading is doing the boundary's job badly. Fixed in §10 . The fallback includes the container the content sits in. The frame (card, table shell, page header) is known at first render and belongs outside the boundary, rendered on the server; only the unknown contents suspend. Putting the container in the fallback means it gets thrown away and rebuilt. Fixed in §9 and §10 . The suspending component brings its own container. Same root cause, mirror image: the frame lives inside the child that suspends instead of in the layout, so it flickers in with the data instead of being there from the first byte. Fixed in §9 . Containers aren't sized on the server. If the box isn't rendered immediately and reserving accurate space for both the fallback and the real content, the page shifts when data lands. Reserve the space up front. Fixed in §9 . A skeleton is standing in for content that was knowable. Skeletons exist to hold space for the unknown . A skeleton over the whole page, or over inputs, selects, buttons, toolbars, headings, and other structure that required no data, means the boundary swallowed content that could have painted finished on the first byte; the user is watching placeholders for things nobody had to wait for. Wholesale page skeletons need an exceptional reason to exist. Draw the boundary around the data, not around the region it happens to sit in. Fixed in §10 and §4 ."},{"f":"17-signs-doing-it-wrong.html","a":"url-state","n":17,"c":"Signs You're Doing It Wrong","h":"17.7 State is living where the URL should own it","t":"The sign What it means, and where it's fixed Tabs are held in useState. A tab is where the user is. That belongs in the route (a segment or a search param), not in client state that resets on reload and can't be linked to. Fixed in §8.3 . The browser reads a query param to decide what to show. Reading ?x= in a client component and conditionally rendering on it turns the URL into a client-state store the server can't use, and delays the content behind hydration. If output depends on the query string, resolve it on the server from the request. Fixed in §8.2 . Exception: genuinely ephemeral, client-only UI (an open menu, a hover state) that has no business in the URL at all, covered in §8.5 ."},{"f":"17-signs-doing-it-wrong.html","a":"bundle","n":17,"c":"Signs You're Doing It Wrong","h":"17.8 Too much JavaScript ships on first render","t":"The sign What it means, and where it's fixed A heavy client package loads on first paint. Charting libraries, rich-text editors, and date pickers pulled into the initial bundle inflate time-to-interactive for UI most users never touch on load. Fixed in §6 . Something not needed at first paint has no dynamic boundary. If it isn't on screen immediately (dialogs, drawers, below-the-fold panels), it should load on demand with next/dynamic , not sit in the bundle every visitor downloads. Fixed in §6.3 . Exception: small modules that are needed right away, where a dynamic import only buys you an extra round-trip. How to use this list Don't treat it as a purity test. Treat it as a set of tripwires: when you catch yourself doing one, stop and ask the question from §17.1 before you write the next line. Sometimes the honest answer lands you on the exception, and that's fine, the exceptions are real and named on purpose. But most of the time the tripwire is telling you the truth, and the fix is one link away. The goal isn't zero client components; it's that every one of them is a decision you could defend out loud."},{"f":"18-applied-to-tracker.html","a":"","n":18,"c":"Applied to Tracker","h":"Applied to Tracker","t":"Everything before this chapter was the model. This chapter is the mirror: a July 2026 audit of all six Next.js UIs in the Tracker monorepo, what it found, and where in this guide each fix lives."},{"f":"18-applied-to-tracker.html","a":"why","n":18,"c":"Applied to Tracker","h":"18.1 Why we audited our own code","t":"The audit exists to answer one question: how far does our actual usage of the App Router diverge from the model React 19 and the App Router require in order to work well? Not the model this guide prefers; the model the platform is built on. Deviating from it does not produce a different-but-valid architecture, it produces an application the framework cannot help: no streaming, no caching, no stability, with the costs compounding page by page ( §1 ). Before asking the team to adopt server-first patterns, we needed a specific, cited picture of where we stand today, so that the guide's recommendations map to real code rather than hypotheticals, and so the fixes can be prioritized by impact instead of by vibes. Every finding below points at concrete files; every fix points back at the chapter that covers the pattern. Read this in the spirit it's written This chapter is deliberately not bashful, and it is just as deliberately not a critique of anyone's work. Every pattern it documents was the industry-default way React was written for a decade, and all of it \"works\" today: pages render, demos pass, sprints close. The reason to write it down anyway is that these problems do not stay the same size. Every new page copies the patterns of the page before it, and every hook built around a client-side fetch becomes a dependency of the next feature. The gaps below will compound and get worse the longer they go unaddressed, and the cost of fixing them is the cheapest it will ever be right now. The focus is on the gaps because the gaps are the work."},{"f":"18-applied-to-tracker.html","a":"verdict","n":18,"c":"Applied to Tracker","h":"18.2 The verdict","t":"Our apps are SPAs wearing an App Router costume. The Next server is used almost exclusively to serve an empty shell and proxy API calls; nearly all data fetching, authorization, and loading-state management happens client-side, after hydration. In the vocabulary of §2 : almost nothing is treated as knowable at first render, so nearly everything a page shows is delivered as the unknown, with all the machinery (spinners, effects, hand-rolled loading flags) that §3 describes as the old model's tax. The representative trace from the audit ( ojl-ui/src/app/(participant)/my/home/page.tsx ): a 5-line server shell hands off to a \"use client\" page whose children fetch through the proxy after hydration. The same request, made by an async server component ( §4 ), is one hop and arrives as HTML. Importantly, none of this is imposed by our infrastructure. The proxy's cookie-to-Bearer logic works identically inside a server component, and one app in the repo already reads the profile API directly from an async server page with the same auth setup. The client-fetch architecture is a choice, not a constraint , which is exactly why it is fixable."},{"f":"18-applied-to-tracker.html","a":"numbers","n":18,"c":"Applied to Tracker","h":"18.3 The numbers","t":"Counted across the monorepo on 2026-07-08, all six apps, Next.js 16. The \"use client\" column reads \"directive files / total source files\". App Pages Client pages Async server pages \"use client\" files useEffect loading.tsx error.tsx Suspense ojl-ui 32 8 1* 454 / 760 141 8 8 5 craft-profile-ui 11 9 0 55 / 74 49 0 0 6 forms-ui 8 5 1 19 / 29 12 0 0 0 iam-ui 7 6 0 9 / 14 6 0 0 4 portal-ui 1 0 1 8 / 19 3 0 0 0 compliance-ui 1 0 0 2 / 4 2 0 0 0 * The one async page in ojl-ui only awaits params , then hands off to an 862-line client component. The zeroes are the headline Across the entire repo: zero server actions ( \"use server\" ), zero caching primitives ( \"use cache\" , revalidate , React.cache() ), and zero SWR . Every <Suspense> that exists is there to silence the useSearchParams build error ( §5.6 ), not to stream. Roughly 60% of all source files carry \"use client\" . The server side of this stack, Part A and Part B of this guide in their entirety, is essentially unused."},{"f":"18-applied-to-tracker.html","a":"findings","n":18,"c":"Applied to Tracker","h":"18.4 The findings","t":"Eight findings, each with representative evidence and a pointer to the chapter that covers the correct pattern. File paths and counts are from the full audit; this is the condensed version."},{"f":"18-applied-to-tracker.html","a":"finding-1","n":18,"c":"Applied to Tracker","h":"Finding 1: All data fetching is client-side, through a self-hosted proxy","t":"ojl-ui has 73 route handlers under src/app/api/ , all pass-through proxies that forward to the upstream API, converting the auth cookie to a Bearer header. Every service call in the app points at /api/proxy , so every read travels browser to Next server to upstream API: an extra network hop per request, and the server never uses any of that data to render. craft-profile-ui is the same pattern hand-rolled: 13 hooks, each duplicating useState + useEffect + manual isLoading / error / refetch , with no dedupe layer, so remounting any page refetches everything. Where the fix lives: page-level reads become async server components calling the upstream API directly ( §4 ), backed by a cache() -wrapped server data layer ( §14.2 )."},{"f":"18-applied-to-tracker.html","a":"finding-2","n":18,"c":"Applied to Tracker","h":"Finding 2: \"use client\" poisons whole subtrees instead of leaves","t":"The directive sits at page and layout level, usually just to call useParams / useSearchParams / useRouter : values a server page receives as props ( §8.2 ). The worst cases are client layouts on the participant and onboarding segments, which force everything below them client-side; one fetches /users/me after hydration just to render a first name the server already has via the auth cookie. Read-mostly views of 600 to 860 lines ship as client JS end to end, when only their filter strips and buttons need interactivity. Where the fix lives: pages and layouts stay server, interactivity is isolated into leaf client components ( §5 ), and treating every \"use client\" as a cost to justify is the habit that keeps it that way ( §1 )."},{"f":"18-applied-to-tracker.html","a":"finding-3","n":18,"c":"Applied to Tracker","h":"Finding 3: useEffect used for fetching, mutations, and prop-syncing","t":"The audit found fetch-in-effect with hand-rolled state next to an installed React Query ; a hand-written React Query (a useState + useEffect fetch with a module-level in-flight-promise dedup hack); a mutation fired from an effect (verify-email POST, with a useRef guard against StrictMode double-invoke, for a token that should be verified server-side and rendered as plain HTML); and prop-to-state sync effects across sibling dialogs, one carrying an eslint-disable to permit it. The reset-key pattern appears in exactly one file: the team knows the right tool and applies it inconsistently. Where the fix lives: initial data moves to server components ( §4 ), client-side needs go through one shared data layer ( §14.3 ), mutations become server actions ( §5.7 ), and React's own You Might Not Need an Effect is the canon for everything else."},{"f":"18-applied-to-tracker.html","a":"finding-4","n":18,"c":"Applied to Tracker","h":"Finding 4: Authorization decided in the browser","t":"An admin subtree is gated by a client component that fetches /users/me/status in an effect and toggles state, showing a spinner while deciding, per mount, with a round trip. ojl-ui has no middleware.ts ; auth lives in the proxy handlers, so unauthenticated users get a full page render before any 401. Authorization is server-resolvable state in the purest sense: the request carries the cookie, so the server can decide before anything renders. Where the fix lives: server layout checks with redirect() / notFound() plus middleware for coarse auth ( §8.5 ); one layout in the repo already does the cookie-presence version of this correctly."},{"f":"18-applied-to-tracker.html","a":"finding-5","n":18,"c":"Applied to Tracker","h":"Finding 5: No streaming, no route-level loading or error boundaries","t":"24 of 32 ojl-ui routes, including all 15 participant and reviewer pages (the highest-traffic surfaces), have no loading.tsx or error.tsx . The other four apps have zero, total. Every loading state is a hand-rolled isLoading conditional; every error path falls through to global-error.tsx or a blank crash. No <Suspense> streams server data anywhere; one page even renders <Suspense> with no fallback. Where the fix lives: containers reserve the space ( §9 ), Suspense boundaries and route-level loading / error files own every loading state ( §10 )."},{"f":"18-applied-to-tracker.html","a":"finding-6","n":18,"c":"Applied to Tracker","h":"Finding 6: Structural waterfalls","t":"Dependency chains are serialized on the client: fetch the profile, read an organization id out of it, then enable the dashboard query. There are 53 enabled: -gated dependent queries in ojl-ui, each one a client round-trip that a server component would resolve in a single pass with Promise.all . A consistencyToken search-param hack exists to get read-your-writes after create: a problem revalidatePath plus server actions solve natively. Where the fix lives: parallel fetching and minimized fetch dependencies ( §12.2 ); invalidate-don't-orchestrate for the post-mutation refresh ( §14.2 )."},{"f":"18-applied-to-tracker.html","a":"finding-7","n":18,"c":"Applied to Tracker","h":"Finding 7: Caching opted out globally, then never replaced","t":"Two apps set export const dynamic = \"force-dynamic\" on the root layout (to read boot-time env), and every fetch is no-store . That opts the apps out of static rendering, partial prerendering, and the data cache, while the server also fetches nothing. The only cache anywhere is React Query's in-memory 60-second staleTime , which evaporates on every full page load. Where the fix lives: scope force-dynamic to what needs it and restore the layers deliberately: cache() per request, \"use cache\" across requests ( §14 )."},{"f":"18-applied-to-tracker.html","a":"finding-8","n":18,"c":"Applied to Tracker","h":"Finding 8: App Router features missing entirely","t":"No parallel routes, no intercepting routes, anywhere, despite many drawer and sheet flows that are pure client state and not URL-addressable ( §8.3 ). No server actions despite dozens of form mutations. No not-found.tsx outside the roots. These are not exotic extras; they are the primitives Part B and §19 are about, and their absence is why the apps re-implement what the framework provides. Where the fix lives: parallel routes and default.tsx ( §12.3 ), server actions ( §5.7 , §19 )."},{"f":"18-applied-to-tracker.html","a":"path","n":18,"c":"Applied to Tracker","h":"18.5 The path out","t":"The fixes are ordered so that each one makes the next cheaper: server reads make leaf boundaries natural, leaf boundaries make streaming possible, streaming makes the hand-rolled state deletable. # Fix Chapter 1 Convert page-level reads to async server components , starting with the program/participant detail pages, the dashboard, and the people and organizations pages. Fetch upstream APIs directly with the cookie, Promise.all for parallel loads. §4 , §12 2 Push \"use client\" to the leaves. Pages and layouts server by default; params and searchParams via props, not hooks. De-clientify the participant and onboarding layouts first: they poison everything below them. §5 , §8 3 Add loading.tsx and error.tsx per segment and stream with <Suspense> , then delete the hand-rolled isLoading / error state they replace. §9 , §10 4 Move authorization server-side: guard components become server layout redirect() checks, plus middleware for coarse auth. §8.5 5 Kill fetch-in-useEffect. Server components for initial data; one client data paradigm (not three) for what genuinely loads client-side; the key prop instead of prop-sync effects; server actions plus revalidatePath instead of the consistencyToken hack. §5.7 , §14.3 6 Restore caching deliberately: scope force-dynamic to what needs it, cache() for per-request dedupe, \"use cache\" with tags for shared reference data. §14 One more time, because it is the audit's most useful fact: the repo already contains an async server page reading the upstream API directly with the exact same auth setup as every other app. Nothing on this list requires new infrastructure. It requires the mental-model shift this guide exists to make, applied one page at a time, starting with the pages users feel the most. Where to go deeper Next.js: Server and Client Components · Next.js: Fetching Data · React: You Might Not Need an Effect · React: \"use client\" . Full list in §20 ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"New Primitives","t":"The reference catalog behind §1 's direction of travel: the primitives React 19 and Next.js 16 ship, what each one is for, and a working example of each. Skim the map, then read the entries you need. Before the catalog, a nudge: a lot of this is genuinely new, and a fair amount of it is easy to miss. The platform ships APIs faster than most of us read the changelogs, and many of them exist precisely to delete work we're still doing by hand : a hook that replaces a whole isLoading / errors state pair ( useActionState ), a value that replaces a debounce ( useDeferredValue ), a component that replaces a mount/unmount dance ( Activity ), a compiler that replaces a wall of useMemo ( the React Compiler ). Half the reason this appendix exists is discovery: to put the current server-first toolkit in one place, so the next time you're about to reach for a manual workaround, you can check here first and find that the framework already ships the thing. If an entry below is new to you, that is exactly the point; skim the map for the names you don't recognize. Two things to notice as you read. First, almost every primitive here assumes the server-first architecture the rest of this guide describes: actions assume mutations live on the server, use() assumes promises are created on the server and read under Suspense, the caching primitives assume the server renders something worth caching. They are the payoff of the model, not add-ons to it. Second, these are primitives , not goals: the chapters own the patterns, and this appendix owns the API surface. Where a primitive already has a home in a chapter, the entry links there instead of repeating the doctrine."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"map","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"19.1 The map","t":"Primitive Ships in What it does Reach for it when Server Functions React Mutations callable from the client , no API route. Any create/update/delete triggered by UI. <Form> Next.js Forms that navigate or mutate with prefetching. Search inputs writing the URL; action-backed forms. useActionState React Form state from an action's result , plus pending. Validation errors and results rendered from a mutation. useFormStatus React Pending state of the nearest form , from any child. Submit buttons that disable themselves. useOptimistic React Paints the expected result immediately , reverts on failure. Likes, toggles, counts: cheap, likely-to-succeed writes. use() React Reads a promise under Suspense in a client component. Server-created promises handed across the boundary. useTransition React Non-blocking updates with a pending flag . Self-contained mutation buttons; router-state writes. useDeferredValue React Lets urgent updates outrun expensive ones . Type-to-filter over a heavy table or list. <Activity> React Hides UI without unmounting it ; state survives. Genuinely-client panels that toggle often. <ViewTransition> React Browser-native animated transitions (experimental). Not yet: canary-only. Watch, don't adopt. useEffectEvent React Reads fresh values from an effect without re-running it. Effects that over-fire because of a logging/config dep. useId React Hydration-safe unique ids . Label/input pairs, aria wiring. Never hand-write ids. useSyncExternalStore React Subscribes to non-React stores , SSR-safe. Browser APIs (online status, media queries), ext. stores. cache() React Per-request memo for server functions . Every function in the server data layer. useLinkStatus Next.js Pending state of the enclosing Link . Slow navigations with prefetching off; inline hints. <Profiler> React Measures render cost programmatically . Chasing a slow tree you can't explain. React Compiler React Automatic memoization at build time . Everywhere, once enabled; ends memo busywork."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"actions","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"19.2 Actions and mutations","t":""},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"use-server","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"Server Functions ( \"use server\" )","t":"A server function is an async function that runs on the server but is callable from client code as if it were local . React serializes the call across the network; Next.js wires it to an endpoint automatically. This is the primitive that makes our 73 pass-through proxy handlers ( §18 ) unnecessary for mutations: no route handler, no manual fetch, and end-to-end type safety, because the function's signature is the contract. actions/programs.ts \"use server\" ; import { revalidateTag } from \"next/cache\" ; export async function archiveProgram (id: string ) { await api.programs. archive (id); // Every reader of this tag refreshes; no callbacks up the tree. revalidateTag ( `program-${id}` ); } The calling side, with its pending state, is the self-contained button pattern that §5.7 owns; the post-mutation refresh channel is §14.2 's invalidation story; the architecture, and why to prefer actions over proxy routes, is the focus of §5.10 . Docs: React: \"use server\" · Next.js: Updating Data ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"form","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"<Form> (next/form)","t":"Next's <Form> extends the HTML form with prefetching, client-side navigation, and progressive enhancement . Its behavior depends on the action prop: a string makes submission a GET navigation with the fields serialized into search params (the URL-writing search input of §8.2 , with the destination's loading.tsx prefetched); a function makes it an action-backed mutation form. components/programs/ProgramSearch.tsx import Form from \"next/form\" ; export function ProgramSearch () { /* A string action means: navigate to /programs?q=… on submit, client-side, with the target's shared UI prefetched. */ return ( < Form action = \"/programs\" > <input name = \"q\" placeholder = \"Search programs\" /> < SearchButton /> <\/ Form > ); } Note the page rendering this is still a server component: no directive, no hooks. Docs: Next.js: Form · React: <form> ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"use-action-state","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"useActionState","t":"Binds a form to an action and gives you back the action's last result as state , plus the dispatch function and a pending flag. This is where validation errors live in the actions world: the action returns them, React renders them, and nobody hand-rolls an errors / isSubmitting state pair. (If you're searching the docs: this hook replaced the earlier useFormState from react-dom.) components/programs/CreateProgramForm.tsx \"use client\" ; import { useActionState } from \"react\" ; import { createProgram } from \"@/actions/programs\" ; export function CreateProgramForm () { /* state is whatever the action last returned: validation errors here. The third value is the pending flag for the whole submission. */ const [state, formAction, isPending] = useActionState (createProgram, { errors: null }); return ( <form action ={formAction}> <input name = \"name\" /> {state.errors && < FieldErrors errors ={state.errors} />} < Button type = \"submit\" isLoading ={isPending}>Create<\/ Button > <\/form> ); } Docs: React: useActionState ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"use-form-status","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"useFormStatus","t":"Reads the pending state of the nearest ancestor form from anywhere inside it. Its point is composition: a shared submit button can disable itself in any form it's dropped into, without a single prop being threaded to it (the §13 spirit, applied to forms). components/forms/SearchButton.tsx \"use client\" ; import { useFormStatus } from \"react-dom\" ; export function SearchButton () { // True while the enclosing form's action is in flight. const { pending } = useFormStatus (); return ( < Button type = \"submit\" isLoading ={pending}> Search <\/ Button > ); } Docs: React: useFormStatus ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"use-optimistic","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"useOptimistic","t":"Shows the expected result of an action immediately , while the real mutation runs. If the action fails, React reverts to the true value automatically; when revalidated data arrives, it confirms. Use it for cheap, likely-to-succeed writes where waiting even 300ms feels broken. Note how it fits the loading doctrine of §10 : the existing content is never replaced by a spinner; it just updates. components/programs/LikeButton.tsx \"use client\" ; import { useOptimistic } from \"react\" ; import { likeProgram } from \"@/actions/programs\" ; export function LikeButton ({ id, count }: { id: string ; count: number }) { const [optimisticCount, setOptimisticCount] = useOptimistic (count); return ( <form action ={ async () => { /* Paint count + 1 now; revert automatically if the action throws, confirm when the revalidated count arrives. */ setOptimisticCount (optimisticCount + 1); await likeProgram (id); }} > < Button type = \"submit\" >{optimisticCount} likes<\/ Button > <\/form> ); } Docs: React: useOptimistic ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"async","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"19.3 Async UI and transitions","t":""},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"use","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"use()","t":"Reads a promise (or a context) during render, suspending until it resolves . Its headline pattern: a server component starts a fetch without awaiting it, hands the promise across the boundary as a prop, and a client component reads it with use() under the Suspense boundary the server already rendered ( §10 ). The request begins on the server, the HTML streams, and the client never runs a fetch-in-effect. app/programs/[programId]/page.tsx export default async function ProgramPage ({ params }: PageProps ) { const { programId } = await params; // Deliberately not awaited: the promise itself crosses the boundary. const notesPromise = getProgramNotes (programId); return ( < Suspense fallback ={< NotesSkeleton />}> < ProgramNotes notesPromise ={notesPromise} /> <\/ Suspense > ); } components/programs/ProgramNotes.tsx \"use client\" ; import { use } from \"react\" ; export function ProgramNotes ({ notesPromise }: { notesPromise: Promise < Note []> }) { // Suspends here; the server-rendered boundary above shows the fallback. const notes = use (notesPromise); return < NotesList notes ={notes} />; } Docs: React: use · Next.js: Fetching Data ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"use-transition","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"useTransition","t":"Marks an update as non-blocking and gives you its pending flag . In this stack it is the engine of the self-contained mutation button ( §5.7 ) and of no-fallback refinements ( §10 ): the old UI stays interactive while the new state, or the server action, resolves. When to reach for it, and the three canonical uses, are covered in §10.7 . components/programs/ArchiveButton.tsx \"use client\" ; import { useTransition } from \"react\" ; import { archiveProgram } from \"@/actions/programs\" ; export function ArchiveButton ({ id }: { id: string }) { const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition (); return ( < Button isLoading ={isPending} onClick ={() => startTransition (() => archiveProgram (id))} > Archive <\/ Button > ); } Docs: React: useTransition ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"use-deferred-value","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"useDeferredValue","t":"Returns a copy of a value that lags behind on purpose , so urgent updates (the keystroke) outrun expensive ones (re-filtering a thousand-row table). This is the fix for the table problem in §10 : the input stays crisp, the table catches up when it can, and nothing flashes a spinner. components/programs/ProgramFilter.tsx \"use client\" ; import { useDeferredValue, useState } from \"react\" ; export function ProgramFilter ({ programs }: { programs: Program [] }) { const [query, setQuery] = useState ( \"\" ); // The input re-renders with query; the heavy table follows with this. const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue (query); return ( <> <input value ={query} onChange ={e => setQuery (e.target.value)} /> < ProgramTable programs ={programs} filter ={deferredQuery} /> <\/> ); } Docs: React: useDeferredValue ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"activity","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"<Activity>","t":"Hides a subtree without unmounting it : state, scroll position, and DOM survive, and hidden trees render at low priority. Before reaching for it, apply the §8.3 ladder: tabs that represent where the user is belong in the URL. Activity is for the panels that genuinely remain client state (interaction, not identity) and are expensive to remount. components/editor/EditorPanels.tsx \"use client\" ; import { Activity } from \"react\" ; export function EditorPanels ({ active }: { active: \"editor\" | \"preview\" }) { return ( <> {/* The hidden panel keeps its unsaved edits instead of remounting. */} < Activity mode ={active === \"editor\" ? \"visible\" : \"hidden\" }> < DraftEditor /> <\/ Activity > < Activity mode ={active === \"preview\" ? \"visible\" : \"hidden\" }> < DraftPreview /> <\/ Activity > <\/> ); } Docs: React: Activity ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"view-transition","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"<ViewTransition>","t":"Animates enter/exit/update/shared-element transitions using the browser's native View Transition API, coordinated with Transitions and Suspense, declared in JSX instead of hand-rolled animation state. Experimental: do not adopt yet As of this writing, <ViewTransition> is available only in React's canary and experimental channels , not in stable React 19.2. It's in this catalog because it shows where the platform is going (animation as a tree concern, like loading states in §10 ), not because we should ship it. Watch the React: ViewTransition page for stabilization."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"utilities","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"19.4 Utilities","t":""},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"use-effect-event","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"useEffectEvent","t":"Extracts the non-reactive part of an effect : the returned function always sees the latest props and state, but is not a dependency, so the effect stops re-running (and re-connecting, re-subscribing, re-firing) when those values change. Stable as of React 19.2. It is a scalpel for the few legitimate effects that remain after §18's Finding 3 is fixed; it is not a license to write more effects. components/jobs/JobStatusToast.tsx \"use client\" ; import { useEffect, useEffectEvent } from \"react\" ; export function JobStatusToast ({ jobId, theme }: { jobId: string ; theme: Theme }) { // Always sees the current theme, without being an effect dependency. const onDone = useEffectEvent (() => toast. success ( \"Job finished\" , { theme })); useEffect (() => { // Re-subscribes only when the job changes, not when the theme does. const sub = jobs. subscribe (jobId, () => onDone ()); return () => sub. unsubscribe (); }, [jobId]); return null ; } Docs: React: useEffectEvent ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"use-id","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"useId","t":"Generates ids that are stable across server render and hydration . Hand-written or randomly generated element ids are a hydration-mismatch factory in an SSR world; this is the sanctioned replacement, chiefly for accessibility wiring. components/forms/EmailField.tsx \"use client\" ; import { useId } from \"react\" ; export function EmailField () { // Identical on the server and in the browser, unique per instance. const id = useId (); return ( <> <label htmlFor ={id}>Email<\/label> <input id ={id} type = \"email\" /> <\/> ); } Docs: React: useId ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"use-sync-external-store","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"useSyncExternalStore","t":"The correct way to subscribe to anything that lives outside React : browser APIs, third-party stores, singletons. It takes a subscribe function, a snapshot reader, and a server snapshot so SSR and hydration render consistently, which is exactly the failure mode of doing this with useEffect + useState . hooks/useOnlineStatus.ts \"use client\" ; import { useSyncExternalStore } from \"react\" ; function subscribe (onStoreChange: () => void ) { window. addEventListener ( \"online\" , onStoreChange); window. addEventListener ( \"offline\" , onStoreChange); return () => { window. removeEventListener ( \"online\" , onStoreChange); window. removeEventListener ( \"offline\" , onStoreChange); }; } export function useOnlineStatus () { /* Subscribe, read the client snapshot, and provide a server snapshot so the SSR pass and hydration agree. */ return useSyncExternalStore (subscribe, () => navigator.onLine, () => true ); } Docs: React: useSyncExternalStore ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"cache","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"cache()","t":"Per-request memoization for server functions: same arguments, one execution, per request . It is the foundation of the server data layer, and it already has a full treatment in §14.2 ; it appears here only so the catalog is complete. Docs: React: cache ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"use-link-status","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"useLinkStatus","t":"Reads the pending state of the enclosing <Link> from a descendant component: an inline hint for navigations that can't be instant (prefetching disabled, or a dynamic route without a loading.tsx ). Prefer route-level fallbacks and prefetching first ( §4.5 ); reach for this when a specific slow transition needs feedback at the link . components/nav/LinkPendingDot.tsx \"use client\" ; import { useLinkStatus } from \"next/link\" ; export function LinkPendingDot () { // Must render inside a Link; true until the navigation commits. const { pending } = useLinkStatus (); /* Fixed-size and always rendered, so toggling it never shifts layout (the §9 container rule, at the scale of a dot). */ return <span aria-hidden className ={pending ? \"dot dot-pending\" : \"dot\" } />; } Docs: Next.js: useLinkStatus ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"profiler","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"<Profiler>","t":"Wraps a subtree and reports what rendering it actually cost , per commit, to an onRender callback. Use it to turn \"this page feels slow\" into a named component and a number before optimizing anything; pair it with the React DevTools performance tracks. Docs: React: Profiler ."},{"f":"19-new-primitives.html","a":"compiler","n":19,"c":"New Primitives","h":"19.5 The React Compiler","t":"The compiler (1.0, October 2025) memoizes automatically at build time: it analyzes component code and skips re-renders that provably change nothing, which is the work useMemo , useCallback , and React.memo asked humans to do by hand. Two consequences for us. First, new code should not hand-roll memoization ; write plain idiomatic components and let the compiler prove what's stable. Second, the compiler rewards the exact discipline this guide teaches: small, decoupled components with narrow props ( §13 , §15 ) give it clean units to optimize, while God components ( §5.9 ) give it one giant opaque one. It changes none of the architecture in this guide; it removes the busywork that made bad architecture feel cheap. Docs: React: React Compiler · Next.js: reactCompiler . Where to go deeper React: API reference · React 19 release post · React 19.2 release post · Next.js: API reference . Full list in §20 ."},{"f":"20-references.html","a":"","n":20,"c":"References","h":"References","t":"Every external source used in this guide, grouped by origin, with a note on what each one backs. The official docs are the territory; go read them. A reminder from §1 : this guide is deliberately not self-sufficient. It stitches together and elaborates on the official Next.js and React documentation, and every chapter's \"where to go deeper\" links land here."},{"f":"20-references.html","a":"nextjs","n":20,"c":"References","h":"20.1 Official docs: Next.js","t":"Next.js 16 release post : Cache Components model, \"use cache\" replacing implicit caching, PPR-by-default under cacheComponents, Turbopack. Getting Started: Caching : the current opt-in caching model. next.config.js: cacheComponents : flag semantics; PPR as default behavior. Guide: Migrating to Cache Components : migration semantics for existing apps. Getting Started: Server and Client Components : boundary mechanics and composition patterns ( §5 ). Getting Started: Fetching Data : parallel vs sequential fetching, dedupe, streaming ( §12 ). Getting Started: Linking and Navigating : prefetch modes, client-side transitions, the RSC payload on navigation, the dynamic-routes-without-loading.tsx trap, hydration delay ( §4 ). Guide: Prefetching : prefetch configuration, hover-only prefetching, disabling for large link lists ( §4.5 ). Rendering: Server Components (v14 guide) : the benefits of server rendering enumerated; static/dynamic/streaming strategies ( §4 ). File conventions: loading.js : instant loading states and streaming ( §10 ). File conventions: error.js : error boundaries per segment ( §10 ). Parallel Routes : slots, default.js, independent loading/error per slot ( §12.3 ). Intercepting Routes : URL-addressable overlays. Lazy Loading / dynamic imports : deferring client bundles ( §6 ). Getting Started: Updating Data : server actions from client components, revalidation after mutations ( §5.7 ). revalidateTag · revalidatePath : cache invalidation as the post-mutation refresh channel ( §5.8 , §13.4 ). useRouter: router.refresh() : refresh for mutations outside server actions ( §5.8 ). Guide: Package Bundling : @next/bundle-analyzer for measuring first-render JS ( §6.4 ). page.js file convention : params and searchParams as async page props ( §8.2 ). useSearchParams : client reads of the query string; the Suspense requirement ( §5.6 , §8 ). cookies : server-side preference reads; dynamic-rendering implications ( §8.5 ). Form component : string vs function action semantics, prefetching, progressive enhancement ( §19 ). useLinkStatus : pending state of the enclosing Link; fixed-size-hint guidance ( §19 ). next.config.js: reactCompiler : enabling the React Compiler in Next.js ( §19 )."},{"f":"20-references.html","a":"react","n":20,"c":"References","h":"20.2 Official docs: React","t":"React 19.2 release post : Activity, useEffectEvent stable, Suspense SSR batching, DevTools performance tracks, the compiler 1.0 era. React blog index : the direction-of-travel narrative ( §1 ). Introducing Zero-Bundle-Size React Server Components (Dec 2020) : the timeline anchor for the server era ( §1 ). React v18 (Mar 2022) : concurrent rendering, streaming SSR ( §1 timeline). React v19 (Dec 2024) : Actions, use(), stable RSC APIs ( §1 timeline). Next.js 13 (Oct 2022) · Next.js 13.4 (May 2023) : App Router beta to stable ( §1 timeline). Server Components : the RSC reference ( §3 , §4 ). cache : per-request memoization for server components ( §14.2 ). Suspense : triggers, fallback semantics, SSR streaming ( §10 ). useDeferredValue : deferred re-rendering for heavy subtrees ( §10 , §19 ). You Might Not Need an Effect : the anti-pattern canon for fetch-in-effect, derived state, and prop-syncing ( §18 ). Server Actions / \"use server\" : server functions callable from the client ( §5.7 , §19 ). 'use client' directive : module-graph semantics and serialization rules ( §5 ). useTransition : isPending indicators, no-fallback transitions ( §5.7 , §10 ). Passing Data Deeply with Context : the \"before you use context\" checklist ( §13 ). lazy : deferred component loading; the top-level declaration rule ( §6.3 ). Choosing the State Structure · Sharing State Between Components : the state-placement principles behind §8 . use : reading promises with Suspense semantics ( §10 , §19 ). useActionState : an action's result as form state plus a pending flag; replaced useFormState ( §19 ). useFormStatus : nearest-form pending state for composable submit buttons ( §19 ). useOptimistic : optimistic values with automatic revert on failure ( §19 ). useId : hydration-safe unique ids ( §19 ). useSyncExternalStore : external-store subscriptions with a server snapshot ( §19 ). useEffectEvent : non-reactive effect logic; stable in 19.2 ( §19 ). Activity : hiding and restoring subtrees without unmounting ( §19 ). ViewTransition : canary-only status verified 2026-07-17; watch for stabilization ( §19 ). Profiler : programmatic render-cost measurement ( §19 ). React Compiler : automatic memoization and 1.0-era guidance ( §19 ). form (react-dom) : function-action form semantics underlying Next's Form ( §19 )."},{"f":"20-references.html","a":"talks","n":20,"c":"References","h":"20.3 Talks and essays","t":"The primary sources for the \"voices\" table in §1 : the platform's authors saying server-first out loud, on the record, across a decade. 7 Principles of Rich Web Applications : Guillermo Rauch, Nov 2014. \"Server rendered pages are not optional,\" two years before Next.js existed. Sneak Peek: Beyond React 16 : Dan Abramov, JSConf Iceland, Mar 2018. The first public Suspense demo. The Two Reacts : Dan Abramov, Jan 2024. UI as a function of data and state; the best deep reading on why the server/client split exists. React Conf 2024 Recap : React 19 RC keynote; the Compiler open-sourced. React for Two Computers : Dan Abramov, React Conf 2024. The conceptual case for the server/client paradigm. Supporting the Future of React : Vercel, Dec 2021. The Markbåge hire; React core co-staffed by Vercel. Meet the Team (react.dev) : the current React core roster across Meta and Vercel. Understanding React Server Components : Vercel's own RSC explainer; complements §4 and §5 ."},{"f":"20-references.html","a":"ecosystem","n":20,"c":"References","h":"20.4 Vercel and ecosystem","t":"Cache Components for Instant and Fresh Pages (Vercel Academy) : the teaching framing for the caching model ( §14 ). SWR docs : client-side dedupe and cache, stale-while-revalidate ( §14.3 ). SWR mutate : keyed revalidation; the key replaces the refetch callback ( §5.8 , §13.4 )."},{"f":"20-references.html","a":"community","n":20,"c":"References","h":"20.5 Community and secondary","t":"Used for verification and narrative cross-checks only; where a claim matters, it is anchored to the official sources above. React 19.2 is here (LogRocket) : 19.2 feature summary cross-check. What's Next for React in 2026 (Telerik) : ecosystem direction cross-check. The State of Server Components in 2026 (PkgPulse) : adoption narrative cross-check."}]};
